(verb :name "list-tail" :std "R7RS-small"  :library "list" :tier "core" :kind "pure" :signature "(list-tail lst k) -> list"
  :summary "Drop the first k elements. R7RS §6.4."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(list-tail (list 1 2 3 4 5) 2)" :note "(3 4 5).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define offsets (iota 10))\n(list-tail offsets 5)" :note "Skip the first half: (5 6 7 8 9).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(define (pages xs per)\n  (if (null? xs) '()\n    (cons (if (< (length xs) per) xs (reverse (list-tail (reverse xs) (- (length xs) per))))\n          (pages (if (< (length xs) per) '() (list-tail xs per)) per))))\n(pages (iota 7) 3)" :note "Paginate into 3-per-page: ((0 1 2) (3 4 5) (6))."))
  :provenance "R7RS §6.4; added 2026-07-18 finalization"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define rows (list 'header (list 'a 1) (list 'b 2) (list 'c 3)))\n(define data (list-tail rows 1))\n(display data)\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (nth-cdr-equivalent xs k)\n  (equal? (list-tail xs k)\n          (let loop ((n k) (r xs)) (if (or (= n 0) (null? r)) r (loop (- n 1) (cdr r))))))\n(display (map (lambda (k) (nth-cdr-equivalent '(a b c d e) k)) '(0 1 2 3 5)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (safe-tail xs k)\n  (cond ((< k 0) 'negative-drop-illegal)\n        ((> k (length xs)) '())\n        (else (list-tail xs k))))\n(display (list (safe-tail '(a b c) 5) (safe-tail '(a b c) -1) (safe-tail '(a b c) 2)))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator is processing a CSV file where the first row is a header and only the data rows should be handed downstream. Candidates: list-tail lst 1 (drop the header), (cdr lst) (drops one, but the k form generalizes), (filter (lambda (x) …) lst) (wrong shape). For 'drop the first k,' list-tail is honest."
     :program "(define rows (list 'header (list 'a 1) (list 'b 2) (list 'c 3)))\n(define data (list-tail rows 1))\n(display data)\n(newline)"
     :explanation "eng: drop CSV header row. math: list-tail = k-fold cdr — this is the definitional identity. cs: bounds-safe wrapper that translates out-of-range k into semantic sentinels."
     :meta "Why list-tail and not cdr: same for k=1; list-tail generalizes to any k without nesting cdrs.")
    (:eng "Prefer list-tail when the drop count is variable; prefer cdr when k=1 is idiomatic."
     :math "list-tail(xs, k) = drop k elements from the front."
     :cs "Related patterns: [k:] in Python, drop in Haskell, skip in LINQ.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "A prefix drop: skip the first k elements of a sequence and return the remaining suffix."
     :rungs ((:domain "list" :code "(define rows (list 'header (list 'a 1) (list 'b 2) (list 'c 3)))\n(define data (list-tail rows 1))\n(display data)\n(newline)")
             (:domain "skip the intro of a book" :code "(define rows (list 'header (list 'a 1) (list 'b 2) (list 'c 3)))\n(define data (list-tail rows 1))\n(display data)\n(newline)")
             (:domain "shift by k in a stream" :code "(define (drop k xs) (if (or (null? xs) (= k 0)) xs (drop (- k 1) (cdr xs))))"))
     :explanation "In list, list-tail skips k elements. Skipping the preface of a book gives the reader the main content. In streams, shifting by k discards k elements. All three share: forget the first k, keep the rest."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a prefix is discarded to focus on the remainder."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define xs (iota 5))\n(display (list-tail xs 0))\n(newline)\n(display (list-tail xs 2))\n(newline)\n(display (list-tail xs 5))\n(newline)\n(display (list 'len (length (list-tail xs 2)) 'expected (- (length xs) 2)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (drop-additive? xs a b)\n  (equal? (list-tail (list-tail xs a) b)\n          (list-tail xs (+ a b))))\n(display (map (lambda (p) (drop-additive? '(a b c d e f g) (car p) (cadr p)))\n              '((0 3) (2 2) (3 1))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (shares-tail? xs k)\n  (let ((t (list-tail xs k)))\n    (eq? t (list-tail xs k))))\n(define xs (list 1 2 3 4 5))\n(display (shares-tail? xs 2))\n(newline)"
     :claim "list-tail returns a list of length max(0, |xs|-k) whose contents are xs[k..]; list-tail with k=0 returns xs unchanged."
     :program "(define xs (iota 5))\n(display (list-tail xs 0))\n(newline)\n(display (list-tail xs 2))\n(newline)\n(display (list-tail xs 5))\n(newline)\n(display (list 'len (length (list-tail xs 2)) 'expected (- (length xs) 2)))\n(newline)"
     :invariant "∀ xs k. 0 ≤ k ≤ |xs| ⇒ |list-tail(xs, k)| = |xs| - k; list-tail(xs, 0) = xs"
     :meta "eng: length formula. math: additivity — dropping a then b is dropping a+b. cs: structural sharing — the returned tail is eq? across calls (no copy)."
     :eng "The engineering payoff is that list-tail is O(k) and does not copy — it returns a suffix that shares structure with xs."
     :math "list-tail is the composition of cdr k times."
     :cs "Because Scheme lists are singly-linked, list-tail is O(k) time and O(1) space.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define pages (list 'cover 'preface 'ch1 'ch2 'ch3 'ch4))\n(define chapters (list-tail pages 2))\n(define (paginate xs per-page) (if (null? xs) (list) (cons (if (< (length xs) per-page) xs (list-tail (reverse (list-tail (reverse xs) (- (length xs) per-page))) 0)) (paginate (if (< (length xs) per-page) (list) (list-tail xs per-page)) per-page))))\n(display chapters)\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (sliding-suffixes xs)\n  (let loop ((k 0) (acc '()))\n    (if (> k (length xs)) (reverse acc) (loop (+ k 1) (cons (list-tail xs k) acc)))))\n(display (sliding-suffixes '(a b c d)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (window xs w step)\n  (let loop ((i 0) (acc '()))\n    (if (> (+ i w) (length xs))\n      (reverse acc)\n      (loop (+ i step)\n            (cons (reverse (list-tail (reverse (list-tail xs i)) (- (length xs) i w))) acc)))))\n(display (window (iota 8) 3 2))\n(newline)"
     :base "list-tail drops the first k elements."
     :composition "(define pages (list 'cover 'preface 'ch1 'ch2 'ch3 'ch4))\n(define chapters (list-tail pages 2))\n(define (paginate xs per-page) (if (null? xs) (list) (cons (if (< (length xs) per-page) xs (list-tail (reverse (list-tail (reverse xs) (- (length xs) per-page))) 0)) (paginate (if (< (length xs) per-page) (list) (list-tail xs per-page)) per-page))))\n(display chapters)\n(newline)"
     :emergent "eng: header-skip and pagination. math: complete family of tail-suffixes (every drop from 0 to |xs|). cs: fixed-size sliding window with step — same shape as time-series windowing."
     :meta "The composition uses list-tail as the prefix-skip step. What emerges is the header/body separation family: split off metadata to isolate content. Same shape as HTTP header/body splitting, as CSV header skipping. See book-of-composition ch10.")
)
)

(verb :name "list-copy" :std "R7RS-small"  :library "list" :tier "core" :kind "pure" :signature "(list-copy lst) -> list"
  :summary "A shallow copy of lst. R7RS §6.4."
  :provenance "R7RS §6.4; added 2026-07-18 finalization"
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(list-copy (list 1 2 3))" :note "(1 2 3) — a fresh copy.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define original (list 1 2 3))\n(define twin (list-copy original))\n(list-set! twin 0 99)\noriginal" :note "(1 2 3) — mutating the copy left the original intact; list-copy handed back an independent list.")
  )
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define original (list 1 2 3))\n(define twin (list-copy original))\n(display (list 'equal? (equal? original twin) 'len-preserved (= (length original) (length twin))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define original '(a b c d))\n(define twin (list-copy original))\n(list-set! twin 0 'z)\n(display (list 'differ-after-mutation (not (equal? original twin)) 'original-preserved (equal? original '(a b c d)) 'independent 'yes))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define original (list (list 'a 1) (list 'b 2)))\n(define twin (list-copy original))\n(list-set! (car twin) 1 99)\n(display (list 'shared-nested-element (car original) 'shallow-copy-witness 'true))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator has a list to hand off to a callee that may mutate it (via list-set!), and the operator wants the original preserved. Candidates: list-copy (fresh top-level structure), passing the same reference (unsafe if callee mutates), deep-copy (overkill unless the ELEMENTS are also mutable). For a shallow protective copy, list-copy is the honest verb. Note: in Motoi eq? compares by value, so it cannot witness object identity — mutation isolation is the observable proof that the copy is independent."
     :program "(define original (list 1 2 3))\n(define twin (list-copy original))\n(list-set! twin 0 9)\n(display (list 'original original 'twin twin))\n(newline)"
     :explanation "Line 1 defines the original. Line 2 makes a shallow copy. Line 3 mutates the copy with list-set!. The display shows the original unchanged — list-copy handed back an independent top-level list. (In Motoi eq? compares by value, so eq? alone cannot prove independence; the mutation test does.)"
     :meta "Why list-copy and not passing the reference: the reference is aliased; any downstream mutation via list-set! is observed by all holders. The copy's top level is fresh, but nested elements are shared — shallow, not deep.")
    (:eng "Use list-copy at handoff boundaries when the callee is untrusted or the caller wants to preserve the original."
     :math "The result is the same sequence as a value; equality holds under equal?, not under eq?."
     :cs "Related patterns: list(original) in Python, ArrayList copy constructor in Java. Shallow copy shares element identity but not spine.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "A defensive copy: produce an independent handle to the same content so that mutations to one do not affect the other."
     :rungs ((:domain "list" :code "(define original (list 1 2 3))\n(define twin (list-copy original))\n(display (list 'equal? (equal? original twin) 'eq? (eq? original twin)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "photocopy the ledger" :code "(define original (list 1 2 3))\n(define twin (list-copy original))\n(display (list 'equal? (equal? original twin) 'eq? (eq? original twin)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "identity on values, distinctness on identities" :code "(define (twin xs) (map (lambda (x) x) xs))"))
     :explanation "In list, list-copy makes a fresh spine. Photocopying a ledger gives you the same values in a physically distinct document. In math, the identity function on a value copies the value into a new container. All three share: same content, different container."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a value has both a content aspect and an identity aspect and the client needs the content without the identity."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define original (list 1 2 3))\n(define twin (list-copy original))\n(list-set! twin 0 99)\n(display (list 'twin-mutated (equal? twin '(99 2 3)) 'original-intact (equal? original '(1 2 3)) 'len-eq (= (length original) (length twin))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (copy-invariants xs)\n  (let ((c (list-copy xs)))\n    (and (equal? xs c)\n         (= (length xs) (length c))\n         (or (null? xs)\n             (begin (list-set! c 0 'sentinel)\n                    (not (equal? (car xs) 'sentinel)))))))\n(display (and (copy-invariants '(1 2 3)) (copy-invariants '()) (copy-invariants '(a b c d e f g))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define original (list 1 2 3 4 5))\n(define c1 (list-copy original))\n(define c2 (list-copy original))\n(list-set! c1 0 99)\n(display (list 'c2-unaffected (equal? c2 original) 'original-unaffected (equal? original '(1 2 3 4 5)) 'c1-changed (= (car c1) 99)))\n(newline)"
     :claim "list-copy returns a list equal? to the original but with an independent spine: equal? is #t and the length is preserved; mutating the copy (via list-set!) leaves the original unchanged. In Motoi eq? compares by value, so independence shows up as mutation isolation, not as an eq? difference."
     :program "(define original (list 1 2 3))\n(define twin (list-copy original))\n(display (list 'equal? (equal? original twin) 'len (list (length original) (length twin))))\n(newline)\n(list-set! twin 0 9)\n(display (list 'after-mutation-original original 'twin twin))\n(newline)"
     :invariant "∀ list xs. list-copy(xs) equal? xs ∧ |list-copy(xs)| = |xs| ∧ mutating list-copy(xs) leaves xs unchanged (independent spine)"
     :meta "The pattern is content-preserving, independent-spine copy. CS pattern name: shallow copy. In Motoi eq? compares by value, so independence is witnessed by mutation isolation (list-set! on the copy), not by an eq? difference. What would falsify: an inequal-equal? result, or a copy whose mutation leaks back into the original. See book-of-code ch07 on equality and identity."
     :eng "The engineering payoff is safe handoff without downstream mutation risk."
     :math "Independent spine, equal value."
     :cs "Shallow copy: spine is copied, elements are aliased.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (with-list-arg xs body-fn)\n  (let ((local (list-copy xs)))\n    (body-fn local)))\n(define original (list 'a 'b 'c))\n(display (with-list-arg original (lambda (l) (list 'inside l))))\n(newline)\n(display (list 'original-still original))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (all-permutation-inputs base)\n  (map (lambda (_) (list-copy base)) '(1 2 3)))\n(define bases (all-permutation-inputs '(a b c)))\n(list-set! (car bases) 0 'mutated)\n(display (list 'rest-unchanged (every (lambda (b) (equal? b '(a b c))) (cdr bases)) 'first-independent (equal? (car bases) '(mutated b c))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (safe-map-batch xs procs)\n  (map (lambda (proc) (proc (list-copy xs))) procs))\n(define batch (safe-map-batch '(1 2 3)\n                              (list (lambda (l) (cons 0 l))\n                                    (lambda (l) (reverse l))\n                                    (lambda (l) (length l)))))\n(display batch)\n(newline)"
     :base "list-copy makes a fresh spine over the same elements."
     :composition "(define (with-list-arg xs body-fn)\n  (let ((local (list-copy xs)))\n    (body-fn local)))\n(define original (list 'a 'b 'c))\n(display (with-list-arg original (lambda (l) (list 'inside l))))\n(newline)\n(display (list 'original-still original))\n(newline)"
     :emergent "isolation wrapper — a helper that gives the body function its own independent list handle so the original is unaffected by any (hypothetical) local mutation"
     :meta "The composition wraps list-copy around a body call. What emerges is the defensive-copy family: functional-boundary safety. Same shape as immutable-wrapper patterns, as passing-by-value contracts. See book-of-composition ch11.")
)
)


;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; SRFI-1 surface completion — 23 verbs appended 2026-08-02.
;; Implemented in lib/std/srfi-1.js; documented against observed
;; runtime behavior, not against the SRFI-1 document.
;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

(verb
  :name "xcons" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(xcons d a) -> pair"
  :summary "Exchanged cons: build the pair whose car is a and whose cdr is d — the tail is given FIRST."
  :explanation "xcons is cons with its arguments swapped. That looks perverse until the tail is the value you already have in hand: then xcons curries cleanly on the tail and a plain lambda is not needed to flip the operands. In Motoi a proper list tail yields a proper list; any other tail mints a real dotted pair, which makes xcons one of the few honest ways to build an improper list."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(xcons (list 2 3) 1)" :note "Tail first, head second: (1 2 3). Compare (cons 1 (list 2 3)) — same answer, arguments swapped.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(xcons 3 1)" :note "A non-list tail mints a real dotted pair: (1 . 3). This is one of the few verbs in Motoi that can build one.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (tag-with tail) (lambda (h) (xcons tail h)))\n(map (tag-with (list 'units 'cm)) (list 3 5 8))" :note "The tail is fixed, so it curries: ((3 units cm) (5 units cm) (8 units cm)). With cons you would need a flipping lambda.")
  )
  :caveats ("The argument order is the OPPOSITE of cons. (xcons 1 2) is (2 . 1), not (1 . 2)." "A non-list second-position tail produces an improper (dotted) pair, not a list. Check with proper-list? before handing the result to a list verb." "Motoi SRFI-1 folds are element-first, so (fold xcons seed lst) does NOT reverse a list — it hands xcons the element as the tail. Use (fold cons (list) lst) for reverse.")
  :usecases ("Currying on a fixed tail so a stream of heads can be mapped." "Building dotted pairs deliberately, for teaching or for alist entries." "Passing a cons-shaped constructor to a higher-order procedure that supplies the accumulator first.")
  :related ("cons" "cons*" "append-reverse" "proper-list?")
  :learn (:concept "Operand exchange — the same constructor with its arguments flipped." :prerequisites ("cons" "lambda") :progression "Learn cons first, then xcons as the flipped form, then cons* for prepending several elements at once.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define (tag-with tail) (lambda (h) (xcons tail h)))\n(define stamp (tag-with (list 'sensor 'celsius)))\n(display (map stamp (list 3 5 8)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (flip f) (lambda (x y) (f y x)))\n(define samples (list (list (list 2 3) 1) (list (list) 'a) (list (list 'x) 'y)))\n(display (map (lambda (s) (equal? (xcons (car s) (cadr s)) ((flip cons) (car s) (cadr s)))) samples))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (shape d a)\n  (let ((r (xcons d a)))\n    (list 'proper? (proper-list? r) 'value r)))\n(display (shape (list 2 3) 1))\n(newline)\n(display (shape 3 1))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator holds one fixed tail and a stream of heads, and must cons each head onto that tail. Candidates: cons (right order for a single call, but needs a flipping lambda to curry on the tail), xcons (tail-first, so the fixed part is the first argument and currying is direct), or append with a one-element wrapper list (allocates a throwaway list per head). For tail-first consing, xcons is the honest verb — it exists for exactly this shape."
     :explanation "eng: the fixed schema tail is captured once and every head rides onto it. math: xcons is cons composed with the flip combinator, so the two agree on every input. cs: the tail decides the result type — a list tail gives a proper list, anything else gives a dotted pair, and proper-list? is the boundary check."
     :meta "Why xcons and not cons: with cons the varying argument comes first, so the fixed tail cannot be partially applied without writing a lambda. xcons puts the fixed part where a closure can hold it.")
    (:eng "Prefer xcons when the tail is fixed and the head varies; prefer cons when you are reading the code aloud as head-then-tail."
     :math "xcons = cons ∘ flip, where flip(f)(x,y) = f(y,x). It is the C combinator applied to a constructor."
     :cs "Same slot as flip/swap adapters elsewhere: Haskell flip, Clojure #(cons %2 %1), Python functools.partial over a reordered wrapper.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Operand exchange: a two-argument constructor whose arguments are swapped so that the value already in hand comes first and the new value comes second."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(xcons (list 2 3) 1)")
             (:domain "stacking trays on a counter (the stack is already there, the new tray goes on top)" :code "(define counter (list 'tray-b 'tray-c))\n(display (xcons counter 'tray-a))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "the C combinator: C f x y = f y x" :code "(define (C f) (lambda (x y) (f y x)))\n(display ((C cons) (list 2 3) 1))\n(newline)\n(display ((C -) 3 10))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, xcons puts the tail first. On a counter, the stack of trays is the thing already present and the new tray is what arrives. In combinatory logic, C is the general operand-exchange operator and xcons is C applied to cons. All three share: the standing value comes first, the arriving value second."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever an operation has a standing operand and an arriving operand and the caller wants the standing one bound first.")
    (:eng "Argument order is an interface decision: put the part that stays fixed where it can be closed over."
     :math "C is one of the three classical combinators (B compose, C flip, K constant); xcons is C cons."
     :cs "This is why languages with currying care about parameter order — it decides what can be partially applied without a wrapper.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define d (list 2 3 4))\n(define r (xcons d 1))\n(display (list 'car (car r) 'cdr (cdr r) 'len (length r) 'expected-len (+ 1 (length d))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (flip-law? d a) (equal? (xcons d a) (cons a d)))\n(display (list (flip-law? (list 2 3) 1)\n               (flip-law? (list) 'a)\n               (flip-law? (list 'p 'q 'r) (list 'nested))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'empty-tail-proper (proper-list? (xcons (list) 1))\n               'atom-tail-proper (proper-list? (xcons 3 1))\n               'grows (= (length (xcons (list 1 2) 0)) 3)))\n(newline)"
     :claim "For every value a and every proper list d, (xcons d a) equals (cons a d): the car is a, the cdr is d, and the length is one more than the length of d. When d is not a list the result is an improper (dotted) pair and proper-list? answers #f."
     :invariant "forall a, forall proper list d. xcons(d, a) = cons(a, d) and car(xcons(d,a)) = a and |xcons(d,a)| = |d| + 1"
     :meta "The pattern is constructor-with-exchanged-operands. What would falsify it: a case where (xcons d a) and (cons a d) disagree, or a proper-list tail that came back dotted. See book-of-code on pairs and improper lists.")
    (:eng "The engineering payoff is that xcons never surprises on length: the result is always exactly one longer than the tail."
     :math "The flip law is an identity, not an approximation: xcons and cons are the same function up to argument order."
     :cs "The tail type is the discriminator between a proper list and a dotted pair — the one place a caller can get an unexpected shape.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (row-builder schema) (lambda (id) (xcons schema id)))\n(define make-row (row-builder (list 'sensor 'celsius)))\n(display (map make-row (list 101 102 103)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (copy xs) (fold-right (lambda (x acc) (xcons acc x)) (list) xs))\n(display (copy (list 1 2 3)))\n(newline)\n(display (equal? (copy (list 1 2 3)) (list 1 2 3)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (dot k v) (xcons v k))\n(define alist (map (lambda (p) (dot (car p) (cadr p))) (list (list 'a 1) (list 'b 2))))\n(display alist)\n(newline)\n(display (map pair? alist))\n(newline)"
     :base "xcons builds a pair with its arguments exchanged: tail first, head second."
     :emergent "eng: a record factory — close over the schema tail once, stamp every id with it. math: fold-right with xcons is the identity copy, because fold-right hands the accumulator second and xcons wants the tail first. cs: a dotted-pair factory for classic (key . value) alist entries, which Motoi otherwise rarely produces."
     :meta "The composition closes xcons over a fixed tail and maps it. What emerges is the adapter family: arg-order adapters that let a plain constructor ride inside a pipeline that supplies its arguments in the other order. See book-of-composition on partial application.")
    (:eng "Closing over the schema means the schema is written once and cannot drift between rows."
     :math "fold-right with xcons reconstructs the list exactly, which is the structural-recursion identity for lists."
     :cs "Dotted-pair alists are the classic Lisp map representation; xcons is how you build them without a lambda.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "proper-list?" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(proper-list? x) -> boolean"
  :summary "True when x is a finite, ()-terminated list; false for a dotted pair, a circular chain, or any non-list value."
  :explanation "Motoi represents a proper list directly, so anything built with list, map, append, filter or iota answers #t immediately. A dotted pair is minted only by cons, xcons or append-reverse onto a non-list tail; those chains are walked with Floyd cycle detection, so a circular structure answers #f instead of hanging the interpreter. proper-list? is total: every value gets #t or #f, never an error."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(proper-list? (list 1 2 3))" :note "A list built with list is proper: #t. So is the empty list.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(list (proper-list? (cons 1 2)) (proper-list? (cons 1 (list 2))))" :note "Consing onto 2 gives a dotted pair (#f); consing onto (2) gives a list (#t).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define candidates (list (list 1 2) (list) 42 \"text\" (cons 1 2) (cons 1 (list 2 3))))\n(map proper-list? candidates)" :note "A shape census over a mixed bag: (#t #t #f #f #f #t).")
  )
  :caveats ("The empty list is proper: (proper-list? (list)) is #t." "A non-list value such as a number, string or symbol is #f, not an error. proper-list? never raises." "Circular structure answers #f (the cdr walk uses tortoise-and-hare cycle detection) rather than looping forever." "proper-list? is a shape test only. It says nothing about the elements — (proper-list? (list (cons 1 2))) is #t because the outer structure is a list.")
  :usecases ("Guarding a module boundary before handing an argument to a list verb." "Classifying a mixed bag of values into lists and non-lists." "Teaching the difference between (1 2 3) and (1 2 . 3).")
  :related ("list?" "pair?" "null?" "xcons")
  :learn (:concept "Well-formedness of a recursive structure: walk to the end and check the terminator." :prerequisites ("list" "cons" "pair?") :progression "Learn pair? and null? first, then proper-list? as the whole-chain question those two answer only one link at a time.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define (safe-length x) (if (proper-list? x) (length x) 'not-a-list))\n(display (map safe-length (list (list 1 2 3) (cons 1 2) 42)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define base (list))\n(define step1 (cons 3 base))\n(define step2 (cons 2 step1))\n(define step3 (cons 1 step2))\n(display (list (proper-list? base) (proper-list? step1) (proper-list? step2) (proper-list? step3)))\n(newline)\n(display (proper-list? (cons 1 7)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (classify x)\n  (cond ((proper-list? x) 'proper)\n        ((pair? x) 'improper)\n        (else 'atom)))\n(display (map classify (list (list 1 2) (cons 1 2) 42 (list))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator receives a value of unknown shape from a caller and is about to hand it to length, map or fold. Candidates: proper-list? (asks the whole-structure question in one call and never raises), pair? (only tells you about the first link — (pair? (cons 1 2)) is #t but the value is not a list), or just calling the list verb and catching the error (works, but turns a predicate into an exception path). For a shape guard, proper-list? is the honest verb."
     :explanation "eng: one guard converts a would-be crash into a reportable value. math: the list set is defined inductively — () is in it and cons of anything onto a member is in it — and proper-list? is exactly that set membership test. cs: a three-way classify (proper / improper / atom) is the complete taxonomy of what a cdr walk can land on."
     :meta "Why proper-list? and not pair?: pair? inspects one link. A dotted pair passes pair? and then fails inside the list verb, several frames away from the caller who made the mistake.")
    (:eng "Guard at the boundary, not in the middle. A predicate at the door names the caller; an exception three frames in does not."
     :math "proper-list? is the characteristic function of the least set containing () and closed under cons with a list cdr."
     :cs "Same role as isinstance(x, list) in Python or Array.isArray in JavaScript, except it must also reject the improper chains that those languages cannot even build.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "A well-formedness check on a recursive structure: follow every link to the end and ask whether the end is the expected terminator."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(proper-list? (list 1 2 3))")
             (:domain "a paper chain (follow every link and see whether the last one closes)" :code "(define chain (cons 'a (cons 'b (list 'c))))\n(display (list 'chain chain 'closed? (proper-list? chain)))\n(newline)\n(define broken (cons 'a (cons 'b 'c)))\n(display (list 'broken broken 'closed? (proper-list? broken)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "membership in an inductively defined set, by structural recursion" :code "(define (member-of-list-set? x)\n  (cond ((null? x) #t)\n        ((pair? x) (member-of-list-set? (cdr x)))\n        (else #f)))\n(display (map member-of-list-set? (list (list 1 2) (cons 1 2) 7)))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, proper-list? walks the cdr chain. A paper chain is well-formed only if the last link is closed — one open link at the end and the whole thing is not a chain. In math, membership in an inductively defined set is decided by recursing on the structure until a base case. All three share: local links are not enough, the terminator decides."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a structure is built link by link and a client needs to know that the last link is the right kind before walking it.")
    (:eng "A structure can be locally valid at every link and globally invalid at the last one; only a full walk sees it."
     :math "Structural recursion terminates on well-founded data; the cycle detector is what makes it total on ill-founded data too."
     :cs "Floyd tortoise-and-hare is the standard bounded walk over a possibly-cyclic chain — the same trick as cycle detection in a linked list.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define vals (list (list) (list 1) (list 1 2 3) (cons 1 2) (cons 1 (list 2)) 0 \"s\" (list (list 1))))\n(display (map proper-list? vals))\n(newline)\n(display (list 'all-boolean (every boolean? (map proper-list? vals))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (closed-under-cons? xs)\n  (and (proper-list? xs) (proper-list? (cons 'new xs))))\n(display (list (closed-under-cons? (list)) (closed-under-cons? (list 1 2))))\n(newline)\n(display (proper-list? (cons 'new 7)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (guarded x thunk) (if (proper-list? x) (thunk) 'refused))\n(display (guarded (cons 1 2) (lambda () 'ran)))\n(newline)\n(display (guarded (list 1 2) (lambda () 'ran)))\n(newline)"
     :claim "proper-list? is total and boolean-valued on every input: it answers #t exactly for finite ()-terminated chains, #f for everything else, and never raises. The list set is closed under cons when the cdr is already a list, and not closed when it is not."
     :invariant "forall x. proper-list?(x) is a boolean and proper-list?(x) = #t iff x is in List, the least set containing () and closed under cons onto a List"
     :meta "The pattern is a total shape predicate. What would falsify it: an input that raises instead of answering, a non-boolean answer, or a finite ()-terminated chain that came back #f. See book-of-code on inductive data definitions.")
    (:eng "Totality is the payoff: a guard that can itself throw is not a guard."
     :math "Closure under cons is the induction step; () is the base case. Together they are the definition, and proper-list? decides it."
     :cs "A predicate that is total lets you write a two-branch dispatch instead of a try/catch, which keeps the error path out of the control flow.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (validate-args args)\n  (if (proper-list? args)\n      (list 'ok (length args))\n      (list 'reject 'not-a-proper-list)))\n(display (map validate-args (list (list 1 2) (cons 1 2))))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define bag (list (list 1) 2 (list) (cons 3 4) (list 5 6)))\n(display (call-with-values (lambda () (partition proper-list? bag)) list))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define inputs (list (list 1 2) (cons 9 9) (list 3) 7))\n(define good (filter proper-list? inputs))\n(display (list 'kept (length good) 'total-elems (apply + (map length good))))\n(newline)"
     :base "proper-list? answers, in one call, whether a value is a finite ()-terminated list."
     :emergent "eng: an argument validator at a module boundary that names the offending shape instead of crashing inside. math: partition on proper-list? splits a heterogeneous bag into exactly the two equivalence classes the predicate induces. cs: filter-then-aggregate, where the guard makes the aggregate (apply + (map length ...)) safe by construction."
     :meta "The composition uses proper-list? as the discriminator inside partition and filter. What emerges is the sieve family: a total predicate turning a mixed input into a homogeneous one that downstream verbs can trust. See book-of-composition on guard-then-aggregate.")
    (:eng "Filtering before aggregating means the aggregate never needs its own guard."
     :math "A predicate partitions its domain into two classes; partition materializes both instead of discarding one."
     :cs "Guard-then-aggregate is the pipeline form of a precondition — the type check moves from the callee into the data.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "cons*" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(cons* e1 e2 ... tail) -> list"
  :summary "Cons every leading argument onto the final argument, in one call."
  :explanation "cons* prepends a known run of elements to an existing list without nesting cons calls and without allocating a throwaway list for append. The LAST argument is the tail; everything before it is an element. One argument is returned unchanged, and no arguments gives the empty list. In Motoi a non-list tail is folded in as a single element, which is a divergence from SRFI-1 — see the caveats."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(cons* 1 2 (list 3 4))" :note "Two elements prepended to a list: (1 2 3 4).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define (with-header rows) (cons* 'name 'score rows))\n(with-header (list 'alice 'bob))" :note "Prepend a header run to a body: (name score alice bob).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(cons* 5)" :note "A single argument is the tail and is returned unchanged: 5. That is the SRFI-1 base case, and it is why (cons* x) is not the same as (list x).")
  )
  :caveats ("The LAST argument is the tail, not an element. (cons* 1 2 3) does not make a three-element list out of three elements by accident — it makes one because of the divergence below." "DIVERGENCE from SRFI-1: when the final argument is not a list, SRFI-1 builds an improper list — (cons* 1 2 3) is (1 2 . 3) in a conforming Scheme. Motoi returns the proper list (1 2 3) instead. To get the dotted result in Motoi, nest cons explicitly: (cons 1 (cons 2 3))." "One argument returns that argument unchanged; zero arguments return the empty list rather than raising.")
  :usecases ("Prepending a fixed header or prefix to a payload list." "Assembling a call form (operator followed by an argument list) programmatically." "Building an argument vector for apply from a fixed part plus a rest part.")
  :related ("cons" "append" "xcons" "list")
  :learn (:concept "Bulk prepend: attach a known run of leading items to an existing sequence in one step." :prerequisites ("cons" "list" "append") :progression "Learn cons for one element, cons* for several, append for joining two whole lists.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define body (list 'x 'y))\n(display (cons* 'begin 'setup body))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (nested-cons a b tail) (cons a (cons b tail)))\n(define tail (list 3 4))\n(display (equal? (cons* 1 2 tail) (nested-cons 1 2 tail)))\n(newline)\n(display (equal? (cons* 1 2 tail) (append (list 1 2) tail)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'one-arg (cons* 5)\n               'one-list (cons* (list 1 2))\n               'len (length (cons* 1 2 (list 3)))\n               'motoi-divergence (cons* 1 2 3)))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator has three known leading elements and an existing list, and needs them joined. Candidates: cons* (one call, the tail stays where it is), nested cons (correct but the parentheses stack up and the reader loses count), or (append (list a b c) tail) (correct, but it allocates a throwaway three-element list just to throw it away). For prepending a known run, cons* is the honest verb."
     :explanation "eng: a header run and a body joined in one call, with the body untouched. math: cons* agrees with both the nested-cons form and the append form on every proper-list tail, so it is a notation, not a new operation. cs: the base cases matter — one argument passes through, and a non-list tail is where Motoi diverges from SRFI-1."
     :meta "Why cons* and not append: append needs the prefix already packaged as a list. If the prefix is a handful of loose values, cons* takes them as arguments and skips the packaging.")
    (:eng "Prefer cons* when the prefix is loose values; prefer append when the prefix is already a list."
     :math "cons*(e1..en, t) = foldr(cons, t, [e1..en]) — the right-nested iterated cons."
     :cs "Same slot as spread-prepend elsewhere: [a, b, ...tail] in JavaScript, (a, b, *tail) in Python, list(a, b) ++ tail in Haskell.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Bulk prepend: attach a known run of leading items to the front of a sequence that already exists, without touching the sequence."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(cons* 1 2 (list 3 4))")
             (:domain "a queue where two people step in front of the line already formed" :code "(define line (list 'dana 'evan))\n(display (cons* 'ana 'ben line))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "iterated cons: the right-nested fold c(e1, c(e2, ... tail))" :code "(define (icons es tail) (fold-right cons tail es))\n(display (icons (list 1 2) (list 3 4)))\n(newline)\n(display (equal? (icons (list 1 2) (list 3 4)) (cons* 1 2 (list 3 4))))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, cons* prepends several elements at once. At a queue, two people stepping to the front do not disturb the order of the line behind them. In math, iterated cons is the right-nested fold that cons* abbreviates. All three share: the existing sequence is untouched and a known run is placed ahead of it."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a fixed prefix must lead a variable body and the body must not be copied or reordered.")
    (:eng "Not disturbing the tail is the property that makes this cheap; append-at-the-end does not have it."
     :math "cons* is syntactic sugar over fold-right with cons — a notation for a fold that appears often enough to deserve a name."
     :cs "Prepend is O(k) in the prefix length and O(1) in the tail length; appending at the far end is the expensive direction.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define tail (list 'c 'd 'e))\n(define r (cons* 'a 'b tail))\n(display (list 'result r 'len (length r) 'expected (+ 2 (length tail))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (append-law? es tail) (equal? (apply cons* (append es (list tail))) (append es tail)))\n(display (list (append-law? (list 1 2) (list 3 4))\n               (append-law? (list) (list 3))\n               (append-law? (list 9) (list))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'srfi-1-would-be-dotted (cons 1 (cons 2 3))\n               'motoi-gives (cons* 1 2 3)\n               'motoi-proper? (proper-list? (cons* 1 2 3))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "When the final argument is a proper list, (cons* e1 ... en tail) equals (append (list e1 ... en) tail) and its length is n + |tail|. A single argument is returned unchanged. When the final argument is NOT a list, Motoi appends it as one more element instead of building the SRFI-1 improper list."
     :invariant "forall e1..en, forall proper list t. cons*(e1,...,en,t) = append([e1,...,en], t) and |cons*(...)| = n + |t| and cons*(x) = x"
     :meta "The pattern is bulk prepend. What would falsify it: a length that is not n + |t|, or a tail whose order changed. The cs program documents a KNOWN divergence from SRFI-1 rather than a falsification — the dotted form must be built with nested cons in Motoi. See broken-examples on improper lists.")
    (:eng "The length law is the cheap check: n arguments before the tail means n more elements, always."
     :math "The append law says cons* adds no new behavior — it is a different spelling of an existing composition."
     :cs "The non-list tail is the one place where a program written against another Scheme will behave differently under Motoi. Test it explicitly.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (frame kind seq payload) (cons* kind seq payload))\n(display (frame 'data 7 (list 'a 'b 'c)))\n(newline)\n(display (frame 'ack 8 (list)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (call-form op args) (cons* op args))\n(display (map (lambda (a) (call-form '+ a)) (list (list 1 2) (list 3 4 5))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (invoke op fixed rest) (apply op (cons* fixed rest)))\n(display (invoke + 10 (list 1 2 3)))\n(newline)\n(display (invoke list 'tag (list 1 2)))\n(newline)"
     :base "cons* prepends a known run of elements onto an existing list."
     :emergent "eng: message framing — a protocol header (kind, sequence number) placed ahead of an untouched payload. math: s-expression construction — an operator symbol placed ahead of its argument list is exactly a call form, which is how a Lisp writes programs as data. cs: argument-vector assembly for apply, where a fixed leading argument is joined to a variable rest before the call."
     :meta "The composition puts cons* between a fixed prefix and a variable body. What emerges is the framing family: headers, call forms, and argument vectors are all the same shape. See book-of-composition on code-as-data.")
    (:eng "A frame built with cons* keeps the payload shared rather than copied, so framing is cheap even for large payloads."
     :math "Operator-then-operands IS the term structure of the language; cons* is the constructor for terms."
     :cs "cons* plus apply is the general call-forwarding idiom — the Scheme equivalent of f(fixed, *rest).")
)
)

(verb
  :name "list-tabulate" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(list-tabulate n proc) -> list"
  :summary "Build the n-element list whose element at index i is (proc i)."
  :explanation "Say how many you want and say the rule; list-tabulate fills every slot. It is the index-driven constructor: where iota gives you the indices, list-tabulate gives you the values computed FROM the indices, without an intermediate list. n is clamped at zero, so a negative count yields the empty list rather than an error."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(list-tabulate 5 (lambda (i) (* i i)))" :note "The first five squares: (0 1 4 9 16).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(list-tabulate 4 (lambda (i) (list i (even? i))))" :note "Index paired with a property: ((0 #t) (1 #f) (2 #t) (3 #f)).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (times-table n)\n  (list-tabulate n (lambda (r) (list-tabulate n (lambda (c) (* (+ r 1) (+ c 1)))))))\n(times-table 3)" :note "Nesting list-tabulate inside itself builds a square table: ((1 2 3) (2 4 6) (3 6 9)).")
  )
  :caveats ("The index starts at 0 and stops at n-1, so (list-tabulate 3 f) calls f on 0, 1, 2." "n is clamped and truncated: a negative n gives the empty list and a fractional n is truncated toward zero. Neither raises." "proc is called once per slot, in index order. If proc has an effect, the effect order is 0 upward.")
  :usecases ("Building lookup or fee tables of a known size." "Initializing a board, grid or matrix." "Precomputing a memo table indexed from zero.")
  :related ("iota" "map" "unfold" "make-vector")
  :learn (:concept "Index-driven construction: name the size, name the rule, fill every slot." :prerequisites ("lambda" "iota" "map") :progression "Learn iota for the indices themselves, then list-tabulate to skip straight to the values, then unfold when the stopping rule is not a count.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define (fee-table n) (list-tabulate n (lambda (i) (list i (* 5 i)))))\n(display (fee-table 4))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (graph-of f n) (list-tabulate n (lambda (i) (list i (f i)))))\n(display (graph-of (lambda (x) (* x x x)) 5))\n(newline)\n(display (equal? (list-tabulate 5 (lambda (i) i)) (iota 5)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (checked-tabulate n proc)\n  (if (and (integer? n) (>= n 0))\n      (list-tabulate n proc)\n      (list 'refused n)))\n(display (list (checked-tabulate 3 (lambda (i) (* 2 i)))\n               (checked-tabulate -3 (lambda (i) i))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator knows how many rows a table needs and knows the rule that turns a row number into a row. Candidates: list-tabulate (count plus rule, one call), (map f (iota n)) (correct, but builds the index list first and then throws it away), or a hand-written named-let counting loop (correct, but four lines of bookkeeping for one idea). For count-plus-rule, list-tabulate is the honest verb."
     :explanation "eng: a fee table where the rule is stated once and the size is a parameter. math: list-tabulate materializes the graph of a function restricted to {0..n-1}; with the identity rule it reproduces iota exactly. cs: n is clamped rather than validated, so a caller who wants a hard error on a bad count must add the check at the boundary."
     :meta "Why list-tabulate and not (map f (iota n)): the map form is correct and readable, but it allocates the index list. list-tabulate names the intent — size and rule — without the intermediate.")
    (:eng "Prefer list-tabulate when the value depends on the index; prefer make-list when every slot is the same."
     :math "list-tabulate(n, f) = [f(0), f(1), ..., f(n-1)] — the finite graph of f on an initial segment of the naturals."
     :cs "Same slot as Array.from({length:n}, (_,i)=>f(i)) in JavaScript, [f(i) for i in range(n)] in Python, Vector.generate in Haskell.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Index-driven construction: state the size and state the rule, and let the rule fill each numbered slot."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(list-tabulate 5 (lambda (i) (* i i)))")
             (:domain "printing numbered raffle tickets (you say how many, the press fills in the number)" :code "(display (list-tabulate 5 (lambda (i) (list 'ticket (+ 1 i)))))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "the graph of a function restricted to {0, ..., n-1}" :code "(define (restrict f n) (list-tabulate n (lambda (i) (list i (f i)))))\n(display (restrict (lambda (x) (* 2 x)) 4))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, list-tabulate fills n slots from a rule on the index. A ticket press does the same: the operator sets the count and the machine supplies the running number. In math, restricting a function to a finite initial segment and listing the pairs is the same construction. All three share: a count, a rule, and a filled sequence."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever the number of outputs is known in advance and each output is determined by its position.")
    (:eng "Separating the count from the rule means either can change without touching the other."
     :math "A function is its graph; on a finite domain the graph is a list, and this is how you get it."
     :cs "Position-determined generation is what makes the result reproducible and parallelizable — no slot depends on another.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define r (list-tabulate 6 (lambda (i) (* 3 i))))\n(display (list 'len (length r) 'value r))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (index-law? n f)\n  (let ((r (list-tabulate n f)))\n    (let loop ((i 0))\n      (cond ((= i n) #t)\n            ((equal? (list-ref r i) (f i)) (loop (+ i 1)))\n            (else #f)))))\n(display (list (index-law? 5 (lambda (i) (* i i)))\n               (index-law? 0 (lambda (i) i))\n               (index-law? 3 (lambda (i) (list i)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'zero (list-tabulate 0 (lambda (i) i))\n               'negative (list-tabulate -4 (lambda (i) i))\n               'same-as-map (equal? (list-tabulate 4 (lambda (i) (* i i)))\n                                    (map (lambda (i) (* i i)) (iota 4)))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "list-tabulate returns a list of exactly n elements whose element at index i equals (proc i) for every i in [0, n). A count of zero or less gives the empty list, and the result always equals (map proc (iota n))."
     :invariant "forall n >= 0, forall proc. |list-tabulate(n, proc)| = n and forall i in [0,n). list-tabulate(n,proc)[i] = proc(i)"
     :meta "The pattern is bounded index-driven generation. What would falsify it: a length other than n, an element that does not match proc at its own index, or a negative count that raised instead of returning empty. See book-of-math on function graphs.")
    (:eng "The length law makes the result safe to zip against any other n-element list without a length check."
     :math "The index law is extensional equality with the restricted function — it is the definition, stated as a test."
     :cs "Clamping instead of raising is a deliberate choice: it makes the verb total, at the cost of hiding a caller bug. Add the check yourself if you want it loud.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (board rows cols fill)\n  (list-tabulate rows (lambda (r) (list-tabulate cols (lambda (c) (fill r c))))))\n(display (board 3 3 (lambda (r c) (if (= r c) 'x 'o))))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (next-row prev)\n  (list-tabulate (+ 1 (length prev))\n                 (lambda (i)\n                   (+ (if (= i 0) 0 (list-ref prev (- i 1)))\n                      (if (= i (length prev)) 0 (list-ref prev i))))))\n(define (pascal n)\n  (let loop ((k 1) (row (list 1)) (acc (list (list 1))))\n    (if (= k n)\n        (reverse acc)\n        (let ((nr (next-row row))) (loop (+ k 1) nr (cons nr acc))))))\n(display (pascal 5))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (buckets n items)\n  (list-tabulate n (lambda (b) (filter (lambda (x) (= b (modulo x n))) items))))\n(display (buckets 3 (list 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8)))\n(newline)"
     :base "list-tabulate fills n slots from a rule on the index."
     :emergent "eng: board initialization — nesting list-tabulate inside itself gives a rows-by-cols grid whose contents depend on both coordinates. math: Pascal triangle — each row is tabulated from the row above, so the construction is a recurrence rather than a closed form. cs: hash-bucket layout — tabulating over bucket indices and filtering the items into them is the whole of a chained hash table body."
     :meta "The composition nests list-tabulate or feeds its output back into its own rule. What emerges is the table family: grids, recurrence triangles, and bucket arrays are all index-driven fills. See book-of-composition on nested generation.")
    (:eng "A grid built this way has no mutation and no aliasing between rows, so it is safe to hand out."
     :math "Pascal shows the rule may reference the previous result — tabulation and recurrence compose."
     :cs "Bucket layout is the standard hash-table body; the tabulate form makes the modulo policy visible in one line.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "append-reverse" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(append-reverse rev-head tail) -> list"
  :summary "The same list as (append (reverse rev-head) tail), built in a single pass."
  :explanation "This is the accumulator flush. A loop that builds its answer with cons ends up holding it backwards; append-reverse turns that backwards accumulator around and joins it to whatever comes next, without the intermediate list that (append (reverse acc) tail) would allocate. The head is reversed, the tail is not. A non-list tail is legal, as in SRFI-1, and produces an improper list."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(append-reverse (list 3 2 1) (list 4 5))" :note "The head is turned around and joined: (1 2 3 4 5). The tail keeps its order.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define (collect-evens xs)\n  (let loop ((rest xs) (acc (list)))\n    (cond ((null? rest) (append-reverse acc (list 'done)))\n          ((even? (car rest)) (loop (cdr rest) (cons (car rest) acc)))\n          (else (loop (cdr rest) acc)))))\n(collect-evens (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))" :note "The classic accumulator flush: the loop conses (so acc is backwards) and append-reverse rights it at the end: (2 4 6 done).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(append-reverse (list 1 2) 9)" :note "A non-list tail is legal and gives an improper list: (2 1 . 9). This is SRFI-1 behavior, not a Motoi quirk.")
  )
  :caveats ("Only the head is reversed. The tail is spliced on as it stands." "A non-list tail produces an improper (dotted) list. Guard with proper-list? if downstream code walks the result." "(append-reverse xs (list)) is a perfectly good reverse, and (append-reverse (list) t) returns t unchanged.")
  :usecases ("Flushing a cons-built accumulator at the end of a loop." "Joining a reversed prefix to a already-correct suffix in one pass." "The rotation step of an amortized two-stack queue.")
  :related ("reverse" "append" "fold" "cons")
  :learn (:concept "Accumulator flush: turn a backwards-built prefix around and attach it to what follows." :prerequisites ("reverse" "append" "cons") :progression "Learn reverse and append separately first, then append-reverse as the fused one-pass form that loops actually want.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define (parse-until-stop tokens)\n  (let loop ((rest tokens) (acc (list)))\n    (cond ((null? rest) (append-reverse acc (list)))\n          ((eq? (car rest) 'stop) (append-reverse acc (cons 'stopped (cdr rest))))\n          (else (loop (cdr rest) (cons (car rest) acc))))))\n(display (parse-until-stop (list 'a 'b 'stop 'c 'd)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (law? h t) (equal? (append-reverse h t) (append (reverse h) t)))\n(display (list (law? (list 3 2 1) (list 4 5))\n               (law? (list) (list 1))\n               (law? (list 1 2) (list))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'proper (append-reverse (list 1 2) (list 3))\n               'dotted (append-reverse (list 1 2) 9)\n               'dotted-is-proper? (proper-list? (append-reverse (list 1 2) 9))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator wrote a loop that accumulates with cons, so the accumulator is in reverse order, and now needs it in forward order joined to a suffix. Candidates: append-reverse (one pass, no intermediate), (append (reverse acc) tail) (two passes and one throwaway list), or building the accumulator with append at each step (correct but quadratic). For flushing a reversed accumulator, append-reverse is the honest verb."
     :explanation "eng: the parser conses tokens as it scans, then flushes once at the stop marker. math: append-reverse is exactly append-of-reverse; the two agree on every proper-list input, including both empty cases. cs: the tail decides the shape of the result — a list tail gives a proper list, anything else gives a dotted one, which is legal SRFI-1 and worth checking at the boundary."
     :meta "Why append-reverse and not (append (reverse acc) tail): the two-verb form allocates the reversed list and then copies it again into the append. The fused verb does it once.")
    (:eng "Cons-then-flush is the standard shape for a linear accumulator loop; append-at-each-step is the quadratic mistake it avoids."
     :math "append-reverse(h, t) = append(reverse(h), t), with reverse(h) = append-reverse(h, ()) as the special case."
     :cs "Same slot as reverse-into / prepend-reversed in other libraries; Haskell reverse xs ++ ys, Clojure into with a reversed transducer.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Flush a stack onto a queue: the accumulator that grew backwards is emptied, front first, onto whatever is already waiting."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(append-reverse (list 3 2 1) (list 4 5))")
             (:domain "unstacking plates onto a tray that already holds some" :code "(define stack (list 'top 'middle 'bottom))\n(define tray (list 'cup))\n(display (append-reverse stack tray))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "the two-step law it collapses: reverse, then append" :code "(define (slow-append-reverse h t) (append (reverse h) t))\n(display (slow-append-reverse (list 3 2 1) (list 4 5)))\n(newline)\n(display (equal? (slow-append-reverse (list 3 2 1) (list 4 5))\n                 (append-reverse (list 3 2 1) (list 4 5))))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, append-reverse empties a reversed head onto a tail. Unstacking plates does the same thing physically: the plate on top came last and lands first, so the stack arrives on the tray in the order it was built. In math, the law is reverse-then-append, and append-reverse is that composition given one name. All three share: a last-in-first-out pile becoming a first-in-first-out sequence."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever something was accumulated in the cheap direction and must be delivered in the readable direction.")
    (:eng "Build in the cheap direction, deliver in the correct one. The flush is the seam between them."
     :math "reverse is an involution, so a flushed accumulator can be flushed back and recovered exactly."
     :cs "LIFO to FIFO conversion is the core of the amortized queue; the cost of the flush is paid once per element.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define h (list 3 2 1))\n(define t (list 4 5))\n(define r (append-reverse h t))\n(display (list 'result r 'len (length r) 'expected (+ (length h) (length t))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (identities? h t)\n  (list (equal? (append-reverse h t) (append (reverse h) t))\n        (equal? (append-reverse (list) t) t)\n        (equal? (append-reverse h (list)) (reverse h))))\n(display (identities? (list 1 2 3) (list 9 8)))\n(newline)\n(display (identities? (list) (list)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (twice-is-identity? xs)\n  (equal? (append-reverse (append-reverse xs (list)) (list)) xs))\n(display (map twice-is-identity? (list (list) (list 1) (list 1 2 3 4))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "For proper lists h and t, append-reverse(h,t) equals append(reverse(h),t) and has length |h| + |t|. An empty head returns the tail unchanged, an empty tail recovers reverse, and flushing twice with an empty tail is the identity."
     :invariant "forall proper lists h, t. append-reverse(h,t) = append(reverse(h),t) and |append-reverse(h,t)| = |h|+|t| and append-reverse(append-reverse(h,()),()) = h"
     :meta "The pattern is a fused reverse-append. What would falsify it: a length other than |h|+|t|, disagreement with the two-verb form, or a double flush that did not return the original. See book-of-math on involutions.")
    (:eng "The length law lets a loop predict its output size before the flush."
     :math "The double-flush identity is reverse being its own inverse — the defining property of an involution."
     :cs "Fusing two traversals into one is the classic deforestation optimization, done here by hand and given a name.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (split-on sep xs)\n  (let loop ((rest xs) (cur (list)) (out (list)))\n    (cond ((null? rest) (reverse (cons (append-reverse cur (list)) out)))\n          ((eq? (car rest) sep) (loop (cdr rest) (list) (cons (append-reverse cur (list)) out)))\n          (else (loop (cdr rest) (cons (car rest) cur) out)))))\n(display (split-on 'gap (list 'a 'b 'gap 'c 'gap 'd)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (rev xs) (append-reverse xs (list)))\n(display (list (rev (list 1 2 3)) (rev (rev (list 1 2 3)))))\n(newline)\n(define (append2 a b) (append-reverse (rev a) b))\n(display (append2 (list 1 2) (list 3 4)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (make-queue front back) (list front back))\n(define (dequeue q)\n  (let ((front (car q)) (back (cadr q)))\n    (if (null? front)\n        (let ((f (append-reverse back (list))))\n          (if (null? f)\n              (list 'empty q)\n              (list (car f) (make-queue (cdr f) (list)))))\n        (list (car front) (make-queue (cdr front) back)))))\n(define q (make-queue (list) (list 'c 'b 'a)))\n(display (dequeue q))\n(newline)"
     :base "append-reverse turns a backwards-built head around and joins it to a tail in one pass."
     :emergent "eng: a tokenizer that splits a stream on a separator, flushing each chunk as it closes. math: reverse and append are both recoverable from append-reverse alone, so the verb generates the whole free-monoid toolkit by itself. cs: the amortized two-stack queue — append-reverse IS the rotation step that makes a functional FIFO queue O(1) amortized per operation."
     :meta "The composition puts append-reverse at the boundary of a loop iteration. What emerges is the chunk-flush family: tokenizers, batchers, and the banker queue rotation. See book-of-composition on accumulator loops and book-of-cs-curriculum on amortized analysis.")
    (:eng "Flushing per chunk keeps each chunk linear, so the whole tokenizer stays linear in the input."
     :math "reverse = append-reverse with an empty tail, and append = append-reverse after a reverse; one primitive, two derived operations."
     :cs "The banker queue is the standard example of amortized analysis in a purely functional setting; the rotation cost is spread over the pushes that caused it.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "take-right" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(take-right lst k) -> list"
  :summary "The last k elements of lst, counted from the far end."
  :explanation "take-right selects a suffix by SIZE rather than by position, so the caller never computes (- (length lst) k) by hand and never gets that subtraction off by one. k must lie between 0 and the length: asking for more elements than the list holds raises a teaching error rather than quietly returning a short list."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(take-right (list 1 2 3 4 5) 2)" :note "The last two: (4 5).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define (tail-n lines n) (take-right lines (min n (length lines))))\n(tail-n (list 'l1 'l2 'l3) 10)" :note "Clamping with min turns the out-of-range error into a safe short answer: (l1 l2 l3).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (windows xs k)\n  (map (lambda (i) (take-right (take xs (+ i k)) k))\n       (iota (+ 1 (- (length xs) k)))))\n(windows (list 1 2 3 4 5) 3)" :note "Every sliding window of width 3: ((1 2 3) (2 3 4) (3 4 5)).")
  )
  :caveats ("k must be in [0, length]. Asking for more than the list holds RAISES; it does not clamp. Clamp yourself with min if a short answer is acceptable." "A negative k is clamped to 0 and returns the empty list." "In a classic pair-based Scheme take-right returns the SHARED tail of the original list. Motoi returns a fresh list of the same elements, so sublist identity is not observable — only the values are.")
  :usecases ("Tailing the last N lines of a log." "Reading a fixed-width trailer or checksum off the end of a record." "Building sliding windows together with take.")
  :related ("drop-right" "take" "list-tail" "split-at" "last")
  :learn (:concept "Suffix selection by size: count from the far end instead of from the start." :prerequisites ("list" "length" "take") :progression "Learn take (prefix by size) first, then take-right as its mirror, then split-at when you want both halves at once.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define log (list 'boot 'load 'warn 'error 'retry 'ok))\n(display (take-right log 3))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (complement-law? l k)\n  (equal? (append (drop-right l k) (take-right l k)) l))\n(display (map (lambda (k) (complement-law? (list 1 2 3 4 5) k)) (iota 6)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (safe-take-right l k)\n  (if (<= k (length l)) (take-right l k) (list 'refused 'k k 'len (length l))))\n(display (safe-take-right (list 1 2 3) 2))\n(newline)\n(display (safe-take-right (list 1 2 3) 9))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator needs the last three entries of a log. Candidates: take-right (says the size and counts from the end), (list-tail log (- (length log) 3)) (correct, but the caller does the subtraction and owns the off-by-one), or (reverse (take (reverse log) 3)) (correct, three passes, and the answer comes out backwards unless reversed again). For a suffix known by size, take-right is the honest verb."
     :explanation "eng: the last three log entries, with no arithmetic at the call site. math: take-right and drop-right are exact complements — appending them in that order reconstructs the original for every k from 0 to the length. cs: the out-of-range case raises, so a caller who may over-ask must clamp at the boundary rather than downstream."
     :meta "Why take-right and not list-tail with a computed index: the subtraction is where the bug lives. take-right takes the number the caller actually has in mind.")
    (:eng "Prefer take-right when you know how many you want from the end; prefer list-tail when you know the index to start from."
     :math "take-right(l,k) is the unique suffix s with |s| = k such that l = drop-right(l,k) ++ s."
     :cs "Same slot as lst[-k:] in Python, takeRight in Scala, .slice(-k) in JavaScript.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Suffix by size: select the trailing run of a sequence by how many you want, not by where it starts."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(take-right (list 1 2 3 4 5) 2)")
             (:domain "the last two carriages of a train (you count from the caboose, not from the engine)" :code "(define train (list 'engine 'coal 'freight 'passenger 'caboose))\n(display (take-right train 2))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "the suffix of length k, complement of the prefix of length n-k" :code "(define (suffix l k) (list-tail l (- (length l) k)))\n(display (suffix (list 1 2 3 4 5) 2))\n(newline)\n(display (equal? (suffix (list 1 2 3 4 5) 2) (take-right (list 1 2 3 4 5) 2)))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, take-right counts back from the end. On a train, the last two carriages are named from the caboose because that is the end you are standing at. In math, a suffix of length k is the complement of the prefix of length n-k, and either description picks out the same segment. All three share: an endpoint you are near and a count you already know."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever the interesting part of a sequence is the recent part and the length of the whole is incidental.")
    (:eng "Naming the count instead of the index removes the one arithmetic step that can be wrong."
     :math "Prefix length plus suffix length equals total length; the two selectors are duals across that equation."
     :cs "Negative indexing exists in many languages for exactly this reason; take-right is the named-verb version of it.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define l (list 'a 'b 'c 'd 'e))\n(define r (take-right l 2))\n(display (list 'result r 'len (length r) 'is-suffix (equal? r (list-tail l (- (length l) 2)))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define l (list 1 2 3 4 5))\n(define (both-laws? k)\n  (and (= (length (take-right l k)) k)\n       (equal? (append (drop-right l k) (take-right l k)) l)))\n(display (map both-laws? (iota 6)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'k-zero (take-right (list 1 2 3) 0)\n               'k-all (take-right (list 1 2 3) 3)\n               'k-negative (take-right (list 1 2 3) -2)\n               'over-raises (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (take-right (list 1 2 3) 9))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "For 0 <= k <= |lst|, take-right returns exactly k elements, and they are the last k in their original order: appending drop-right and take-right at the same k reconstructs the list. k = 0 gives the empty list, k = |lst| gives the whole list, negative k is clamped to 0, and k > |lst| raises."
     :invariant "forall list l, forall k in [0,|l|]. |take-right(l,k)| = k and drop-right(l,k) ++ take-right(l,k) = l"
     :meta "The pattern is size-addressed suffix selection. What would falsify it: a result of the wrong length, an order change, or a k in range that failed to reconstruct under append. See book-of-math on set complements and book-of-cs-curriculum on boundary testing.")
    (:eng "The reconstruction law is the strongest single check: it catches both wrong length and wrong contents in one comparison."
     :math "Complement laws hold at both boundaries (k = 0 and k = n), which is where most off-by-one errors show up."
     :cs "Raising on over-ask instead of clamping means a caller bug surfaces at the call site. The clamp, when wanted, belongs to the caller.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (ring-push buf x cap)\n  (let ((grown (append buf (list x))))\n    (if (> (length grown) cap) (take-right grown cap) grown)))\n(define b1 (ring-push (list) 'a 3))\n(define b2 (ring-push b1 'b 3))\n(define b3 (ring-push b2 'c 3))\n(define b4 (ring-push b3 'd 3))\n(display (list b1 b2 b3 b4))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (split-by-suffix l k) (list (drop-right l k) (take-right l k)))\n(define parts (split-by-suffix (list 1 2 3 4 5 6) 2))\n(display parts)\n(newline)\n(display (equal? (append (car parts) (cadr parts)) (list 1 2 3 4 5 6)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (frame-trailer packet trailer-width)\n  (list 'body (drop-right packet trailer-width)\n        'checksum (take-right packet trailer-width)))\n(display (frame-trailer (list 'd1 'd2 'd3 'c1 'c2) 2))\n(newline)"
     :base "take-right selects the last k elements of a list."
     :emergent "eng: a bounded ring buffer — push, then keep only the last cap elements, and the buffer never grows past its cap. math: the suffix/prefix decomposition, which turns one list into an ordered pair of parts that append back exactly. cs: trailer parsing — a fixed-width checksum at the end of a packet is split off with take-right while drop-right keeps the body."
     :meta "The composition pairs take-right with drop-right at the same k. What emerges is the decomposition family: bounded buffers, trailer parsing, and any format whose meaning is fixed relative to the END of the data. See book-of-composition on complementary selectors.")
    (:eng "A ring buffer written this way has no index arithmetic at all, so it cannot wrap incorrectly."
     :math "The pair (drop-right, take-right) is a splitting of the list; split-at is the same idea addressed from the other end."
     :cs "End-anchored formats (trailers, checksums, magic footers) are exactly where counting from the front goes wrong.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "drop-right" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(drop-right lst k) -> list"
  :summary "Everything except the last k elements of lst."
  :explanation "The mirror of take-right: instead of keeping the trailing run, drop it. drop-right is how you strip a fixed-size trailer — a checksum, a terminator, a sentinel — without knowing or computing the length of the body. k must lie between 0 and the length; over-dropping raises rather than returning the empty list."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(drop-right (list 1 2 3 4 5) 2)" :note "Everything but the last two: (1 2 3).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define (strip-terminator record) (drop-right record 1))\n(strip-terminator (list 'a 'b 'c 'end))" :note "Remove the trailing sentinel: (a b c).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (body-and-check packet w)\n  (list (drop-right packet w) (take-right packet w)))\n(body-and-check (list 'd1 'd2 'd3 'c1 'c2) 2)" :note "drop-right and take-right at the same k split a record into body and trailer: ((d1 d2 d3) (c1 c2)).")
  )
  :caveats ("k must be in [0, length]. Dropping more than the list holds RAISES; it does not return the empty list." "A negative k is clamped to 0, so the whole list comes back unchanged." "drop-right always copies, because the answer is a prefix and a prefix cannot share structure with a linked list from the front.")
  :usecases ("Stripping a fixed-width trailer, checksum or sentinel." "Removing the last N samples of a signal to align two series." "Taking the body half of a body/trailer split.")
  :related ("take-right" "drop" "split-at" "list-head")
  :learn (:concept "Suffix removal by size: cut a known number of items off the end." :prerequisites ("list" "length" "take-right") :progression "Learn take-right first, then drop-right as the complement, then split-at when you want both parts in one call.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define record (list 'field-a 'field-b 'field-c 'crc))\n(display (drop-right record 1))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define l (list 1 2 3 4 5))\n(display (map (lambda (k) (= (length (drop-right l k)) (- (length l) k))) (iota 6)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (safe-drop-right l k)\n  (if (<= k (length l)) (drop-right l k) (list 'refused 'k k 'len (length l))))\n(display (safe-drop-right (list 1 2 3) 1))\n(newline)\n(display (safe-drop-right (list 1 2 3) 7))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator has records that carry a one-element checksum at the end and needs the body without it. Candidates: drop-right (says how many to cut off the end), (take record (- (length record) 1)) (correct, but the subtraction is at the call site), or reverse-cdr-reverse (correct and wasteful). For removing a known-size trailer, drop-right is the honest verb."
     :explanation "eng: the checksum comes off in one call and the body is untouched. math: the length law is exact for every k in range, including both ends. cs: over-dropping raises, so a stream whose records may be shorter than the trailer needs a guard before the cut."
     :meta "Why drop-right and not take with a computed count: the count is (- (length l) k), and computing it at every call site is how a one-element format change becomes a fleet-wide bug.")
    (:eng "Express the cut in the units the format is specified in. A checksum is described as N elements at the end, so cut N at the end."
     :math "drop-right(l,k) is the prefix of length |l| - k; it is the complement of take-right at the same k."
     :cs "Same slot as lst[:-k] in Python, dropRight in Scala, .slice(0, -k) in JavaScript.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Suffix removal by size: cut a fixed number of items off the far end and keep the rest."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(drop-right (list 1 2 3 4 5) 2)")
             (:domain "trimming the stub off a ticket (the stub is always the same size, wherever the ticket was torn from)" :code "(define ticket (list 'seat 'row 'date 'stub))\n(display (drop-right ticket 1))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "the prefix of length n-k, complement of the suffix of length k" :code "(define (prefix l k) (take l (- (length l) k)))\n(display (prefix (list 1 2 3 4 5) 2))\n(newline)\n(display (equal? (prefix (list 1 2 3 4 5) 2) (drop-right (list 1 2 3 4 5) 2)))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, drop-right removes a trailing run. A ticket stub is a fixed size and is removed from the end no matter how long the ticket is. In math, removing a suffix of length k leaves the prefix of length n-k. All three share: a fixed amount removed from a known end, with the remainder undisturbed."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a format appends a fixed-size region that the consumer must discard before reading the body.")
    (:eng "Trailer sizes belong in one constant; drop-right lets that constant be the only number in the code."
     :math "The two lengths sum to the whole, so either selector determines the other."
     :cs "End-anchored trailers are common precisely because they can be stripped without parsing the body first.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define l (list 'a 'b 'c 'd 'e))\n(define r (drop-right l 2))\n(display (list 'result r 'len (length r) 'expected (- (length l) 2)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define l (list 1 2 3 4 5))\n(define (laws? k)\n  (and (= (length (drop-right l k)) (- (length l) k))\n       (equal? (append (drop-right l k) (take-right l k)) l)\n       (equal? (drop-right l k) (take l (- (length l) k)))))\n(display (map laws? (iota 6)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'k-zero (drop-right (list 1 2 3) 0)\n               'k-all (drop-right (list 1 2 3) 3)\n               'k-negative (drop-right (list 1 2 3) -2)\n               'over-raises (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (drop-right (list 1 2 3) 9))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "For 0 <= k <= |lst|, drop-right returns the first |lst| - k elements in their original order, equals (take lst (- (length lst) k)), and appends with take-right at the same k to reconstruct the list. k = 0 returns the whole list, k = |lst| returns the empty list, negative k is clamped to 0, and k > |lst| raises."
     :invariant "forall list l, forall k in [0,|l|]. |drop-right(l,k)| = |l| - k and drop-right(l,k) ++ take-right(l,k) = l"
     :meta "The pattern is size-addressed suffix removal. What would falsify it: a length other than |l| - k, disagreement with the take form, or a failure to reconstruct under append. See book-of-cs-curriculum on boundary testing.")
    (:eng "Checking both boundary values of k (0 and the full length) catches the two most common off-by-one shapes."
     :math "drop-right and take-right are a splitting: together they are total and disjoint on the index set."
     :cs "The clamp on negative k and the raise on over-large k are deliberately asymmetric; know which one you are relying on.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (align-series a b)\n  (let ((n (min (length a) (length b))))\n    (list (drop-right a (- (length a) n))\n          (drop-right b (- (length b) n)))))\n(display (align-series (list 1 2 3 4 5) (list 10 20 30)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (differences xs)\n  (map - (cdr xs) (drop-right xs 1)))\n(display (differences (list 1 4 9 16 25)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (parse-record r trailer)\n  (list 'body (drop-right r trailer) 'trailer (take-right r trailer)))\n(display (map (lambda (r) (parse-record r 2))\n              (list (list 'a 'b 'c1 'c2) (list 'x 'y 'z 'c1 'c2))))\n(newline)"
     :base "drop-right removes the last k elements and keeps the prefix."
     :emergent "eng: series alignment — trimming the longer of two sequences from the end so both can be zipped element by element. math: finite differences — pairing each element with its predecessor is exactly (cdr xs) against (drop-right xs 1), which is the discrete derivative. cs: record parsing, where the same trailer width splits every record into body and trailer."
     :meta "The composition pairs drop-right with cdr or with take-right to make two aligned views of one list. What emerges is the pairing family: finite differences, sliding comparisons, and body/trailer splits. See book-of-math on difference sequences.")
    (:eng "Aligning by trimming from the end keeps the most recent samples, which is usually the correct choice for time series."
     :math "The finite-difference operator on a length-n list produces a length n-1 list; drop-right 1 is what makes the two operands the same length."
     :cs "Zipping two lists of different lengths is a classic silent-truncation bug; explicit alignment makes the policy visible.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "split-at" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(split-at lst i) -> two values: (prefix rest)"
  :summary "Cut a list at index i and return BOTH halves as two values."
  :explanation "split-at is take and drop in one traversal, and it returns TWO VALUES rather than a list of two lists. Consume it with call-with-values or let-values. i must lie between 0 and the length; splitting past the end raises rather than clamping. Note that SRFI-8 receive is NOT part of Motoi standard, so the SRFI-1 document examples that use receive must be rewritten with let-values."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(call-with-values (lambda () (split-at (list 1 2 3 4) 2)) list)" :note "call-with-values packages the two values into a list so you can see them: ((1 2) (3 4)).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let-values (((head rest) (split-at (list 1 2 3 4 5) 2)))\n  (list (quote head) head (quote rest) rest))" :note "let-values names both halves at once: (head (1 2) rest (3 4 5)).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (chunk xs n)\n  (if (< (length xs) n)\n      (list xs)\n      (let-values (((head rest) (split-at xs n)))\n        (cons head (chunk rest n)))))\n(chunk (list 1 2 3 4 5 6 7) 3)" :note "Recursive chunking: ((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7)).")
  )
  :caveats ("split-at returns TWO VALUES, not a two-element list. (car (split-at ...)) is a mistake; use call-with-values or let-values." "SRFI-8 receive, which the SRFI-1 document uses in its examples, is NOT bound in Motoi. Use let-values instead." "i must be in [0, length]. Splitting past the end RAISES; it does not clamp." "A negative i is clamped to 0, giving an empty prefix and the whole list as the rest.")
  :usecases ("Chunking a list into fixed-size batches." "Peeling a fixed-size header off the front of a record." "Any place take and drop are both wanted at the same index.")
  :related ("take" "drop" "span" "break" "partition" "drop-right")
  :learn (:concept "One cut, two pieces: split a sequence at a known index and keep both sides." :prerequisites ("take" "drop" "call-with-values") :progression "Learn take and drop separately, then split-at to get both without traversing twice, then span and break when the cut point is decided by a predicate instead of an index.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(let-values (((header body) (split-at (list 'v 1 'payload 'a 'b) 2)))\n  (display (list 'header header 'body body))\n  (newline))"
     :math-program "(define l (list 1 2 3 4 5))\n(define (splitting-law? i)\n  (let-values (((a b) (split-at l i)))\n    (and (= (length a) i) (= (length b) (- (length l) i)) (equal? (append a b) l))))\n(display (map splitting-law? (iota 6)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (safe-split l i)\n  (if (<= i (length l))\n      (call-with-values (lambda () (split-at l i)) list)\n      (list 'refused 'i i 'len (length l))))\n(display (safe-split (list 1 2 3) 2))\n(newline)\n(display (safe-split (list 1 2 3) 8))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator has a record whose first two elements are a header and needs the header and the body separately. Candidates: split-at (one traversal, both halves, and the index appears once), take plus drop at the same index (correct, but the index is written twice and can drift), or a manual named-let that accumulates the prefix (correct and four lines longer). For one cut with both sides kept, split-at is the honest verb."
     :explanation "eng: header and body named in one form, with the cut index written once. math: the splitting law holds at every index in range — the prefix has length i, the rest has length n-i, and appending them gives back the original. cs: the out-of-range case raises, so any code that splits at a caller-supplied index needs a range check first."
     :meta "Why split-at and not take plus drop: writing the index twice is the defect. When one of the two is updated and the other is not, the halves silently overlap or lose an element.")
    (:eng "A single source of truth for the cut index is the whole point; two calls means two places to be wrong."
     :math "split-at induces an ordered partition of the index set into [0,i) and [i,n)."
     :cs "Same slot as Python (lst[:i], lst[i:]), Scala splitAt, Haskell splitAt — all of which likewise return a pair rather than one side.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "One cut, two pieces: divide a sequence at a named position and keep both sides rather than discarding one."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(call-with-values (lambda () (split-at (list 1 2 3 4) 2)) list)")
             (:domain "cutting a deck of cards at a chosen card (both halves stay on the table)" :code "(define deck (list 'ace 'two 'three 'four 'five 'six))\n(let-values (((top bottom) (split-at deck 3)))\n  (display (list 'top top 'bottom bottom))\n  (newline))")
             (:domain "an ordered partition of an index set into [0,i) and [i,n)" :code "(define (partition-indices n i) (list (iota i) (map (lambda (j) (+ i j)) (iota (- n i)))))\n(display (partition-indices 6 3))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, split-at cuts once and hands back both parts. Cutting a deck leaves both halves in play; a cut that discarded one half would not be a cut, it would be a discard. In math, an index set is partitioned into a low block and a high block by a single boundary. All three share: one boundary, two complete pieces, nothing lost."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a boundary divides a whole into exactly two parts that must both survive.")
    (:eng "Returning both halves means the caller cannot forget that the other half exists."
     :math "An ordered partition into two blocks is determined entirely by the single boundary index."
     :cs "Returning multiple values instead of a container avoids allocating a wrapper the caller immediately destructures.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(let-values (((a b) (split-at (list 'p 'q 'r 's 't) 2)))\n  (display (list 'prefix a 'rest b 'lens (list (length a) (length b))))\n  (newline))"
     :math-program "(define l (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))\n(define (agrees-with-take-drop? i)\n  (let-values (((a b) (split-at l i)))\n    (and (equal? a (take l i)) (equal? b (drop l i)) (equal? (append a b) l))))\n(display (map agrees-with-take-drop? (iota 7)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'i-zero (call-with-values (lambda () (split-at (list 1 2 3) 0)) list)\n               'i-all (call-with-values (lambda () (split-at (list 1 2 3) 3)) list)\n               'i-negative (call-with-values (lambda () (split-at (list 1 2 3) -1)) list)\n               'over-raises (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (split-at (list 1 2) 5))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "For 0 <= i <= |lst|, split-at returns two values whose lengths are i and |lst| - i, which equal (take lst i) and (drop lst i) respectively, and which append back to lst. i = 0 and i = |lst| give the two degenerate splits, negative i is clamped to 0, and i > |lst| raises."
     :invariant "forall list l, forall i in [0,|l|]. split-at(l,i) = (take(l,i), drop(l,i)) and take(l,i) ++ drop(l,i) = l"
     :meta "The pattern is a single-boundary ordered split. What would falsify it: halves that do not append back to the original, or a disagreement with take and drop at the same index. See book-of-cs-curriculum on multiple return values.")
    (:eng "The append-back check is the one test that catches every split bug at once."
     :math "The two degenerate splits (i = 0 and i = n) are the identity elements of the splitting operation."
     :cs "Testing both degenerate cases plus one interior case is the standard three-point boundary suite for an index-parameterized verb.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (chunk xs n)\n  (if (< (length xs) n)\n      (list xs)\n      (let-values (((head rest) (split-at xs n)))\n        (cons head (chunk rest n)))))\n(display (chunk (list 1 2 3 4 5 6 7) 3))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (merge-sort xs)\n  (if (< (length xs) 2)\n      xs\n      (let-values (((a b) (split-at xs (quotient (length xs) 2))))\n        (let merge ((l (merge-sort a)) (r (merge-sort b)) (out (list)))\n          (cond ((null? l) (append-reverse out r))\n                ((null? r) (append-reverse out l))\n                ((< (car l) (car r)) (merge (cdr l) r (cons (car l) out)))\n                (else (merge l (cdr r) (cons (car r) out))))))))\n(display (merge-sort (list 5 3 9 1 7 2 8)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (deal players cards)\n  (if (null? players)\n      (list)\n      (let-values (((hand rest) (split-at cards (quotient (length cards) (length players)))))\n        (cons (list (car players) hand) (deal (cdr players) rest)))))\n(display (deal (list 'ana 'ben 'cy) (iota 9)))\n(newline)"
     :base "split-at cuts a list once and returns both halves as two values."
     :emergent "eng: fixed-size chunking — split, keep the head, recurse on the rest, and a flat list becomes a list of batches. math: merge sort — splitting at the midpoint and recursing is the divide half of divide-and-conquer, and the whole O(n log n) bound comes from that halving. cs: dealing — repeatedly splitting off an equal share turns one deck into per-player hands."
     :meta "The composition applies split-at recursively to the rest half. What emerges is the divide-and-conquer family: chunking, merge sort, and fair dealing are all repeated single-boundary splits. See book-of-cs-curriculum on divide-and-conquer.")
    (:eng "Recursing on the rest half rather than looping with an index keeps the batch code free of arithmetic."
     :math "Splitting at the midpoint gives the recurrence T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n), whose solution is the n log n bound."
     :cs "Divide-and-conquer is the archetype: the split is cheap, the recursion is structural, and the combine step carries the real work.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "take-while" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(take-while pred lst) -> list"
  :summary "The longest leading run of elements that all satisfy pred."
  :explanation "take-while walks from the front and stops at the FIRST element that fails the predicate. It never looks past that element, which makes it the right verb for reading a header block, a run of digits, or any prefix defined by a property rather than by a count. Its partner drop-while returns exactly the part take-while stopped at, and span returns both in one call."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(take-while odd? (list 1 3 4 5))" :note "Stops at 4, so the 5 behind it is not collected: (1 3).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define (header lines) (take-while (lambda (l) (not (eq? l 'blank))) lines))\n(header (list 'title 'author 'blank 'body1 'body2))" :note "Read the header block up to the first blank marker: (title author).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (leading-digits xs) (take-while number? xs))\n(list (leading-digits (list 1 2 (quote x) 3)) (leading-digits (list (quote x) 1)))" :note "A longest-match lexer rule in one call: ((1 2) ()).")
  )
  :caveats ("take-while stops at the FIRST failure. Elements that satisfy pred after that point are NOT collected; use filter if you want all of them." "If the first element fails, the result is the empty list." "The predicate is called at most once per element, and not at all past the stopping point. That short-circuit is observable if pred has effects." "A non-list argument returns the empty list rather than raising.")
  :usecases ("Reading a header block terminated by a marker." "Longest-match lexing of a leading run." "Trimming a leading run of padding or whitespace tokens.")
  :related ("drop-while" "span" "break" "filter" "take")
  :learn (:concept "Prefix by property: consume a leading run while a condition holds and stop the moment it fails." :prerequisites ("lambda" "filter" "take") :progression "Learn filter (keeps every match anywhere) against take-while (stops at the first miss), then span to get both halves at once.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define lines (list 'title 'author 'blank 'body1 'body2))\n(display (take-while (lambda (l) (not (eq? l 'blank))) lines))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define l (list 1 3 5 4 7 9))\n(display (equal? (append (take-while odd? l) (drop-while odd? l)) l))\n(newline)\n(display (list 'prefix-all-satisfy (every odd? (take-while odd? l))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define calls 0)\n(define (counted p) (lambda (x) (set! calls (+ calls 1)) (p x)))\n(define r (take-while (counted odd?) (list 1 3 4 5 7 9 11)))\n(display (list 'result r 'predicate-calls calls 'list-length 7))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator is reading a record whose header runs until the first blank line, and the body may itself contain non-blank lines. Candidates: take-while (stops at the first blank, which is exactly the format rule), filter with a not-blank predicate (WRONG — it would pull body lines into the header), or a manual loop with a flag (correct and noisier). For a prefix defined by a property, take-while is the honest verb."
     :explanation "eng: the header rule is stated once as a predicate and the stopping behavior comes free. math: take-while and drop-while are complementary at the same predicate, so appending them reconstructs the list, and every element of the prefix satisfies the predicate by construction. cs: the predicate is called only up to and including the first failure, so short-circuit behavior is observable and can be measured."
     :meta "Why take-while and not filter: filter has no notion of position. It would happily reach past the header terminator and pull matching body lines forward, silently corrupting the record.")
    (:eng "When a format says \"until\", the verb is take-while. When it says \"all\", the verb is filter. Choosing the wrong one produces plausible-looking wrong data."
     :math "take-while(p, l) is the longest prefix of l on which p is universally true."
     :cs "Same slot as itertools.takewhile in Python, takeWhile in Haskell and Scala, take-while in Rust iterators.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Prefix by property: consume a leading run while a condition holds, and stop at the first element that breaks it."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(take-while odd? (list 1 3 4 5))")
             (:domain "boarding a bus while seats remain (the first person who finds it full ends boarding, even if a seat opens later)" :code "(define queue (list 'seat 'seat 'full 'seat))\n(display (take-while (lambda (s) (eq? s 'seat)) queue))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "the maximal prefix on which a predicate is universally true" :code "(define (maximal-prefix p l)\n  (let loop ((rest l) (acc (list)))\n    (cond ((null? rest) (reverse acc))\n          ((p (car rest)) (loop (cdr rest) (cons (car rest) acc)))\n          (else (reverse acc)))))\n(display (maximal-prefix odd? (list 1 3 4 5)))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, take-while collects until the first failure. Boarding stops at the first person who cannot board, even though a seat may free up behind them, because the process is sequential and the rule is applied in order. In math, the maximal prefix satisfying a predicate is defined by the position of the first counterexample. All three share: order matters, and the first failure is final."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a sequential process must halt on first violation rather than skip violations and continue.")
    (:eng "Halting on first violation is a policy, not an accident; document it or a maintainer will replace it with filter."
     :math "The stopping index is the least index at which the predicate fails, or n if it never does."
     :cs "Short-circuit evaluation is the same idea one level down: stop as soon as the answer is determined.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define l (list 2 4 6 7 8 10))\n(define r (take-while even? l))\n(display (list 'result r 'len (length r) 'all-even (every even? r) 'next (list-ref l (length r))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (decomposition? p l)\n  (and (equal? (append (take-while p l) (drop-while p l)) l)\n       (every p (take-while p l))))\n(display (list (decomposition? odd? (list 1 3 4 5))\n               (decomposition? odd? (list 2 4))\n               (decomposition? odd? (list 1 3 5))\n               (decomposition? odd? (list))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'first-fails (take-while odd? (list 2 1 3))\n               'all-pass (take-while odd? (list 1 3 5))\n               'empty (take-while odd? (list))\n               'is-prefix (equal? (take-while odd? (list 1 3 4 5)) (take (list 1 3 4 5) 2))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "take-while returns a PREFIX of the list, every element of which satisfies pred, and it is the longest such prefix: appending it to (drop-while pred lst) reconstructs the list exactly. If the first element fails, the result is empty; if none fail, the result is the whole list."
     :invariant "forall pred p, forall list l. take-while(p,l) ++ drop-while(p,l) = l and every element of take-while(p,l) satisfies p and (drop-while(p,l) is empty or its first element fails p)"
     :meta "The pattern is longest-satisfying-prefix. What would falsify it: a result that is not a prefix, an element in the result that fails pred, or a drop-while remainder whose head still satisfies pred. See book-of-cs-curriculum on lexing and longest match.")
    (:eng "The three-part invariant (prefix, all satisfy, remainder head fails) fully characterizes the verb; any two of them alone do not."
     :math "Maximality is the third clause: without it, the empty list would satisfy the first two for every input."
     :cs "This is exactly the longest-match rule that a lexer applies at each token boundary.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (strip-pad pad xs)\n  (let ((n (length (take-while (lambda (x) (eq? x pad)) xs))))\n    (drop xs n)))\n(display (strip-pad 'pad (list 'pad 'pad 'a 'b 'pad)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (run-lengths xs)\n  (if (null? xs)\n      (list)\n      (let* ((head (car xs))\n             (run (take-while (lambda (x) (equal? x head)) xs)))\n        (cons (list head (length run)) (run-lengths (drop xs (length run)))))))\n(display (run-lengths (list 'a 'a 'b 'c 'c 'c)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (lex xs)\n  (if (null? xs)\n      (list)\n      (let* ((kind (if (number? (car xs)) number? symbol?))\n             (tok (take-while kind xs)))\n        (cons tok (lex (drop xs (length tok)))))))\n(display (lex (list 1 2 'a 'b 3)))\n(newline)"
     :base "take-while collects the longest leading run satisfying a predicate."
     :emergent "eng: padding strip — measure the leading run of pad tokens and drop exactly that many. math: run-length encoding — take the run equal to the head, record its length, recurse on the rest, and an arbitrary sequence becomes a list of (value count) pairs. cs: a lexer — pick the token class from the first character, take the longest run of that class, emit it, and repeat."
     :meta "The composition applies take-while at the head, measures the run, and recurses on the remainder. What emerges is the tokenizer family: run-length encoding, lexing, and padding strips are the same loop with different predicates. See book-of-cs-curriculum on scanners.")
    (:eng "Measuring the run and then dropping it keeps the loop obviously terminating: the remainder is strictly shorter whenever the run is non-empty."
     :math "Run-length encoding is a bijection on sequences with no adjacent equal runs; take-while finds the run boundaries."
     :cs "Maximal-munch lexing is the classic application; the predicate selects the character class and take-while does the munching.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "drop-while" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(drop-while pred lst) -> list"
  :summary "Discard the longest leading run satisfying pred and return what is left."
  :explanation "drop-while is the complement of take-while at the same predicate: it skips past the prefix and hands back the rest, starting with the first element that FAILS the predicate. It is the verb for skipping a prologue — leading whitespace, a comment banner, a run of zeros — when the length of that prologue is not known in advance."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(drop-while odd? (list 1 3 4 5))" :note "Skips the leading odds and returns from the first even onward: (4 5).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define (skip-banner lines) (drop-while (lambda (l) (eq? l 'comment)) lines))\n(skip-banner (list 'comment 'comment 'code1 'comment 'code2))" :note "Only the LEADING comments are skipped; the later one is kept: (code1 comment code2).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (strip-leading-zeros digits) (drop-while zero? digits))\n(list (strip-leading-zeros (list 0 0 4 0 2)) (strip-leading-zeros (list 0 0 0)))" :note "Normalizing a digit string: ((4 0 2) ()).")
  )
  :caveats ("Only the LEADING run is dropped. Later elements that satisfy pred are kept; use remove if you want all of them gone." "If every element satisfies pred the result is the empty list; if the first fails, the whole list comes back unchanged." "The result always starts with an element that FAILS pred (when it is non-empty). That is the defining property." "A non-list argument returns the empty list rather than raising.")
  :usecases ("Skipping a comment or banner prologue of unknown length." "Normalizing leading zeros or leading padding." "Advancing a cursor to the first interesting element.")
  :related ("take-while" "span" "break" "remove" "drop")
  :learn (:concept "Skip by property: discard a leading run while a condition holds, and resume at the first element that breaks it." :prerequisites ("lambda" "take-while" "drop") :progression "Learn take-while first, then drop-while as its complement, then span to get both in one traversal.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define lines (list 'comment 'comment 'code1 'comment 'code2))\n(display (drop-while (lambda (l) (eq? l 'comment)) lines))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define l (list 1 3 5 4 7))\n(define rest (drop-while odd? l))\n(display (list 'reconstructs (equal? (append (take-while odd? l) rest) l)\n               'head-fails (not (odd? (car rest)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define calls 0)\n(define (counted p) (lambda (x) (set! calls (+ calls 1)) (p x)))\n(define r (drop-while (counted odd?) (list 1 3 4 5 7 9)))\n(display (list 'result r 'predicate-calls calls 'list-length 6))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator has a source file whose first lines are a comment banner, and comments also appear later in the body. Candidates: drop-while (skips only the leading run, which is what a banner is), remove (WRONG — it would strip the in-body comments too, changing the program), or a loop with an index (correct and hand-written). For skipping a prologue, drop-while is the honest verb."
     :explanation "eng: only the banner disappears, and the body keeps its own comments. math: drop-while is the exact complement of take-while, so the two append back to the original and the returned head is guaranteed to fail the predicate. cs: the predicate is called exactly once per skipped element plus once for the stopping element, and never again."
     :meta "Why drop-while and not remove: remove is position-blind. A banner-stripper built on remove passes its tests on files whose bodies happen to have no comments, and corrupts the first file that does.")
    (:eng "Position-sensitive and position-blind verbs look interchangeable on small examples. They are not."
     :math "drop-while(p,l) is the suffix of l starting at the least index where p fails, or the empty list if p never fails."
     :cs "Same slot as itertools.dropwhile in Python, dropWhile in Haskell and Scala, skip_while in Rust.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Skip by property: advance past a leading run that satisfies a condition and resume at the first element that does not."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(drop-while odd? (list 1 3 4 5))")
             (:domain "walking past the ads at the front of a magazine (you stop at the first article, and later ads stay in the magazine)" :code "(define mag (list 'ad 'ad 'article 'ad 'article))\n(display (drop-while (lambda (p) (eq? p 'ad)) mag))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "the suffix beginning at the least index where a predicate fails" :code "(define (first-failure-index p l)\n  (let loop ((i 0) (rest l))\n    (cond ((null? rest) i)\n          ((p (car rest)) (loop (+ i 1) (cdr rest)))\n          (else i))))\n(define l (list 1 3 4 5))\n(display (equal? (drop l (first-failure-index odd? l)) (drop-while odd? l)))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, drop-while advances past the leading run. Flipping past the ads at the front of a magazine stops at the first article; the ads between later articles are still there because you only skipped the front. In math, the answer is the suffix beginning at the least failing index. All three share: skip from the front only, and stop permanently at the first exception."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a stream has a prologue whose length is unknown but whose membership rule is known.")
    (:eng "Front-only skipping is a different contract from global removal; the difference only shows up on real data."
     :math "The least failing index is the single number that determines the answer."
     :cs "This is the cursor-advance step of a parser: move to the first significant token, then hand control to the grammar.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define l (list 0 0 4 0 2))\n(define r (drop-while zero? l))\n(display (list 'result r 'len (length r) 'head-nonzero (not (zero? (car r)))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (complement? p l)\n  (and (equal? (append (take-while p l) (drop-while p l)) l)\n       (= (+ (length (take-while p l)) (length (drop-while p l))) (length l))))\n(display (list (complement? odd? (list 1 3 4 5))\n               (complement? odd? (list 2 4))\n               (complement? odd? (list 1 3 5))\n               (complement? odd? (list))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'all-satisfy (drop-while odd? (list 1 3 5))\n               'none-satisfy (drop-while odd? (list 2 4))\n               'empty (drop-while odd? (list))\n               'is-suffix (equal? (drop-while odd? (list 1 3 4 5)) (drop (list 1 3 4 5) 2))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "drop-while returns a SUFFIX of the list whose first element (if any) fails pred, and it is the complement of take-while at the same predicate: the two lengths sum to the original length and the two lists append back to the original. All-satisfy gives the empty list; none-satisfy gives the whole list."
     :invariant "forall pred p, forall list l. take-while(p,l) ++ drop-while(p,l) = l and (drop-while(p,l) is empty or p fails on its first element)"
     :meta "The pattern is prefix-skip. What would falsify it: a result that is not a suffix, a returned head that still satisfies pred, or lengths that do not sum. See book-of-cs-curriculum on scanners and cursors.")
    (:eng "The head-fails property is what downstream code relies on; check it, not just the length."
     :math "take-while and drop-while form an exact splitting of the list at the first failing index."
     :cs "The two degenerate cases (all satisfy, none satisfy) are where a hand-rolled loop most often gets the boundary wrong.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (parse-config lines)\n  (let ((body (drop-while (lambda (l) (eq? (car l) 'comment)) lines)))\n    (map cadr body)))\n(display (parse-config (list (list 'comment 'banner) (list 'comment 'legal) (list 'set 'width) (list 'set 'height))))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (trim p xs)\n  (let ((front (drop-while p xs)))\n    (reverse (drop-while p (reverse front)))))\n(display (trim zero? (list 0 0 5 7 0 0)))\n(newline)\n(display (trim zero? (list 0 0 0)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (tokens xs)\n  (let loop ((rest (drop-while (lambda (x) (eq? x 'space)) xs)) (out (list)))\n    (if (null? rest)\n        (reverse out)\n        (let* ((tok (take-while (lambda (x) (not (eq? x 'space))) rest))\n               (after (drop-while (lambda (x) (not (eq? x 'space))) rest)))\n          (loop (drop-while (lambda (x) (eq? x 'space)) after) (cons tok out))))))\n(display (tokens (list 'space 'a 'b 'space 'space 'c 'space)))\n(newline)"
     :base "drop-while skips the longest leading run satisfying a predicate."
     :emergent "eng: a config reader that skips its banner and then treats every remaining line uniformly. math: two-sided trim — drop-while from the front, reverse, drop-while again, reverse back, which is the standard strip built from one primitive. cs: a whitespace-skipping tokenizer, where drop-while advances the cursor and take-while lifts the token, alternating until the input is consumed."
     :meta "The composition alternates drop-while with take-while or with reverse. What emerges is the scanner family: trims, config prologues, and whitespace tokenizers are all built from these two verbs in a loop. See book-of-composition on cursor loops.")
    (:eng "Skipping the prologue once, up front, means the rest of the reader has one case instead of two."
     :math "Trimming both ends from one primitive uses reverse as the symmetry that turns a left operation into a right one."
     :cs "Skip-then-take is the entire structure of a hand-written scanner; everything else is which predicates you pass.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "last-pair" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(last-pair lst) -> list"
  :summary "The last pair of a list. In Motoi that is a fresh one-element list holding the last element."
  :explanation "In a classic pair-based Scheme, last-pair returns the final cons cell itself, which is shared with the original list and whose cdr is the terminator. Motoi does not build proper lists out of chained cons cells, so there is no cell to hand back; last-pair returns a one-element list containing the last element instead. Read it as the last SEGMENT rather than as an identity handle."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(last-pair (list 1 2 3))" :note "A one-element list holding the last element: (3). Note the parentheses — it is not the bare 3.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(list (last-pair (list 1 2 3)) (last (list 1 2 3)))" :note "last-pair gives the segment, last gives the element: ((3) 3).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (ends xs)\n  (if (null? xs) (list) (list (list (car xs)) (last-pair xs))))\n(list (ends (list 1 2 3)) (ends (list)))" :note "First segment and last segment as matching shapes: (((1) (3)) ()).")
  )
  :caveats ("DIVERGENCE from SRFI-1 (structural): SRFI-1 returns the last CONS CELL, shared with the argument, so (set-cdr! (last-pair l) tail) extends l in place. Motoi returns a FRESH one-element list, so no such mutation channel exists and eq? identity with the original is not observable." "DIVERGENCE from SRFI-1 (improper lists): given an improper list such as (cons 1 (cons 2 3)), SRFI-1 returns the last pair (2 . 3). Motoi returns the EMPTY LIST for any non-list argument, including improper ones. Do not use last-pair to inspect a dotted tail." "The empty list gives the empty list, not an error." "If you want the last ELEMENT, use last. If you want the last k elements, use take-right.")
  :usecases ("Getting the final segment of a list in the same shape as other segments." "Teaching the difference between a list, its last element, and its last pair." "Symmetric first-segment / last-segment reporting.")
  :related ("last" "take-right" "list-tail" "reverse")
  :learn (:concept "Segment versus element: the tail end of a list as a list, not as a value." :prerequisites ("list" "car" "cdr") :progression "Learn last for the element and take-right for k elements; reach for last-pair when you want the tail end in list shape.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define trail (list 'start 'middle 'finish))\n(display (list 'segment (last-pair trail) 'element (last trail)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define l (list 1 2 3 4))\n(display (list 'equals-take-right-1 (equal? (last-pair l) (take-right l 1))\n               'length-always-1 (= (length (last-pair l)) 1)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'proper (last-pair (list 1 2 3))\n               'singleton (last-pair (list 9))\n               'empty (last-pair (list))\n               'improper-diverges (last-pair (cons 1 (cons 2 3)))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator wants the tail end of a list in the same shape as the other segments they are handling, so that one downstream function accepts all of them. Candidates: last-pair (returns a list, matching the shape of the other segments), last (returns the bare element, which then needs wrapping), or (take-right l 1) (identical result and clearer about the count). For segment-shaped access to the end, last-pair is the SRFI-1 spelling and take-right 1 is the plainer one."
     :explanation "eng: segment and element are different shapes and the caller must pick deliberately. math: in Motoi last-pair is exactly take-right with k = 1, so it always returns a length-1 list for a non-empty argument. cs: the boundaries are where the SRFI divergences live — empty gives empty, and an improper list gives empty instead of the dotted tail SRFI-1 promises."
     :meta "Why last-pair and not last: they return different SHAPES. Mixing them is the classic source of a downstream car applied to a non-list.")
    (:eng "Pick the shape your consumer wants, not the one that reads shortest at the call site."
     :math "In Motoi, last-pair = take-right with k = 1 on every proper list."
     :cs "In a pair-based Scheme this verb is an identity handle used for in-place extension; in Motoi it is a value, so that idiom is simply unavailable.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "The tail end of a sequence, returned in the shape of a sequence rather than as a bare value."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(last-pair (list 1 2 3))")
             (:domain "the caboose of a train, still described as a train section rather than as a single car" :code "(define train (list 'engine 'freight 'caboose))\n(display (list 'end-section (last-pair train) 'end-car (last train)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "the length-1 suffix of a sequence" :code "(define (suffix1 l) (take-right l 1))\n(display (list (suffix1 (list 1 2 3)) (equal? (suffix1 (list 1 2 3)) (last-pair (list 1 2 3)))))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, last-pair returns the end as a list. Describing the caboose as a one-car section rather than as a car keeps it composable with descriptions of other sections. In math, the length-1 suffix is a suffix like any other, and suffixes compose with prefixes. All three share: keeping the container so the piece stays combinable."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever an extremum must stay in the same type as the collection so that one pipeline handles both.")
    (:eng "Type-preserving accessors compose; unwrapping accessors force a rewrap at every joint."
     :math "A one-element list is still a list; the empty case is what makes it a total operation rather than a partial one."
     :cs "This is the same reason database queries return a rowset of one rather than a scalar: the consumer stays uniform.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define l (list 'a 'b 'c))\n(define r (last-pair l))\n(display (list 'result r 'len (length r) 'car-is-last (equal? (car r) (last l))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (laws? l)\n  (if (null? l)\n      (equal? (last-pair l) (list))\n      (and (= (length (last-pair l)) 1)\n           (equal? (last-pair l) (take-right l 1))\n           (equal? (car (last-pair l)) (last l)))))\n(display (map laws? (list (list) (list 9) (list 1 2 3) (list 'a 'b))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'empty-gives-empty (last-pair (list))\n               'improper-gives-empty (last-pair (cons 1 (cons 2 3)))\n               'srfi-1-would-give-dotted-pair 'documented-divergence))\n(newline)"
     :claim "For a non-empty proper list, last-pair returns a one-element list whose car is the last element, identical to (take-right lst 1). For the empty list it returns the empty list. For an IMPROPER list Motoi returns the empty list, whereas SRFI-1 would return the final dotted pair — a documented divergence, not a falsification of the claim."
     :invariant "forall non-empty proper list l. |last-pair(l)| = 1 and car(last-pair(l)) = last(l) and last-pair(l) = take-right(l,1); last-pair(()) = ()"
     :meta "The pattern is type-preserving extremum access. What would falsify the claim: a length other than 1 on a non-empty list, or a car that is not the last element. The improper-list case is a KNOWN divergence recorded in the caveats and reported to the maintainers, not a bug found by this proof. See broken-examples on improper lists.")
    (:eng "A verb whose empty case returns the same type as its non-empty case never needs a null check at the joint."
     :math "take-right with k = 1 is the general form; last-pair is that special case under an older name."
     :cs "Divergences from a cited standard belong in the record, next to the claim they qualify. Silence about them is what makes ported code fail mysteriously.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (bookends xs)\n  (if (null? xs)\n      (list 'empty)\n      (list 'first (list (car xs)) 'last (last-pair xs))))\n(display (bookends (list 'a 'b 'c)))\n(newline)\n(display (bookends (list)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (segments xs)\n  (if (null? xs)\n      (list)\n      (list (list (car xs)) (drop-right (cdr xs) 1) (last-pair xs))))\n(display (segments (list 1 2 3 4 5)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (append-at-end xs y) (append (drop-right xs 1) (last-pair xs) (list y)))\n(display (append-at-end (list 1 2 3) 99))\n(newline)\n(display (equal? (append-at-end (list 1 2 3) 99) (append (list 1 2 3) (list 99))))\n(newline)"
     :base "last-pair returns the final segment of a list, in list shape."
     :emergent "eng: bookend reporting — first segment and last segment in matching shapes, so the empty case falls out without a special branch at the consumer. math: three-way segmentation into head, middle and tail, where every piece is a list and the pieces append back to the original. cs: end-extension written functionally — in a pair-based Scheme this would be set-cdr! on the last pair, and here it is an append, which is the honest way to say the same thing without mutation."
     :meta "The composition uses last-pair together with drop-right to carve a list into named segments. What emerges is the segmentation family: bookends, head-middle-tail splits, and the functional replacement for last-pair mutation. See book-of-composition on segment algebra.")
    (:eng "Matching shapes at both ends means the reporting code has one branch, not three."
     :math "Head, middle and tail are a partition: they are disjoint, ordered, and their concatenation is the whole."
     :cs "The classic set-cdr! on last-pair trick is unavailable in Motoi by design; the append form is what replaces it, and it is O(n) rather than O(1).")
)
)

(verb
  :name "span" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(span pred lst) -> two values: (prefix rest)"
  :summary "Split the list at the first element that FAILS pred, returning both halves as two values."
  :explanation "span is take-while and drop-while fused into one traversal. The first value is the longest prefix all of whose elements satisfy pred; the second is everything from the first failure onward. Because it returns TWO VALUES, consume it with call-with-values or let-values. Its mirror is break, which splits at the first element that SATISFIES pred."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(call-with-values (lambda () (span odd? (list 1 3 4 5))) list)" :note "The odds up front, then everything from the 4 on: ((1 3) (4 5)).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let-values (((header body) (span (lambda (l) (not (eq? l 'blank))) (list 'title 'author 'blank 'b1 'b2))))\n  (list 'header header 'body body))" :note "One traversal gives both parts of a header/body record: (header (title author) body (blank b1 b2)).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(let-values (((a b) (span odd? (list 1 3 5))))\n  (list a b))" :note "When nothing fails, the whole list is the prefix and the rest is empty: ((1 3 5) ()).")
  )
  :caveats ("span returns TWO VALUES, not a two-element list. Use call-with-values or let-values." "SRFI-8 receive, which the SRFI-1 document uses in its examples, is NOT bound in Motoi. Rewrite those examples with let-values." "The split point is the first FAILURE. Elements satisfying pred that occur after it stay in the second value." "The second value, when non-empty, always begins with an element that fails pred. That is the defining property." "A non-list argument raises a teaching error rather than returning empty, unlike take-while and drop-while.")
  :usecases ("Reading a header block and its body in one pass." "Grouping a sequence into runs of equal elements." "Any place take-while and drop-while are both wanted at the same predicate.")
  :related ("break" "take-while" "drop-while" "split-at" "partition")
  :learn (:concept "One predicate, one cut, two pieces: split where a condition first stops holding." :prerequisites ("take-while" "drop-while" "call-with-values") :progression "Learn take-while and drop-while separately, then span to fuse them, then break for the mirrored condition.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(let-values (((header body) (span (lambda (l) (not (eq? l 'blank)))\n                                  (list 'title 'author 'blank 'b1 'b2))))\n  (display (list 'header header 'body body))\n  (newline))"
     :math-program "(define l (list 1 3 4 5))\n(let-values (((a b) (span odd? l)))\n  (display (list 'matches-take-while (equal? a (take-while odd? l))\n                 'matches-drop-while (equal? b (drop-while odd? l))\n                 'reconstructs (equal? (append a b) l)))\n  (newline))"
     :cs-program "(define calls 0)\n(define (counted p) (lambda (x) (set! calls (+ calls 1)) (p x)))\n(let-values (((a b) (span (counted odd?) (list 1 3 4 5 7 9))))\n  (display (list 'prefix a 'rest b 'predicate-calls calls 'list-length 6))\n  (newline))"
     :problem "The operator needs both the header block and the body of a record, where the header runs until the first blank. Candidates: span (one traversal, both halves, predicate written once), take-while plus drop-while at the same predicate (correct, but the predicate is written twice and traverses twice), or a hand-written loop with an accumulator (correct and longer). For one predicate yielding both sides, span is the honest verb."
     :explanation "eng: header and body named together, from a single statement of the rule. math: span agrees exactly with the take-while / drop-while pair and the two halves append back to the original. cs: the predicate is called once per prefix element plus once for the failing element and never again, so span costs exactly one partial traversal."
     :meta "Why span and not take-while plus drop-while: writing the predicate twice invites drift. When one copy is edited and the other is not, the two halves overlap or lose an element silently.")
    (:eng "One statement of a rule is one place to change it. Two copies are two places to be wrong."
     :math "span(p,l) = (take-while(p,l), drop-while(p,l)), and the two components are complementary."
     :cs "Same slot as Haskell span and Scala span; both likewise return a pair rather than one side.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "One predicate, one cut, two pieces: divide a sequence where a condition first stops holding, and keep both sides."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(call-with-values (lambda () (span odd? (list 1 3 4 5))) list)")
             (:domain "a shift ending (the workers who were on before the whistle, and everyone from the whistle onward)" :code "(define log (list 'on 'on 'whistle 'off 'on))\n(let-values (((before after) (span (lambda (e) (eq? e 'on)) log)))\n  (display (list 'before before 'after after))\n  (newline))")
             (:domain "the splitting of a sequence at the least index where a predicate fails" :code "(define (split-at-first-failure p l)\n  (let loop ((i 0) (rest l))\n    (cond ((null? rest) (list (take l i) (list)))\n          ((p (car rest)) (loop (+ i 1) (cdr rest)))\n          (else (list (take l i) (drop l i))))))\n(display (split-at-first-failure odd? (list 1 3 4 5)))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, span cuts at the first failure. A shift log cuts at the whistle: everyone before it was on shift, and everything after it is the next phase, including workers who come back on. In math, the cut is determined by the least failing index, and both blocks are kept. All three share: one condition, one boundary, two surviving parts."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a condition holds for a while and the moment it stops is itself the interesting boundary.")
    (:eng "The boundary event is usually more informative than either side alone; keeping both sides preserves it."
     :math "The least failing index determines the whole answer, so span carries no more information than that one number."
     :cs "Returning multiple values avoids allocating a pair the caller immediately takes apart.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(let-values (((a b) (span even? (list 2 4 6 7 8))))\n  (display (list 'prefix a 'rest b 'lens (list (length a) (length b)) 'all-even (every even? a)))\n  (newline))"
     :math-program "(define (laws? p l)\n  (let-values (((a b) (span p l)))\n    (and (equal? (append a b) l)\n         (= (+ (length a) (length b)) (length l))\n         (every p a)\n         (or (null? b) (not (p (car b)))))))\n(display (map (lambda (l) (laws? odd? l))\n              (list (list 1 3 4 5) (list 2 4) (list 1 3 5) (list))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (as-list p l) (call-with-values (lambda () (span p l)) list))\n(display (list 'none-pass (as-list odd? (list 2 4))\n               'all-pass (as-list odd? (list 1 3))\n               'empty (as-list odd? (list))\n               'non-list-raises (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (span odd? 5))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "span returns two values that append back to the original list; every element of the first satisfies pred; and the second, when non-empty, begins with an element that fails pred. Those three together say the cut is at the FIRST failure and nowhere else."
     :invariant "forall pred p, forall list l. span(p,l) = (a,b) with a ++ b = l and every element of a satisfies p and (b = () or p fails on car(b))"
     :meta "The pattern is first-failure splitting. What would falsify it: halves that do not append back, an element in the prefix that fails pred, or a remainder whose head still satisfies pred. See book-of-cs-curriculum on scanners.")
    (:eng "The third clause is the one that pins down maximality; without it an empty prefix would pass the other two on every input."
     :math "The three clauses together are a complete characterization, so they can serve as a specification for a reimplementation."
     :cs "span raising on a non-list while take-while returns empty is an inconsistency inside the same part; guard your inputs rather than relying on either behavior.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (parse-sections lines)\n  (if (null? lines)\n      (list)\n      (let-values (((sec rest) (span (lambda (l) (not (eq? l 'rule))) lines)))\n        (cons sec (parse-sections (if (null? rest) rest (cdr rest)))))))\n(display (parse-sections (list 'a 'b 'rule 'c 'rule 'd)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (notp p) (lambda (x) (not (p x))))\n(define l (list 1 3 4 5))\n(let-values (((a b) (span odd? l)))\n  (let-values (((c d) (break (notp odd?) l)))\n    (display (list 'span-equals-break-of-complement (and (equal? a c) (equal? b d))))\n    (newline)))"
     :cs-composition "(define (group-runs xs)\n  (if (null? xs)\n      (list)\n      (let-values (((run rest) (span (lambda (x) (equal? x (car xs))) xs)))\n        (cons run (group-runs rest)))))\n(display (group-runs (list 'a 'a 'b 'c 'c 'c 'a)))\n(newline)"
     :base "span cuts a list at the first element failing a predicate and returns both sides."
     :emergent "eng: a section parser — span up to the rule, emit the section, recurse past the rule, and a flat line list becomes a list of sections. math: the De Morgan duality made concrete — span with p and break with the complement of p are the same function, so one of the two verbs is definitionally redundant. cs: run grouping — span against the head element collects a maximal run, and recursing gives the classic group-consecutive operation that run-length encoding is built on."
     :meta "The composition recurses on the second value of span. What emerges is the segmentation family: section parsers, run groupers, and record splitters are the same loop with different predicates. See book-of-composition on cursor loops.")
    (:eng "Recursing on the remainder guarantees termination as long as each pass consumes at least one element; dropping the delimiter is what guarantees that."
     :math "span p = break (not p) is an identity, which means the pair of verbs exists for readability, not for power."
     :cs "Group-consecutive is the standard preprocessing step for run-length encoding and for chunked diffing.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "break" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(break pred lst) -> two values: (prefix rest)"
  :summary "Split the list at the first element that SATISFIES pred, returning both halves as two values."
  :explanation "break is the mirror of span. Where span runs while the predicate holds, break runs until the predicate holds: the first value is everything before the first match, and the second value starts AT the match. It is the verb for splitting on a delimiter, a sentinel, or the first error, because the thing you are looking for stays in the second half where you can inspect it."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(call-with-values (lambda () (break even? (list 1 3 4 5))) list)" :note "Everything before the first even, then from the even on: ((1 3) (4 5)).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let-values (((before at) (break (lambda (x) (eq? x 'sep)) (list 'a 'b 'sep 'c 'd))))\n  (list 'before before 'at at))" :note "The delimiter is preserved at the head of the second half: (before (a b) at (sep c d)).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(let-values (((ok rest) (break (lambda (r) (eq? (car r) 'error)) (list (list 'ok 1) (list 'ok 2) (list 'error 'boom) (list 'ok 3)))))\n  (list 'processed (length ok) 'stopped-at (car rest)))" :note "Process until the first failure and keep the failure for reporting: (processed 2 stopped-at (error boom)).")
  )
  :caveats ("break returns TWO VALUES, not a two-element list. Use call-with-values or let-values." "SRFI-8 receive is NOT bound in Motoi. Rewrite SRFI-1 document examples that use it with let-values." "The matching element goes into the SECOND value, not the first, and it is not consumed. If you want it dropped, take (cdr rest) yourself." "break is not a loop-control form. It has nothing to do with break in C-like languages; the name comes from breaking a list in two." "A non-list argument raises a teaching error rather than returning empty.")
  :usecases ("Splitting a sequence on the first delimiter or sentinel." "Stopping a pipeline at the first error record while keeping the error." "Locating a marker and getting both sides in one traversal.")
  :related ("span" "take-while" "drop-while" "find" "split-at")
  :learn (:concept "Split on first match: everything before a marker, and everything from the marker onward." :prerequisites ("span" "lambda" "call-with-values") :progression "Learn span first, then break as its mirror; remember which one keeps the boundary element by asking which condition stopped the scan.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(let-values (((before at) (break (lambda (x) (eq? x 'sep)) (list 'a 'b 'sep 'c 'd))))\n  (display (list 'before before 'at at))\n  (newline))"
     :math-program "(define (notp p) (lambda (x) (not (p x))))\n(define l (list 1 3 4 5))\n(let-values (((a b) (break even? l)))\n  (let-values (((c d) (span (notp even?) l)))\n    (display (list 'break-equals-span-of-complement (and (equal? a c) (equal? b d))))\n    (newline)))"
     :cs-program "(let-values (((ok rest) (break (lambda (r) (eq? (car r) 'error))\n                              (list (list 'ok 1) (list 'ok 2) (list 'error 'boom) (list 'ok 3)))))\n  (display (list 'processed (length ok) 'stopped-at (car rest) 'unprocessed (length rest)))\n  (newline))"
     :problem "The operator is processing a stream of result records and must stop at the first error, report which record failed, and know what was left unprocessed. Candidates: break (splits at the match and keeps the match in the second half where it can be reported), find (locates the failing record but tells you nothing about position or the remainder), or take-while plus drop-while with a negated predicate (correct but says the condition twice, inverted, which is easy to get backwards). For stop-at-first-match with the match preserved, break is the honest verb."
     :explanation "eng: the delimiter stays visible at the head of the second half, so the caller can decide whether to drop it. math: break with p and span with the complement of p are the same function; the pair exists so the code can be written the way the requirement is phrased. cs: the split gives all three numbers a failure report needs — how many succeeded, which one failed, and how many were never attempted."
     :meta "Why break and not find: find returns the element and loses the position. A retry or a resume needs the remainder, and only a splitting verb provides it.")
    (:eng "Report what succeeded, what failed, and what was skipped. A verb that returns only the failure forces two more traversals to get the rest."
     :math "break(p,l) = span(not p, l); the two are interdefinable, which is why SRFI-1 documents them together."
     :cs "Same slot as Haskell break, Scala span with a negated predicate, and the split-on-first-match idiom in string libraries.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Split on first match: cut a sequence where a sought condition first appears, keeping the matching element with the second part."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(call-with-values (lambda () (break even? (list 1 3 4 5))) list)")
             (:domain "reading a road until the first junction (the junction itself belongs to the next stretch, because that is where you decide)" :code "(define road (list 'straight 'straight 'junction 'straight))\n(let-values (((leg rest) (break (lambda (s) (eq? s 'junction)) road)))\n  (display (list 'leg leg 'from-junction rest))\n  (newline))")
             (:domain "the splitting of a sequence at the least index where a predicate holds" :code "(define (split-at-first-match p l)\n  (let loop ((i 0) (rest l))\n    (cond ((null? rest) (list l (list)))\n          ((p (car rest)) (list (take l i) (drop l i)))\n          (else (loop (+ i 1) (cdr rest))))))\n(display (split-at-first-match even? (list 1 3 4 5)))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, break cuts at the first match. On a road, the junction belongs to the next stretch because it is where the next decision is made, not where the last one ended. In math, the cut is at the least index satisfying the predicate, and that index belongs to the upper block. All three share: a sought event, a boundary at its first occurrence, and the event kept on the far side."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever the thing you are scanning FOR must survive the scan so it can be examined.")
    (:eng "Consuming the delimiter is a separate decision from finding it; keeping it lets the caller choose."
     :math "Least-index-satisfying and least-index-failing are the same construction under complementation."
     :cs "Delimiter-inclusive splitting is what lets a parser see the token that ended a production.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(let-values (((a b) (break even? (list 1 3 5 6 7))))\n  (display (list 'before a 'from b 'lens (list (length a) (length b)) 'none-even-in-prefix (not (any even? a))))\n  (newline))"
     :math-program "(define (laws? p l)\n  (let-values (((a b) (break p l)))\n    (and (equal? (append a b) l)\n         (= (+ (length a) (length b)) (length l))\n         (not (any p a))\n         (or (null? b) (p (car b))))))\n(display (map (lambda (l) (laws? even? l))\n              (list (list 1 3 4 5) (list 2 4) (list 1 3 5) (list))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (as-list p l) (call-with-values (lambda () (break p l)) list))\n(display (list 'first-matches (as-list even? (list 2 3))\n               'none-match (as-list even? (list 1 3))\n               'empty (as-list even? (list))\n               'non-list-raises (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (break even? 5))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "break returns two values that append back to the original list; NO element of the first satisfies pred; and the second, when non-empty, begins with an element that DOES satisfy pred. Together those say the cut is at the first match and the match is on the far side."
     :invariant "forall pred p, forall list l. break(p,l) = (a,b) with a ++ b = l and no element of a satisfies p and (b = () or p holds on car(b))"
     :meta "The pattern is first-match splitting. What would falsify it: halves that do not append back, a matching element hiding in the prefix, or a remainder whose head does not match. See book-of-cs-curriculum on delimiters.")
    (:eng "Checking that no element of the prefix matches is the clause that catches an off-by-one at the cut."
     :math "The claim for break is the claim for span with the predicate negated; proving one proves the other."
     :cs "When nothing matches, the whole list is the prefix and the remainder is empty; that is the case a hand-written loop most often gets wrong.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (split-all sep xs)\n  (if (null? xs)\n      (list (list))\n      (let-values (((seg rest) (break (lambda (x) (eq? x sep)) xs)))\n        (if (null? rest)\n            (list seg)\n            (cons seg (split-all sep (cdr rest)))))))\n(display (split-all 'sep (list 'a 'b 'sep 'c 'sep 'd 'e)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (notp p) (lambda (x) (not (p x))))\n(define (span-via-break p l) (call-with-values (lambda () (break (notp p) l)) list))\n(define l (list 1 3 4 5))\n(display (list (span-via-break odd? l)\n               (call-with-values (lambda () (span odd? l)) list)\n               (equal? (span-via-break odd? l) (call-with-values (lambda () (span odd? l)) list))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (run-until-error records)\n  (let-values (((ok rest) (break (lambda (r) (eq? (car r) 'error)) records)))\n    (if (null? rest)\n        (list 'complete (length ok))\n        (list 'halted 'after (length ok) 'on (car rest) 'skipped (length (cdr rest))))))\n(display (run-until-error (list (list 'ok 1) (list 'ok 2) (list 'error 'boom) (list 'ok 3))))\n(newline)\n(display (run-until-error (list (list 'ok 1) (list 'ok 2))))\n(newline)"
     :base "break cuts a list at the first element satisfying a predicate, keeping that element in the second half."
     :emergent "eng: full delimiter splitting — break, emit the segment, drop the delimiter, recurse, and one list becomes a list of fields. math: span reconstructed out of break by negating the predicate, showing the two verbs are one operation seen from two sides. cs: fail-fast batch processing that reports how many succeeded, which record halted the run, and how many were skipped."
     :meta "The composition recurses past the delimiter on the second value. What emerges is the field-splitting family: CSV-style splits, fail-fast batches, and protocol framing. See book-of-composition on delimiter loops.")
    (:eng "Dropping the delimiter before recursing is what makes each pass strictly shorter, so the loop terminates."
     :math "Deriving span from break demonstrates that only one of the two needs to be primitive."
     :cs "Fail-fast with a full report is the difference between an error message and a diagnosis.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "delete" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(delete x lst [=]) -> list"
  :summary "Remove every element of lst equal to x, keeping the rest in order."
  :explanation "delete removes by VALUE rather than by predicate: name the thing you do not want and it is gone, every occurrence of it. The default comparison is structural, so lists and nested structures compare by shape and contents. An optional third argument supplies a comparison procedure, and it is called as (= x element) with the sought value FIRST — which matters the moment the comparison is not symmetric."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(delete 2 (list 1 2 3 2 1))" :note "Every 2 goes, order is otherwise untouched: (1 3 1).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(delete (list 'a) (list (list 'a) (list 'b) (list 'a) (list 'a 'b)))" :note "The default comparison is structural, so nested lists compare by contents: ((b) (a b)).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(delete 3 (list 1 2 3 4 5) <)" :note "The comparator is called as (< 3 element), so this deletes every element GREATER than 3: (1 2 3).")
  )
  :caveats ("The optional comparator is called as (= x element) with the SOUGHT value first. With a non-symmetric comparator such as < that reads \"x is less than the element\", not the other way round." "Motoi collapses eq?, eqv? and equal? into one structural test, so the default comparison is deep. Two separately built lists with the same contents are equal here." "delete removes ALL occurrences, not just the first. There is no delete-first in the part." "A non-list second argument returns the empty list rather than raising." "The original list is not modified; SRFI-1 delete! is not part of Motoi.")
  :usecases ("Removing a known value from a list of options or tags." "Filtering by a threshold using a comparator instead of a lambda." "Cleaning a sentinel or placeholder out of a sequence.")
  :related ("remove" "filter" "delete-duplicates" "member")
  :learn (:concept "Removal by value: name what you do not want rather than describing it." :prerequisites ("list" "equal?" "filter") :progression "Learn delete for a known value, remove for a predicate, and delete-duplicates when the thing you do not want is repetition itself.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define tags (list 'draft 'urgent 'draft 'review))\n(display (delete 'draft tags))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define l (list 1 2 3 2 1))\n(define r (delete 2 l))\n(display (list 'no-2-left (not (member 2 r))\n               'others-kept (equal? r (filter (lambda (x) (not (equal? x 2))) l))\n               'length-drop (- (length l) (length r))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'default-structural (delete (list 'a) (list (list 'a) (list 'b) (list 'a)))\n               'comparator-order (delete 3 (list 1 2 3 4 5) <)\n               'comparator-reversed (delete 3 (list 1 2 3 4 5) >)))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator has a tag list and must strip one known tag wherever it appears. Candidates: delete (name the value, one call), (filter (lambda (t) (not (equal? t tag))) tags) (correct, and exactly what delete does, but three more forms to read), or remove with the same lambda (identical, and still a lambda). For removal of a known value, delete is the honest verb."
     :explanation "eng: the tag name appears once and the intent is legible at a glance. math: delete is filter with the negated equality test, so the kept elements are exactly the non-equal ones and their order is preserved. cs: the comparator argument is where surprises live — its argument order is fixed by the standard and reversing it reverses the meaning of the whole call."
     :meta "Why delete and not filter: filter with a negated equality lambda is what delete IS, but the lambda hides the intent behind a double negative that readers routinely misparse.")
    (:eng "Prefer delete when you know the value; prefer remove when you know a property."
     :math "delete(x,l) = filter(y -> not(x = y), l); it is a restriction of filter, not a new operation."
     :cs "Same slot as list.remove in Python (which deletes only the first) and Array.filter with a negated test in JavaScript. Note the difference: SRFI-1 delete removes ALL.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Removal by named value: state the thing you do not want and take out every occurrence of it, leaving order intact."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(delete 2 (list 1 2 3 2 1))")
             (:domain "pulling every copy of one card out of a hand" :code "(define hand (list 'ace 'joker 'king 'joker 'two))\n(display (delete 'joker hand))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "set difference against a singleton: S minus {x}" :code "(define s (list 1 2 3 2))\n(display (list (delete 2 s) (lset-difference equal? s (list 2))))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, delete strips a value. Pulling every joker out of a hand leaves the rest of the hand in the order it was held. In math, removing all copies of one value is set difference against a singleton, except that a list keeps multiplicity for everything else. All three share: one named item out, everything else exactly as it was."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a specific known item must be excluded and the arrangement of everything else matters.")
    (:eng "Order preservation is the property that makes delete safe to drop into an existing pipeline."
     :math "On lists, delete is set difference against a singleton lifted to multisets, dropping every copy."
     :cs "Removing all occurrences versus removing the first is a real API split across languages; SRFI-1 chose all.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define l (list 'a 'b 'a 'c 'a))\n(define r (delete 'a l))\n(display (list 'result r 'removed (- (length l) (length r)) 'none-left (not (member 'a r))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (laws? x l)\n  (let ((r (delete x l)))\n    (and (equal? r (filter (lambda (y) (not (equal? x y))) l))\n         (not (member x r))\n         (equal? r (delete x r)))))\n(display (map (lambda (l) (laws? 2 l))\n              (list (list 1 2 3 2) (list 2 2 2) (list 1 3) (list))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'idempotent (equal? (delete 1 (delete 1 (list 1 2 1))) (delete 1 (list 1 2 1)))\n               'all-removed (delete 5 (list 5 5 5))\n               'none-present (delete 9 (list 1 2 3))\n               'comparator-asymmetry (list (delete 3 (list 1 2 3 4) <) (delete 3 (list 1 2 3 4) >))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "delete returns the sublist of elements NOT equal to x, in their original order; it removes every occurrence, so x is absent from the result; and it is idempotent — deleting the same value again changes nothing. With a comparator, the test is (= x element) with x first, so an asymmetric comparator gives asymmetric results."
     :invariant "forall x, forall list l. delete(x,l) = filter(y -> not equal?(x,y), l) and x is not a member of delete(x,l) and delete(x, delete(x,l)) = delete(x,l)"
     :meta "The pattern is value-removal with order preservation. What would falsify it: a surviving occurrence of x, a reordering, or a second delete that changed the answer. The comparator-order case is a specification detail worth testing explicitly. See book-of-math on idempotent operations.")
    (:eng "Idempotence means delete is safe to run twice, which matters when a pipeline may be retried."
     :math "delete is a projection onto the sublist avoiding x; projections are idempotent by definition."
     :cs "Comparator argument order is the single most common porting bug in the lset and delete family. Test it, do not assume it.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (retire tag records)\n  (map (lambda (r) (cons (car r) (list (delete tag (cadr r))))) records))\n(display (retire 'draft (list (list 'doc1 (list 'draft 'urgent))\n                              (list 'doc2 (list 'final))\n                              (list 'doc3 (list 'draft 'draft 'review)))))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (delete-all xs l) (fold (lambda (x acc) (delete x acc)) l xs))\n(display (delete-all (list 2 4) (list 1 2 3 4 5 4 2)))\n(newline)\n(display (equal? (delete-all (list 2 4) (list 1 2 3 4 5))\n                 (lset-difference equal? (list 1 2 3 4 5) (list 2 4))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (below-threshold t xs) (delete t xs <))\n(display (below-threshold 3 (list 1 2 3 4 5)))\n(newline)\n(define (at-or-above-threshold t xs) (delete t xs >))\n(display (at-or-above-threshold 3 (list 1 2 3 4 5)))\n(newline)"
     :base "delete removes every occurrence of one named value."
     :emergent "eng: bulk retirement of a tag across a whole record set, with each record keeping its remaining tags in order. math: folding delete over a list of unwanted values reconstructs set difference, so the general operation is generated by repeated application of the singleton one. cs: threshold filtering by comparator — passing < or > turns a value-removal verb into a range filter without writing a lambda, at the cost of an argument order that must be read carefully."
     :meta "The composition folds delete over several values or supplies a comparator. What emerges is the exclusion family: bulk tag retirement, set difference, and comparator-driven thresholds. See book-of-composition on folded removals.")
    (:eng "Retiring a tag fleet-wide with one fold keeps the policy in one place instead of one per record type."
     :math "Repeated singleton difference equals difference by a set; that is the induction that lset-difference short-circuits."
     :cs "Using a comparator as a filter is clever and slightly dangerous: it is compact, and it depends entirely on the argument order the standard fixed.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "remove" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(remove pred lst) -> list"
  :summary "Keep exactly the elements that do NOT satisfy pred."
  :explanation "remove is the complement of filter: same traversal, same order, opposite decision. Its purpose is readability — a condition stated positively and then removed reads better than the same condition wrapped in not and handed to filter. Together, filter and remove partition a list, and the two results append back to the original in a shuffled but recoverable way."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(remove odd? (list 1 2 3 4 5))" :note "The odds go, the evens stay: (2 4).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(remove (lambda (r) (eq? (car r) 'draft)) (list (list 'draft 'a) (list 'final 'b) (list 'draft 'c)))" :note "Drop the draft records: ((final b)).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define l (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))\n(list (filter even? l) (remove even? l))" :note "filter and remove are the two halves of one partition: ((2 4 6) (1 3 5)).")
  )
  :caveats ("remove is filter with the predicate negated, not delete. delete takes a VALUE; remove takes a PREDICATE." "An element is removed when the predicate returns anything other than #f. In Scheme every value except #f is true, so a predicate that returns 0 or the empty list counts as true." "A non-list argument returns the empty list rather than raising." "Order among the survivors is preserved exactly.")
  :usecases ("Dropping records that match a condition." "Stating an exclusion rule positively instead of as a double negative." "Taking the complement half of a filter.")
  :related ("filter" "delete" "partition" "delete-duplicates")
  :learn (:concept "Exclusion by property: describe what goes, keep what is left." :prerequisites ("filter" "lambda") :progression "Learn filter first, then remove as its complement, then partition when you want both halves at once.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define records (list (list 'draft 'a) (list 'final 'b) (list 'draft 'c)))\n(display (remove (lambda (r) (eq? (car r) 'draft)) records))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define l (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))\n(display (list 'partition-lengths (+ (length (filter even? l)) (length (remove even? l)))\n               'original-length (length l)\n               'disjoint (null? (lset-intersection equal? (filter even? l) (remove even? l)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'remove (remove odd? (list 1 2 3 4))\n               'filter-not (filter (lambda (x) (not (odd? x))) (list 1 2 3 4))\n               'agree (equal? (remove odd? (list 1 2 3 4))\n                              (filter (lambda (x) (not (odd? x))) (list 1 2 3 4)))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator must drop every draft record from a report. Candidates: remove with a positively stated draft? predicate (the rule reads the way the requirement was written), filter with a negated predicate (identical result, and the reader has to invert the condition in their head), or delete (WRONG — delete matches a value, and the records differ in their other fields). For an exclusion stated as a property, remove is the honest verb."
     :explanation "eng: the exclusion rule reads forward, not inverted. math: filter and remove partition the list — their lengths sum to the original and they share no elements — which is the formal content of \"complement\". cs: remove and filter-with-not are the same computation, so the choice between them is entirely about which one a maintainer will read correctly at 2am."
     :meta "Why remove and not filter with not: double negatives are where reading errors happen. The requirement says drop the drafts; the code should say drop the drafts.")
    (:eng "Match the shape of the code to the shape of the requirement, and reviews get cheaper."
     :math "remove(p,l) = filter(not p, l); the pair (filter, remove) is a partition of l by p."
     :cs "Same slot as itertools.filterfalse in Python, reject in Ruby, filterNot in Kotlin and Scala.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Exclusion by property: describe the elements that must go, and keep everything else in its original order."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(remove odd? (list 1 2 3 4 5))")
             (:domain "sorting damaged fruit out of a crate (you describe the damage, not each individual fruit)" :code "(define crate (list (list 'apple 'good) (list 'pear 'bruised) (list 'plum 'good)))\n(display (remove (lambda (f) (eq? (cadr f) 'bruised)) crate))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "the complement of a subset defined by a predicate" :code "(define l (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))\n(display (list 'subset (filter even? l) 'complement (remove even? l)))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, remove drops the matches. Sorting a crate works by describing damage, because you cannot name every bruised pear in advance. In math, the complement of a predicate-defined subset is what is left, and the two together are the whole. All three share: a rule that classifies, and the half you did not ask to keep."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever the exclusion criterion is easier to state than the inclusion criterion.")
    (:eng "Some rules are naturally negative. Forcing them positive to fit filter is how conditions get inverted by mistake."
     :math "A predicate always defines two sets; naming both makes the partition explicit."
     :cs "Having both filter and reject in a library is redundancy on purpose — it buys legibility, not power.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define l (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))\n(define r (remove odd? l))\n(display (list 'result r 'len (length r) 'none-odd (not (any odd? r)) 'order-kept (equal? r (filter even? l))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (laws? p l)\n  (and (equal? (remove p l) (filter (lambda (x) (not (p x))) l))\n       (= (+ (length (filter p l)) (length (remove p l))) (length l))\n       (equal? (remove p (remove p l)) (remove p l))))\n(display (map (lambda (l) (laws? odd? l))\n              (list (list 1 2 3) (list 1 3 5) (list 2 4) (list))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'all-removed (remove odd? (list 1 3 5))\n               'none-removed (remove odd? (list 2 4))\n               'empty (remove odd? (list))\n               'truthiness (remove (lambda (x) 0) (list 1 2 3))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "remove returns exactly the elements for which pred is #f, in their original relative order. It is the complement of filter at the same predicate: the two lengths sum to the original length, and remove is idempotent. Note that anything other than #f counts as true, so a predicate returning 0 removes everything."
     :invariant "forall pred p, forall list l. remove(p,l) = filter(not p, l) and |filter(p,l)| + |remove(p,l)| = |l| and remove(p, remove(p,l)) = remove(p,l)"
     :meta "The pattern is complementary selection. What would falsify it: an element surviving that satisfies pred, a reordering, or lengths that do not sum. The truthiness case shows why a predicate must return #f, not just a falsy-looking value. See book-of-code on Scheme truthiness.")
    (:eng "The length-sum check catches a whole class of predicate bugs in one assertion."
     :math "Idempotence follows from the result already containing no satisfying elements."
     :cs "Scheme truthiness has exactly one false value. Predicates ported from languages where 0 or empty is falsy will misbehave here, and this is the test that shows it.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (clean rows)\n  (remove (lambda (r) (or (null? r) (eq? (car r) 'comment))) rows))\n(display (clean (list (list 'data 1) (list) (list 'comment 'x) (list 'data 2))))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define l (list 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8))\n(define parts (call-with-values (lambda () (partition odd? l)) list))\n(display (list 'partition parts\n               'same-as-filter-remove (equal? parts (list (filter odd? l) (remove odd? l)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (sieve limit)\n  (let loop ((candidates (cdr (cdr (iota (+ 1 limit))))) (primes (list)))\n    (if (null? candidates)\n        (reverse primes)\n        (let ((p (car candidates)))\n          (loop (remove (lambda (n) (zero? (modulo n p))) (cdr candidates))\n                (cons p primes))))))\n(display (sieve 30))\n(newline)"
     :base "remove keeps exactly the elements that fail a predicate."
     :emergent "eng: a cleaning stage that drops blanks and comments in one pass with one composite rule. math: partition materializes both halves at once and agrees exactly with the filter/remove pair, which is the constructive statement that a predicate splits a set. cs: the sieve of Eratosthenes — take the head as prime, remove its multiples from the rest, recurse, and the whole classical algorithm is one remove inside one loop."
     :meta "The composition applies remove repeatedly against a changing predicate. What emerges is the sieving family: data cleaning, partitioning, and the prime sieve. See book-of-cs-curriculum on the sieve of Eratosthenes.")
    (:eng "One composite rule beats a chain of single-rule passes when the rules are all exclusions."
     :math "partition is filter and remove computed together; it exists so the second half is not recomputed."
     :cs "The sieve is the standard first example of an algorithm whose cost analysis is not obvious from its length; it is four lines and it is not linear.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "reduce-right" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(reduce-right f ridentity lst) -> value"
  :summary "Right fold seeded by the LAST element of the list; ridentity is returned only for the empty list."
  :explanation "reduce-right is fold-right without a starting value: the last element seeds the accumulator and the fold proceeds leftward, so nothing artificial is ever combined in. That is the whole point — for an operation with no natural identity, such as max or a pairing constructor, there is no honest seed to supply. ridentity is a fallback for the empty list ONLY. Like every SRFI-1 fold, f is called element-first: (f element accumulator)."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(reduce-right + 0 (list 1 2 3))" :note "Sums to 6. With a genuine identity the seed never matters.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(list (reduce-right - 0 (list 1 2 3)) (reduce-right + 999 (list 7)))" :note "Right association gives 1-(2-3) = 2; and a one-element list returns that element, ignoring the seed entirely: (2 7).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(reduce-right (lambda (x acc) (list x acc)) 'none (list 1 2 3))" :note "Right-nesting a pairing constructor: (1 (2 3)). The last element seeded it, so there is no stray none in the tree.")
  )
  :caveats ("THE TRAP: ridentity is IGNORED for a non-empty list. It is returned only when the list is empty. R6RS fold-right and Racket foldr fold their seed in every time — porting code between them silently changes the answer." "f is called ELEMENT-FIRST: (f element accumulator). R6RS fold-right passes the accumulator first." "For a one-element list the answer is that element; f is never called." "A non-list third argument raises a teaching error." "Use fold-right instead when you genuinely want a starting value combined in.")
  :usecases ("Combining with an operation that has no natural identity, such as max or a pairing constructor." "Right-associated reduction where the last element is the natural base." "Building a right-nested structure from a flat list.")
  :related ("reduce" "fold-right" "fold" "append-reverse")
  :learn (:concept "Seedless right fold: let the last element be the base case instead of inventing one." :prerequisites ("fold-right" "lambda" "reduce") :progression "Learn fold-right (explicit seed) first, then reduce-right for operations that have no honest seed; read broken-examples/reduce-arg-order for the cross-standard trap.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define readings (list 3 9 4 7))\n(display (reduce-right max 0 readings))\n(newline)\n(display (reduce-right (lambda (x acc) (if (> x acc) x acc)) 0 readings))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (right-assoc? l)\n  (equal? (reduce-right - 0 l) (fold-right - (last l) (drop-right l 1))))\n(display (map right-assoc? (list (list 1 2 3) (list 4 9) (list 5))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'empty-uses-ridentity (reduce-right + 999 (list))\n               'single-ignores-ridentity (reduce-right + 999 (list 7))\n               'many-ignores-ridentity (reduce-right + 999 (list 1 2 3))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator must combine a list of readings with max, and there is no honest starting value — any seed they invent could win and corrupt the answer for a list of negative readings. Candidates: reduce-right (the last element seeds it, so nothing artificial enters), fold-right with a seed (correct only if the seed is a true identity for the operation, which max does not have over the integers), or a manual loop over (cdr lst) starting from (car lst) (correct and hand-written). For a seedless combine, reduce-right is the honest verb."
     :explanation "eng: max over readings with no invented floor, so negative readings cannot be masked by a seed of 0. math: reduce-right on a non-empty list is fold-right seeded by the last element over the remaining prefix; that identity is what makes the seedless form well defined. cs: the three arities of the same call — empty, single, many — show exactly when ridentity is consulted, which is only in the empty case."
     :meta "Why reduce-right and not fold-right with a seed: the seed must be an identity for the operation or it changes the answer. max has no identity in the integers, so any seed is a lie that happens to be true for the data you tested on.")
    (:eng "A seed that is not an identity is a bug waiting for the right input. Prefer the seedless form when there is no identity."
     :math "For non-empty l, reduce-right(f, z, l) = fold-right(f, last(l), drop-right(l,1)); z appears only when l is empty."
     :cs "Same slot as reduce without an initializer in Python functools, reduceRight in JavaScript without an initial value, foldr1 in Haskell.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Seedless combination: fold a sequence using its own last element as the base, so no artificial starting value enters the result."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(reduce-right + 0 (list 1 2 3))")
             (:domain "a relay run backwards from the finish (the last runner starts holding the baton, nobody hands in an empty one)" :code "(define legs (list 12 9 14 7))\n(display (reduce-right + 0 legs))\n(newline)\n(display (reduce-right max 0 legs))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "a right-associated binary operation over a non-empty sequence" :code "(define (right-assoc f l)\n  (if (null? (cdr l)) (car l) (f (car l) (right-assoc f (cdr l)))))\n(display (list (right-assoc - (list 1 2 3)) (reduce-right - 0 (list 1 2 3))))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, reduce-right seeds from the last element. In a relay run backwards from the finish, the anchor runner already holds the baton — nobody starts with an empty hand. In math, a right-associated binary operation over a non-empty sequence needs no unit, only a last term. All three share: the base case is drawn from the data, not invented."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever an operation is meaningful on non-empty input but has no neutral element to start from.")
    (:eng "Drawing the base case from the data means the empty case must be handled separately and explicitly, which is honest."
     :math "A semigroup has an associative operation but no identity; reduce-right is the fold that a semigroup admits and a monoid fold does not require."
     :cs "Library functions that demand an initial value force callers to invent identities; the seedless form pushes the empty case back to where the decision belongs.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define l (list 1 2 3 4))\n(display (list 'sum (reduce-right + 0 l)\n               'diff (reduce-right - 0 l)\n               'max (reduce-right max 0 l)\n               'single (reduce-right + 999 (list 42))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (seed-ignored? f z l)\n  (equal? (reduce-right f z l) (reduce-right f (+ z 1000) l)))\n(display (map (lambda (l) (seed-ignored? + 0 l))\n              (list (list 1 2 3) (list 7) (list 5 5))))\n(newline)\n(display (list 'empty-differs (list (reduce-right + 0 (list)) (reduce-right + 1000 (list)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define order (list))\n(define (trace x acc) (set! order (cons (list x acc) order)) (+ x acc))\n(define total (reduce-right trace 0 (list 1 2 3 4)))\n(display (list 'total total 'calls (reverse order)))\n(newline)"
     :claim "For a non-empty list, reduce-right ignores ridentity entirely and returns the right-associated combination seeded by the last element; changing ridentity cannot change the answer. For the empty list it returns ridentity unchanged. f is applied element-first, from the right end leftward."
     :invariant "forall f, forall z, forall non-empty list l. reduce-right(f,z,l) = fold-right(f, last(l), drop-right(l,1)) and reduce-right(f,z,()) = z"
     :meta "The pattern is a seedless right fold. What would falsify it: a non-empty result that changed when ridentity changed, or a call order that ran left to right. The seed-ignored test IS the cross-standard trap made executable. See broken-examples/reduce-arg-order.slatl.")
    (:eng "Testing that the seed does not matter is the only test that catches a port from R6RS fold-right."
     :math "The semigroup fold is determined by associativity alone; the identity element is extra structure that reduce-right does not require."
     :cs "The trace program makes the call order and the argument order both visible, which is the fastest way to settle an argument about a fold.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (longest-run readings)\n  (reduce-right (lambda (x acc) (if (>= (cadr x) (cadr acc)) x acc)) (list 'none 0) readings))\n(display (longest-run (list (list 'a 3) (list 'b 9) (list 'c 9) (list 'd 4))))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (to-nested l) (reduce-right (lambda (x acc) (list x acc)) 'none l))\n(display (to-nested (list 1 2 3 4)))\n(newline)\n(define (flatten-nested t) (if (pair? t) (cons (car t) (flatten-nested (cadr t))) (list)))\n(display (flatten-nested (to-nested (list 1 2 3 4))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (compose-all fs)\n  (reduce-right (lambda (f g) (lambda (x) (f (g x)))) (lambda (x) x) fs))\n(define pipeline (compose-all (list (lambda (x) (* 2 x)) (lambda (x) (+ x 3)) (lambda (x) (- x 1)))))\n(display (pipeline 10))\n(newline)"
     :base "reduce-right folds from the right with the last element as the seed."
     :emergent "eng: an argmax that returns the whole winning record rather than just its score, because the accumulator is a record and never a bare number. math: the flat-to-nested transform and its inverse, showing that a right fold with a pairing constructor is exactly the list-to-cons-tree isomorphism. cs: function composition — reduce-right over a list of procedures builds the composed pipeline, and right association is what makes the leftmost function apply last."
     :meta "The composition passes reduce-right a non-numeric accumulator. What emerges is the right-association family: argmax over records, nested-structure building, and function composition. See book-of-composition on folds over non-numeric accumulators.")
    (:eng "Folding records rather than scores means the answer carries its own provenance."
     :math "Right folding with a pairing constructor over a list is the canonical isomorphism between a sequence and a right-nested tree."
     :cs "Composing functions right-associatively matches the mathematical convention that f after g applies g first; folding left would silently reverse the pipeline.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "unfold" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(unfold p f g seed [tail-gen]) -> list"
  :summary "Grow a list from a seed: while (p seed) is FALSE, emit (f seed) and step the seed with (g seed)."
  :explanation "unfold is the dual of fold. A fold consumes a list into a value; unfold produces a list from a value. Three procedures do the work: p decides when to STOP, f says what to emit at each step, and g says how to advance. It is the general loop, written as data flow rather than as control flow, and it is how you build a sequence whose length is decided by a condition rather than by a count."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(unfold (lambda (x) (> x 5)) (lambda (x) x) (lambda (x) (+ x 1)) 1)" :note "Count from 1 while not greater than 5: (1 2 3 4 5).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(unfold zero? (lambda (m) (modulo m 10)) (lambda (m) (quotient m 10)) 4071)" :note "Peel decimal digits off the bottom until nothing is left: (1 7 0 4). Reverse it to read the number.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(unfold (lambda (n) (= n 1))\n        (lambda (n) n)\n        (lambda (n) (if (even? n) (quotient n 2) (+ 1 (* 3 n))))\n        6)" :note "The Collatz trajectory from 6, stopping before the 1: (6 3 10 5 16 8 4 2).")
  )
  :caveats ("p is the STOP predicate. Generation continues while (p seed) is FALSE, and the seed that finally satisfies p is NOT emitted." "If p never becomes true, unfold does not terminate. Make sure g moves the seed toward the stopping condition." "DIVERGENCE from SRFI-1: when tail-gen is supplied and returns a LIST, Motoi splices it onto the end (matching SRFI-1 for the list case). When tail-gen returns a NON-list, SRFI-1 makes it the improper tail; Motoi SILENTLY DROPS it. Do not rely on a non-list tail-gen." "The three procedures are each called once per step, so an expensive f or g costs once per emitted element.")
  :usecases ("Generating a sequence whose length depends on a condition rather than a count." "Peeling digits, factors or bits off a number." "Turning an iterative state machine into a list of its states.")
  :related ("fold" "fold-right" "iota" "list-tabulate" "reduce-right")
  :learn (:concept "Generation from a seed: stop rule, emit rule, step rule." :prerequisites ("lambda" "fold" "list-tabulate") :progression "Learn list-tabulate when the count is known, then unfold when only the stopping condition is known; then read fold as its exact mirror.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define (countdown n)\n  (unfold (lambda (x) (< x 0)) (lambda (x) x) (lambda (x) (- x 1)) n))\n(display (countdown 5))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (digits n)\n  (reverse (unfold zero? (lambda (m) (modulo m 10)) (lambda (m) (quotient m 10)) n)))\n(display (list (digits 4071) (digits 9) (digits 0)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define steps 0)\n(define r (unfold (lambda (x) (> x 4))\n                  (lambda (x) (begin (set! steps (+ steps 1)) (* x x)))\n                  (lambda (x) (+ x 1))\n                  1))\n(display (list 'result r 'emit-calls steps))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator must produce a sequence whose length is not known in advance — it ends when a condition on the running state becomes true. Candidates: unfold (state the stop rule, the emit rule and the step rule, and the length falls out), list-tabulate (WRONG — it needs the count up front, which is exactly what is unknown), or a named-let accumulating with cons and reversing at the end (correct, and it is what unfold compiles to conceptually). For condition-terminated generation, unfold is the honest verb."
     :explanation "eng: a countdown whose length comes from the seed rather than from a separate count. math: digit extraction is the canonical unfold — repeated division by the base, emitting the remainder, until the quotient is zero. cs: counting the emit calls shows the cost model directly, one call to each of the three procedures per emitted element."
     :meta "Why unfold and not list-tabulate: list-tabulate is indexed by position and needs n. unfold is driven by state and needs only a predicate that the state will eventually satisfy.")
    (:eng "When the length is a consequence rather than an input, express the termination rule and let the length follow."
     :math "unfold is the anamorphism dual to fold (the catamorphism): fold destroys structure, unfold creates it."
     :cs "Same slot as itertools.takewhile over an infinite generator in Python, unfoldr in Haskell, iterate-until in various loop libraries.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Generation from a seed: state when to stop, what to emit, and how to advance, and let the sequence build itself."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(unfold (lambda (x) (> x 5)) (lambda (x) x) (lambda (x) (+ x 1)) 1)")
             (:domain "paying out change (keep handing over the largest coin that fits until nothing is owed)" :code "(define (change amount coins)\n  (unfold (lambda (s) (zero? (car s)))\n          (lambda (s) (car (cdr s)))\n          (lambda (s)\n            (let ((owed (car s)) (cs (cdr (cdr s))))\n              (let ((c (car (filter (lambda (c) (<= c owed)) cs))))\n                (cons (- owed c) (cons c cs)))))\n          (let ((c (car (filter (lambda (c) (<= c amount)) coins))))\n            (cons (- amount c) (cons c coins)))))\n(display (change 37 (list 25 10 5 1)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "an anamorphism: the categorical dual of a fold" :code "(define (ana stop emit step seed)\n  (if (stop seed) (list) (cons (emit seed) (ana stop emit step (step seed)))))\n(display (ana (lambda (x) (> x 5)) (lambda (x) x) (lambda (x) (+ x 1)) 1))\n(newline)\n(display (equal? (ana (lambda (x) (> x 5)) (lambda (x) x) (lambda (x) (+ x 1)) 1)\n                 (unfold (lambda (x) (> x 5)) (lambda (x) x) (lambda (x) (+ x 1)) 1)))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, unfold builds until the stop rule fires. Making change works the same way: keep handing over the largest coin that fits and reduce what is owed, until nothing is owed. In category terms this is an anamorphism, the exact dual of the fold that consumes a list. All three share: a state, a rule for stopping, a rule for emitting, and a rule for advancing."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a process is defined by its termination condition rather than by its length.")
    (:eng "Separating stop, emit and step makes each one testable on its own; a hand-rolled loop entangles all three."
     :math "Anamorphism and catamorphism are duals; unfold and fold are their list instances, and their composition is a hylomorphism."
     :cs "Generators and iterators in other languages are the same idea with the state hidden inside a closure instead of passed explicitly.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define r (unfold (lambda (x) (> x 4)) (lambda (x) (* x 10)) (lambda (x) (+ x 1)) 1))\n(display (list 'result r 'len (length r) 'expected-len 4))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (unfold-then-fold n)\n  (let ((xs (unfold (lambda (x) (> x n)) (lambda (x) x) (lambda (x) (+ x 1)) 1)))\n    (list xs (fold + 0 xs) (quotient (* n (+ n 1)) 2))))\n(display (unfold-then-fold 5))\n(newline)\n(display (equal? (unfold (lambda (x) (>= x 5)) (lambda (x) x) (lambda (x) (+ x 1)) 0) (iota 5)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'stop-immediately (unfold (lambda (x) #t) (lambda (x) x) (lambda (x) (+ x 1)) 0)\n               'tail-gen-list (unfold (lambda (x) (> x 3)) (lambda (x) x) (lambda (x) (+ x 1)) 1 (lambda (s) (list 'end s)))\n               'tail-gen-nonlist-dropped (unfold (lambda (x) (> x 3)) (lambda (x) x) (lambda (x) (+ x 1)) 1 (lambda (s) s))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "unfold emits (f seed) once for each state at which p is false, advancing by g, and stops at the first state where p is true WITHOUT emitting it. With the identity emit and an increment step it reproduces iota exactly. A tail-gen returning a list is spliced on; a tail-gen returning a NON-list is silently dropped by Motoi where SRFI-1 would build an improper list."
     :invariant "unfold(p,f,g,s) = if p(s) then () else cons(f(s), unfold(p,f,g,g(s))) — the emitted sequence is [f(s), f(g(s)), f(g(g(s))), ...] up to but excluding the first state satisfying p"
     :meta "The pattern is anamorphic generation. What would falsify it: emission of the stopping state, a length that does not match the number of false-p states, or disagreement with iota on the counting case. The dropped non-list tail-gen is a KNOWN divergence recorded in the caveats and reported to the maintainers. See book-of-math on recursion and corecursion.")
    (:eng "The iota agreement test is the cheapest correctness anchor: a verb that can reproduce a known verb is doing what you think."
     :math "The recursive equation IS the definition; the implementation is a loop, and the invariant is what says the two agree."
     :cs "Non-termination is a real failure mode here and no test can rule it out in general; the discipline is to prove that g drives the seed toward p.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (base-digits n b)\n  (reverse (unfold zero? (lambda (m) (modulo m b)) (lambda (m) (quotient m b)) n)))\n(display (list 'decimal (base-digits 4071 10) 'binary (base-digits 13 2) 'hexish (base-digits 255 16)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (collatz n)\n  (append (unfold (lambda (m) (= m 1))\n                  (lambda (m) m)\n                  (lambda (m) (if (even? m) (quotient m 2) (+ 1 (* 3 m))))\n                  n)\n          (list 1)))\n(display (collatz 6))\n(newline)\n(display (list 'length-from-27 (length (collatz 27))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (run-machine transitions start)\n  (unfold (lambda (s) (eq? s 'halt))\n          (lambda (s) s)\n          (lambda (s) (cadr (assq s transitions)))\n          start))\n(display (run-machine (list (list 'idle 'load) (list 'load 'run) (list 'run 'flush) (list 'flush 'halt)) 'idle))\n(newline)"
     :base "unfold grows a list from a seed under a stop rule, an emit rule and a step rule."
     :emergent "eng: base conversion for any radix — the same three rules with the base as a parameter give decimal, binary or hexadecimal digits. math: the Collatz trajectory, a sequence whose length is not predictable from its seed by any known formula, which is exactly why a count-based constructor cannot build it. cs: a state-machine tracer — feeding unfold a transition table turns the machine into the list of states it visits, which is the execution trace."
     :meta "The composition parameterizes the step rule. What emerges is the trajectory family: radix conversion, iterated maps, and state-machine traces are all one seed plus one step rule. See book-of-sim-games on state machines and book-of-math on iterated maps.")
    (:eng "Parameterizing the base turns three algorithms into one, and the tests for one cover all of them."
     :math "Collatz is the standing example that termination can be observed for every tested input and still be unproven in general."
     :cs "Turning a machine into its trace is the debugging move: the states become data, and data can be inspected, diffed and asserted on.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "lset-adjoin" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(lset-adjoin = lst elt ...) -> list"
  :summary "Add each element to the list unless an equal one is already there; new elements go on the FRONT."
  :explanation "lset-adjoin treats lst as a set and adds elements one at a time. An element is added only if no existing member compares equal to it under the supplied predicate. Additions are consed onto the front, so the answer reads newest-first: elements that were accepted come out in the reverse of the order they were offered. The equality test is the first argument, which is the signature every SRFI-1 lset procedure shares."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(lset-adjoin equal? (list 1 2) 3)" :note "A genuinely new element goes on the front: (3 1 2).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(lset-adjoin equal? (list 1 2) 3 1 4)" :note "The 1 is already present so it is skipped; 3 and 4 are added front-first, so the answer is (4 3 1 2).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (collect-tags seen tags) (apply lset-adjoin (cons eq? (cons seen tags))))\n(collect-tags (list 'draft) (list 'urgent 'draft 'review))" :note "Applying lset-adjoin to a whole batch dedupes against what was already seen: (review urgent draft).")
  )
  :caveats ("The equality predicate is the FIRST argument, always. (lset-union equal? a b), never (lset-union a b). Getting this wrong raises a teaching error that names the fix." "New elements are consed on the FRONT, so accepted elements appear in REVERSE order of arrival. If you need arrival order, reverse the result or build with append." "The predicate is called as (= new-element existing-member) — the candidate FIRST. lset-union calls it the other way round. With a non-symmetric predicate such as < the two verbs give different answers." "These verbs treat lists AS SETS but never normalize them. Duplicates already present in the input survive into the output; nothing here calls delete-duplicates for you." "Adding nothing returns the original list unchanged, duplicates and all: (lset-adjoin equal? (list 1 1)) is (1 1).")
  :usecases ("Accumulating a set of tags, ids, or visited nodes without repeats." "Interning a value into a table of already-known values." "Folding a stream into a set one element at a time.")
  :related ("lset-union" "delete-duplicates" "member" "cons")
  :learn (:concept "Insert-if-absent: adding to a set is a conditional cons, guarded by a membership test." :prerequisites ("member" "cons" "equal?") :progression "Learn member first (the test), then lset-adjoin (the guarded insert), then lset-union (the same insert applied to a whole list).")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define (see seen tag) (lset-adjoin eq? seen tag))\n(define visited (see (see (see (list) 'home) 'shop) 'home))\n(display (list 'visited visited 'count (length visited)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define s (list 1 2))\n(display (list 'idempotent (equal? (lset-adjoin equal? s 1) s)\n               'grows-by-one (= (length (lset-adjoin equal? s 3)) (+ 1 (length s)))\n               'member-after (if (member 3 (lset-adjoin equal? s 3)) #t #f)\n               'old-still-there (lset<= equal? s (lset-adjoin equal? s 3))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define tests 0)\n(define (counted a b) (set! tests (+ tests 1)) (equal? a b))\n(define r (lset-adjoin counted (list 1 2 3 4) 3 9))\n(display (list 'result r 'comparisons tests 'note 'linear-scan-per-element))\n(newline)"
     :problem "A crawler must remember which pages it has visited and must not record a page twice. Candidates: lset-adjoin (one call per page, membership handled for you), cons plus a hand-written member check (correct, but the guard is now the caller problem and gets forgotten), or cons everything then delete-duplicates at the end (correct only if nothing reads the set mid-run, and it pays for the duplicates it later throws away). For maintaining a set incrementally, lset-adjoin is the honest verb."
     :explanation "eng: revisiting home does not lengthen the list, so the set stays a set at every step. math: adjoining an existing member is the identity, adjoining a new one grows the length by exactly one, and the old set is always a subset of the new one. cs: the counter shows one linear membership scan per offered element, so the cost is the product of the set size and the batch size — fine for tens, wrong for millions."
     :meta "Why lset-adjoin and not cons plus delete-duplicates at the end: the intermediate list is not a set, so any code that reads it mid-run sees duplicates. Maintaining the invariant continuously is cheaper to reason about than restoring it later.")
    (:eng "An invariant kept at every step never has to be repaired, and never has a window in which it is false."
     :math "Adjoin is idempotent, so applying it twice is the same as applying it once. That is what makes it a set operation and not a list operation."
     :cs "Linear membership means quadratic total cost. For large sets a hash table is the right structure; lset-adjoin is for small sets where clarity wins.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Insert-if-absent: add a thing to a collection only when nothing equal to it is already in there."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(lset-adjoin equal? (list 1 2) 3 1 4)")
             (:domain "a guest list for a party (a name already on the list is not written twice, and the newest RSVP sits at the top of the page)" :code "(define guests (lset-adjoin string=? (list \"ana\" \"bo\") \"cy\" \"ana\" \"di\"))\n(display (list 'guests guests 'headcount (length guests)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "the union of a set with a singleton, applied one singleton at a time" :code "(define (adjoin1 s x) (if (member x s) s (cons x s)))\n(define (adjoin-all s xs) (fold (lambda (x acc) (adjoin1 acc x)) s xs))\n(display (list 'hand-rolled (adjoin-all (list 1 2) (list 3 1 4))\n               'library (lset-adjoin equal? (list 1 2) 3 1 4)))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, lset-adjoin skips what is already present. On a guest list, a repeated RSVP does not get a second seat, and the headcount stays honest. In math, adjoining is union with a one-element set, folded across the offered elements. All three share: a membership test, a conditional insert, and a size that grows only on genuinely new material."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever the same thing can be offered twice and must count once: guest lists, tag sets, visited nodes, interned symbols.")
    (:eng "Idempotent operations are safe to retry, which is why they are the backbone of anything that might be delivered twice."
     :math "S union {x} is the whole definition; the fold is just the plural of it."
     :cs "Interning, memoization keys, and deduplicated queues are all this one operation wearing different names.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define base (list 'a 'b))\n(define grown (lset-adjoin eq? base 'c 'a))\n(display (list 'base base 'grown grown\n               'base-unchanged (equal? base (list 'a 'b))\n               'added-only-c (= (length grown) 3)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (laws? s x)\n  (let ((once (lset-adjoin equal? s x)))\n    (and (equal? (lset-adjoin equal? once x) once)\n         (lset<= equal? s once)\n         (if (member x s) (equal? once s) (= (length once) (+ 1 (length s))))\n         (if (member x once) #t #f))))\n(display (map (lambda (p) (laws? (car p) (car (cdr p))))\n              (list (list (list 1 2) 3) (list (list 1 2) 1) (list (list) 7) (list (list 1 1) 1))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'no-elements (lset-adjoin equal? (list 1 1))\n               'empty-base (lset-adjoin equal? (list) 1 2 3)\n               'non-list-raises (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (lset-adjoin equal? 7 1))\n               'missing-predicate-raises (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (lset-adjoin (list 1) (list 2)))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "lset-adjoin is idempotent and monotone: adjoining an element twice is the same as adjoining it once, the original set is always a subset of the result, and the result always contains the element. The length grows by exactly one for a new element and not at all for a present one."
     :invariant "forall predicate =, set s, element x. adjoin(s,x) contains x, and s is a subset of adjoin(s,x), and adjoin(adjoin(s,x),x) = adjoin(s,x)"
     :meta "The pattern is idempotent insertion. What would falsify it: a second adjoin that changes the answer, a lost original member, or a result that does not contain the thing just added. See book-of-cs-curriculum on set abstract data types.")
    (:eng "The base list is untouched: lset-adjoin allocates a new list rather than mutating, so shared references do not surprise you."
     :math "Idempotence plus monotonicity is exactly the closure-operator shape; that is why repeated application converges."
     :cs "Note that the empty-base case returns (3 2 1) for elements offered 1 2 3 — front-consing means the boundary case is where the reversal is most visible.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (add-tag db id tag)\n  (map (lambda (row)\n         (if (eq? (car row) id)\n             (list id (lset-adjoin eq? (car (cdr row)) tag))\n             row))\n       db))\n(define db (list (list 'a (list 'red)) (list 'b (list 'blue))))\n(display (add-tag (add-tag db 'a 'hot) 'a 'red))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (union-by-adjoin a b) (apply lset-adjoin (cons equal? (cons a b))))\n(define a (list 1 2 3))\n(define b (list 3 4))\n(display (list 'adjoin-fold (union-by-adjoin a b)\n               'library (lset-union equal? a b)\n               'same-set (lset= equal? (union-by-adjoin a b) (lset-union equal? a b))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (reachable graph start)\n  (let loop ((todo (list start)) (seen (list)))\n    (if (null? todo)\n        seen\n        (let ((n (car todo)))\n          (if (member n seen)\n              (loop (cdr todo) seen)\n              (let ((nbrs (let ((e (assq n graph))) (if e (car (cdr e)) (list)))))\n                (loop (append nbrs (cdr todo)) (lset-adjoin eq? seen n))))))))\n(define g (list (list 'a (list 'b 'c)) (list 'b (list 'a)) (list 'c (list 'd)) (list 'd (list 'a))))\n(display (list 'reachable (reachable g 'a)))\n(newline)"
     :base "lset-adjoin adds an element to a list only when no equal member is already present."
     :emergent "eng: a tagging system — adjoin inside a map over rows gives idempotent labelling, so re-applying the same tag is a no-op and the operation is safe to replay. math: folding adjoin across a second list reconstructs lset-union, showing that union is not a separate primitive but the plural of adjoin. cs: graph traversal — the visited set is exactly an adjoin accumulator, and it is the only thing standing between a cyclic graph and an infinite loop."
     :meta "The composition puts adjoin inside a loop that can revisit. What emerges is the visited-set family: cycle detection, memoization, and idempotent event handling are the same accumulator. See book-of-composition on accumulators.")
    (:eng "Idempotent updates are what make a retryable pipeline possible; the same message delivered twice must not double the effect."
     :math "Union built from adjoin shows the operation hierarchy: singleton insert is primitive, everything else is a fold over it."
     :cs "Without the visited set that traversal never terminates on a cycle. The set is not an optimization here; it is the termination argument.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "lset-union" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(lset-union = lst ...) -> list"
  :summary "Combine any number of lists into one list holding every element that appears in at least one of them."
  :explanation "lset-union walks the lists left to right, keeping an answer that starts as the first list. Every element of every later list that is not already in the answer is consed onto the FRONT. The result therefore preserves the first list in its original order at the tail, with newly discovered elements stacked in front of it in reverse order of discovery. The equality test is the first argument."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(lset-union equal? (list 1 2 3) (list 3 4))" :note "The 3 is already there; the new 4 goes on the front: (4 1 2 3).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(lset-union equal? (list 1) (list 2) (list 3))" :note "Any number of lists is allowed, and each new element is consed on the front, so the answer is (3 2 1).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(lset-union eq? (list 'read) (list) (list 'read 'write))" :note "Empty lists contribute nothing and are skipped entirely: (write read).")
  )
  :caveats ("The equality predicate is the FIRST argument, always. (lset-union equal? a b), never (lset-union a b). Getting this wrong raises a teaching error that names the fix." "New elements are consed on the FRONT, so the output order is not the input order. Sort or reverse the answer if order matters." "The predicate is called as (= existing-member new-element) — the OPPOSITE of lset-adjoin, which puts the candidate first. Only a non-symmetric predicate can tell, but then it tells loudly." "These verbs treat lists AS SETS but never normalize them. Duplicates already present in the input survive into the output; nothing here calls delete-duplicates for you." "When the answer so far is empty, the next list is adopted WHOLE, duplicates included: (lset-union equal? (list) (list 1 1)) is (1 1)." "With zero lists the answer is the empty list; with one list the answer is that list unchanged.")
  :usecases ("Merging permission sets, tag sets, or feature flags from several sources." "Collecting the distinct keys appearing across several records." "Combining a default configuration with an override list.")
  :related ("lset-adjoin" "lset-intersection" "lset-difference" "append" "delete-duplicates")
  :learn (:concept "Union: everything that is in at least one of the collections, counted once." :prerequisites ("lset-adjoin" "member" "append") :progression "Learn lset-adjoin first (union with one element), then lset-union, then lset-intersection and lset-difference as the other two Boolean combinations.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define default (list 'read))\n(define role (list 'read 'write))\n(define grant (list 'admin))\n(define perms (lset-union eq? default role grant))\n(display (list 'permissions perms 'can-write (if (memq 'write perms) #t #f)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define a (list 1 2 3))\n(define b (list 3 4))\n(display (list 'commutative-as-sets (lset= equal? (lset-union equal? a b) (lset-union equal? b a))\n               'a-subset (lset<= equal? a (lset-union equal? a b))\n               'b-subset (lset<= equal? b (lset-union equal? a b))\n               'idempotent (lset= equal? (lset-union equal? a a) a)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (union-then-sort . ls) (sort (apply lset-union (cons equal? ls)) <))\n(display (list 'raw (lset-union equal? (list 1 2 3) (list 3 4) (list 0))\n               'normalized (union-then-sort (list 1 2 3) (list 3 4) (list 0))\n               'lesson 'union-is-order-unstable))\n(newline)"
     :problem "A service must compute the effective permissions of a user from a default set, a role set, and per-user grants, with no permission listed twice. Candidates: lset-union (all three lists in one call, dedupe built in), append followed by delete-duplicates (correct and order-stable, but allocates the duplicates first), or nested lset-adjoin folds (correct, but spells out what union already means). For combining whole sets, lset-union is the honest verb."
     :explanation "eng: three sources collapse into one permission list in a single expression. math: union is commutative, idempotent, and both operands are subsets of the result — the standard laws, verified against the implementation rather than assumed. cs: the raw answer is not in any predictable order, so any code that compares or displays a union must normalize it first."
     :meta "Why lset-union and not append plus delete-duplicates: append plus delete-duplicates IS order-stable and is the better choice when display order matters. Union is the better choice when you want the set semantics stated in the code. Pick for the reader, not for the machine.")
    (:eng "Effective-permission calculations are a classic union; the bug is always a duplicate or a dropped source, never the arithmetic."
     :math "A union B contains A and contains B and is the smallest such set — up to duplicates, which lists carry and sets do not."
     :cs "Order instability is the recurring trap. Two runs that produce set-equal answers can print differently, which breaks naive golden-file tests.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Union: pool several collections into one, counting anything that appears in more than one of them exactly once."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(lset-union equal? (list 1 2 3) (list 3 4))")
             (:domain "merging two clubs (the combined roster lists each person once, even the people who belonged to both)" :code "(define club-a (list \"ana\" \"bo\"))\n(define club-b (list \"bo\" \"cy\"))\n(define merged (lset-union string=? club-a club-b))\n(display (list 'roster merged 'members (length merged) 'naive-sum (+ (length club-a) (length club-b))))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "the set-theoretic union, whose cardinality obeys inclusion-exclusion" :code "(define a (list 1 2 3))\n(define b (list 3 4))\n(define u (lset-union equal? a b))\n(define i (lset-intersection equal? a b))\n(display (list 'union-size (length u)\n               'inclusion-exclusion (- (+ (length a) (length b)) (length i))\n               'agree (= (length u) (- (+ (length a) (length b)) (length i)))))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, lset-union pools the elements and keeps each once. Merging two clubs, the person who belonged to both is one member of the merged club, not two, which is why the roster is shorter than the sum of the sizes. In math, that shortfall is exactly the size of the intersection, and inclusion-exclusion states it as a law. All three share: pooling, a shared region counted once, and a total smaller than the naive sum."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever overlapping collections are combined and double counting is the failure mode: mailing lists, inventories, audience reach.")
    (:eng "The naive sum is always an overestimate when the sources overlap, and the sources almost always overlap."
     :math "Inclusion-exclusion is the correction term made explicit; the union verb applies it silently."
     :cs "Deduplicated merge is the core of set-based query planning and of any reach or coverage metric.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define a (list 'read 'write))\n(define b (list 'write 'admin))\n(define u (lset-union eq? a b))\n(display (list 'union u\n               'contains-all-a (every (lambda (x) (if (memq x u) #t #f)) a)\n               'contains-all-b (every (lambda (x) (if (memq x u) #t #f)) b)\n               'no-new-members (every (lambda (x) (if (or (memq x a) (memq x b)) #t #f)) u)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (laws? a b)\n  (let ((u (lset-union equal? a b)))\n    (and (lset<= equal? a u)\n         (lset<= equal? b u)\n         (lset= equal? u (lset-union equal? b a))\n         (lset= equal? (lset-union equal? u u) u)\n         (lset= equal? (lset-union equal? a (list)) a))))\n(display (map (lambda (p) (laws? (car p) (car (cdr p))))\n              (list (list (list 1 2 3) (list 3 4))\n                    (list (list) (list 1))\n                    (list (list 1) (list))\n                    (list (list 1 1) (list 1)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'zero-lists (lset-union equal?)\n               'one-list (lset-union equal? (list 1 1 2))\n               'empty-first (lset-union equal? (list) (list 1 2))\n               'empty-answer-adopts-dups (lset-union equal? (list) (list 1 1))\n               'non-list-raises (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (lset-union equal? (list 1) 7))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "lset-union produces a list containing every element of every input and nothing else. It is commutative and idempotent up to set equality, the empty list is its identity, and every input is a subset of the result."
     :invariant "forall predicate =, lists a b. a is a subset of union(a,b) and b is a subset of union(a,b) and every member of union(a,b) is a member of a or of b"
     :meta "The pattern is a least upper bound under the subset ordering. What would falsify it: a lost element from either input, an element in the result that came from neither input, or an answer that depends on argument order in set terms. See book-of-cs-curriculum on lattices.")
    (:eng "The no-new-members clause is the one people forget to test, and it is the one a bad membership predicate breaks."
     :math "Union with empty as identity, commutative, associative, idempotent — that is a semilattice, and the subset relation is its ordering."
     :cs "The empty-answer path adopts the next list WHOLE without deduping it, so (lset-union equal? (list) (list 1 1)) keeps both ones. That is a real edge in the implementation, not a typo.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (effective . layers) (apply lset-union (cons eq? layers)))\n(define base (list 'read))\n(define role (list 'read 'write))\n(define user (list 'export))\n(define p (effective base role user))\n(display (list 'effective p 'denied-delete (if (memq 'delete p) 'no 'yes)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define a (list 1 2 3))\n(define b (list 2 3 4))\n(define u (lset-union equal? a b))\n(define i (lset-intersection equal? a b))\n(define d (lset-difference equal? u i))\n(display (list 'symmetric-difference d\n               'via-two-differences (lset= equal? d (lset-union equal? (lset-difference equal? a b) (lset-difference equal? b a)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (index-keys records)\n  (apply lset-union (cons eq? (map (lambda (r) (map car r)) records))))\n(define rows (list (list (list 'id 1) (list 'name 'ana))\n                   (list (list 'id 2) (list 'email 'e))\n                   (list (list 'id 3))))\n(define keys (index-keys rows))\n(display (list 'columns (sort (map symbol->string keys) string<?) 'width (length keys)))\n(newline)"
     :base "lset-union pools several lists, keeping each distinct element once."
     :emergent "eng: layered configuration — union across a base, a role, and a user layer yields effective permissions, and adding a layer is adding an argument rather than editing a merge routine. math: symmetric difference falls out as union minus intersection, and the same set arrives from the union of the two one-sided differences, so the three set verbs together span the Boolean algebra. cs: schema inference — unioning the key sets of heterogeneous records discovers the full column set of a sparse table without a declared schema."
     :meta "The composition applies union across a variable number of sources. What emerges is the layering family: configuration merges, schema discovery, and capability resolution are all n-ary unions. See book-of-composition on variadic folds.")
    (:eng "A merge expressed as a variadic union scales by adding an argument; a merge expressed as nested pairwise calls scales by editing code."
     :math "Union, intersection, and difference generate every Boolean combination; symmetric difference is the first compound one meets."
     :cs "Schema inference over sparse records is precisely this union, which is why document stores can report a column set they never declared.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "lset-intersection" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(lset-intersection = lst1 lst ...) -> list"
  :summary "Keep the elements of the FIRST list that also appear in every one of the other lists."
  :explanation "lset-intersection filters the first list. An element survives only if each of the remaining lists contains something equal to it. Because it is a filter over the first list, the answer preserves the first list order — unlike lset-union, which reverses. If any later list is empty the answer is empty, and if there are no later lists the first list is returned unchanged."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(lset-intersection equal? (list 1 2 3) (list 2 3 4))" :note "Only 2 and 3 are in both, and they come out in first-list order: (2 3).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(lset-intersection eq? (list 'a 'b 'c) (list 'a 'b) (list 'b 'c))" :note "An element must be in EVERY later list; only b clears all three: (b).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(lset-intersection equal? (list 1 2) (list 3 4))" :note "Disjoint sets intersect to nothing, which is the empty list: ().")
  )
  :caveats ("The equality predicate is the FIRST argument, always. (lset-union equal? a b), never (lset-union a b). Getting this wrong raises a teaching error that names the fix." "Order and duplicates come from the FIRST list. (lset-intersection equal? (list 1 2 2 3) (list 2 3)) is (2 2 3) — both twos survive." "It is not symmetric in its output. Swapping the arguments gives a set-equal answer but a different list." "With a single list and no others, the answer is that list unchanged; no filtering and no deduplication happen." "If any later list is empty the answer is empty immediately, even if the first list is long.")
  :usecases ("Finding what two permission sets, tag sets, or inventories have in common." "Filtering a candidate list down to entries confirmed by every source." "Computing overlap for a reporting or matching feature.")
  :related ("lset-union" "lset-difference" "lset<=" "filter" "member")
  :learn (:concept "Intersection: keep only what every collection agrees on." :prerequisites ("filter" "member" "every") :progression "Learn filter and member first, then lset-intersection as the filter whose predicate is membership in all the other lists.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define alice (list 'mon 'tue 'thu))\n(define bob (list 'tue 'thu 'fri))\n(define carol (list 'thu 'fri))\n(define common (lset-intersection eq? alice bob carol))\n(display (list 'everyone-free common 'options (length common)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define a (list 1 2 3))\n(define b (list 2 3 4))\n(define i (lset-intersection equal? a b))\n(display (list 'subset-of-a (lset<= equal? i a)\n               'subset-of-b (lset<= equal? i b)\n               'commutative-as-sets (lset= equal? i (lset-intersection equal? b a))\n               'idempotent (lset= equal? (lset-intersection equal? a a) a)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (intersect-by-filter a b) (filter (lambda (x) (if (member x b) #t #f)) a))\n(define a (list 1 2 3))\n(define b (list 2 3 4))\n(display (list 'library (lset-intersection equal? a b)\n               'hand-rolled (intersect-by-filter a b)\n               'identical (equal? (lset-intersection equal? a b) (intersect-by-filter a b))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "A scheduler must find the days on which three people are all free. Candidates: lset-intersection (all three lists in one call, first-list order preserved), nested filter plus member calls (correct, and it is literally what the library does, but it grows a level of nesting per person), or sorting all three and merging (faster for large sets, much more code). For agreement across several small lists, lset-intersection is the honest verb."
     :explanation "eng: one call collapses three calendars into the days everybody shares. math: the intersection is a subset of each input, is commutative as a set, and is idempotent — the standard laws checked against the implementation. cs: the hand-rolled filter-plus-member version is not merely set-equal to the library answer, it is element-for-element identical, which pins down the order guarantee."
     :meta "Why lset-intersection and not nested filters: nesting hides the arity. A three-way agreement written as a variadic call reads as one idea; written as two nested filters it reads as two.")
    (:eng "Scheduling is intersection. When the answer is empty the honest report is that no common slot exists, not that the code failed."
     :math "A intersect B is contained in both operands, which is the dual of union containing both."
     :cs "Filter-plus-member is quadratic. Sort-merge or a hash set is the scalable form; the library version is for readable, small-set code.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Intersection: keep only the items that every one of the collections contains."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(lset-intersection equal? (list 1 2 3) (list 2 3 4))")
             (:domain "finding a meeting time three people can all make (a slot survives only if it is free on every calendar)" :code "(define cal-a (list 9 10 11))\n(define cal-b (list 10 11 12))\n(define cal-c (list 11 12))\n(define slots (lset-intersection equal? cal-a cal-b cal-c))\n(display (list 'slots slots 'meeting (if (null? slots) 'none (car slots))))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "the set-theoretic intersection, whose size is bounded by the smallest operand" :code "(define a (list 1 2 3 4))\n(define b (list 2 3))\n(define i (lset-intersection equal? a b))\n(display (list 'size (length i)\n               'bounded (<= (length i) (min (length a) (length b)))\n               'subset-both (and (lset<= equal? i a) (lset<= equal? i b))))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, lset-intersection keeps what all the lists share. Finding a meeting time, a slot survives only if it appears on every calendar, and one busy person is enough to eliminate it. In math, the intersection is contained in every operand, so its size can never exceed the smallest one. All three share: unanimous agreement, elimination by a single dissent, and an answer no larger than the smallest input."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a veto is enough: shared availability, common dependencies, capabilities held by every member of a group.")
    (:eng "One dissent removes a candidate. Intersection is the veto operation, which is why the empty answer is common and not a bug."
     :math "Containment in every operand is the defining property, and the size bound is its immediate corollary."
     :cs "Search-engine AND queries are intersections of posting lists; the smallest list is scanned first for exactly the size-bound reason.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define a (list 'x 'y 'z))\n(define b (list 'y 'z 'w))\n(define i (lset-intersection eq? a b))\n(display (list 'common i\n               'all-in-a (every (lambda (e) (if (memq e a) #t #f)) i)\n               'all-in-b (every (lambda (e) (if (memq e b) #t #f)) i)\n               'nothing-missed (every (lambda (e) (if (memq e b) (if (memq e i) #t #f) #t)) a)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (laws? a b)\n  (let ((i (lset-intersection equal? a b)))\n    (and (lset<= equal? i a)\n         (lset<= equal? i b)\n         (lset= equal? i (lset-intersection equal? b a))\n         (<= (length i) (min (length a) (length b)))\n         (lset= equal? (lset-intersection equal? a (lset-union equal? a b)) a))))\n(display (map (lambda (p) (laws? (car p) (car (cdr p))))\n              (list (list (list 1 2 3) (list 2 3 4))\n                    (list (list 1) (list 2))\n                    (list (list) (list 1))\n                    (list (list 1 2) (list 1 2)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'one-list (lset-intersection equal? (list 1 2 2))\n               'empty-other (lset-intersection equal? (list 1 2) (list))\n               'empty-first (lset-intersection equal? (list) (list 1 2))\n               'dups-kept (lset-intersection equal? (list 1 2 2 3) (list 2 3))\n               'non-list-raises (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (lset-intersection equal? (list 1) 7))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "lset-intersection returns exactly those members of the first list present in all the others: nothing shared is dropped and nothing unshared is kept. The result is a subset of every input and can never be longer than the shortest one."
     :invariant "forall predicate =, lists a b. every member of intersection(a,b) is a member of a and of b, and every member of a that is a member of b is a member of intersection(a,b)"
     :meta "The pattern is a greatest lower bound under the subset ordering. What would falsify it: a shared element dropped, an unshared element kept, or a result longer than the shorter operand. See book-of-cs-curriculum on lattices.")
    (:eng "The nothing-missed clause is the half people skip. Without it, always returning the empty list would satisfy every other check."
     :math "Absorption — intersecting a set with a union containing it gives the set back — is the law that ties intersection and union into a lattice."
     :cs "Duplicates in the first list survive intersection. If the caller assumes a set, that assumption must be enforced upstream with delete-duplicates.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (all-can permissions-per-user)\n  (apply lset-intersection (cons eq? permissions-per-user)))\n(define team (list (list 'read 'write 'deploy)\n                   (list 'read 'write)\n                   (list 'read 'write 'audit)))\n(define shared (all-can team))\n(display (list 'team-baseline shared 'deploy-is-shared (if (memq 'deploy shared) #t #f)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define a (list 1 2 3 4))\n(define b (list 2 3 4 5))\n(display (list 'jaccard-numerator (length (lset-intersection equal? a b))\n               'jaccard-denominator (length (lset-union equal? a b))\n               'de-morgan (lset= equal? (lset-difference equal? a (lset-intersection equal? a b))\n                                        (lset-difference equal? a b))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (resolve-versions constraints)\n  (apply lset-intersection (cons equal? constraints)))\n(define pkg (list (list 1 2 3) (list 2 3 4) (list 2 3)))\n(define ok (resolve-versions pkg))\n(display (list 'satisfying ok 'pick (if (null? ok) 'unsatisfiable (apply max ok))))\n(newline)"
     :base "lset-intersection keeps the members of the first list that appear in all the rest."
     :emergent "eng: a team baseline — intersecting every member permission set gives the capabilities the whole team can be assumed to have, which is the correct set for a shared script to rely on. math: the intersection size over the union size is the Jaccard index, the standard similarity measure, and a De Morgan identity relating difference and intersection falls out of the same three verbs. cs: dependency resolution — intersecting the acceptable version sets of every constraint gives the satisfying versions, and an empty answer is precisely the unsatisfiable case a resolver must report."
     :meta "The composition applies intersection across a variable number of constraint sets. What emerges is the constraint family: resolvers, similarity metrics, and least-common-capability calculations are all n-ary intersections. See book-of-composition on variadic folds.")
    (:eng "A shared baseline must be an intersection, never a union; assuming the union is how a script works for the admin and fails for everyone else."
     :math "Jaccard similarity is intersection over union — two of these verbs and one division."
     :cs "Version resolution as set intersection is the simple case; real resolvers add ordering and backtracking, but the empty-intersection failure report is the same.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "lset-difference" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(lset-difference = lst1 lst ...) -> list"
  :summary "Keep the elements of the FIRST list that appear in NONE of the other lists."
  :explanation "lset-difference filters the first list, removing anything found in any of the later lists. It preserves the first list order and its duplicates, since it is a filter and not a rebuild. Empty later lists subtract nothing and are discarded before the scan, and with no later lists at all the first list is returned unchanged."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(lset-difference equal? (list 1 2 3 4) (list 2 4))" :note "The 2 and the 4 are removed; the rest keeps its order: (1 3).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(lset-difference eq? (list 'a 'b 'c 'd) (list 'b) (list 'd))" :note "Several subtrahends are allowed; anything in any of them goes: (a c).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(lset-difference equal? (list 1 1 2) (list 2))" :note "Duplicates in the first list are preserved — this is a filter, not a set rebuild: (1 1).")
  )
  :caveats ("The equality predicate is the FIRST argument, always. (lset-union equal? a b), never (lset-union a b). Getting this wrong raises a teaching error that names the fix." "Order and duplicates come from the FIRST list. Removing an element removes every copy of it, but untouched duplicates stay." "It is NOT symmetric. (lset-difference equal? a b) and (lset-difference equal? b a) are different questions with different answers." "Empty later lists are dropped before the scan, so they cannot affect the answer." "With a single list and no others, the answer is that list unchanged — no deduplication happens." "The predicate is called as (= first-list-element other-element), the first list element FIRST.")
  :usecases ("Computing what is present in one inventory but missing from another." "Removing a blocklist or an already-processed set from a candidate list." "Finding the new items since the last run.")
  :related ("lset-intersection" "lset-union" "delete" "remove" "filter")
  :learn (:concept "Difference: what is in the first collection and in none of the others." :prerequisites ("filter" "member" "lset-intersection") :progression "Learn filter and member, then lset-difference, then note that union, intersection, and difference together express every Boolean set question.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define wanted (list 'flour 'eggs 'milk 'salt))\n(define pantry (list 'salt 'flour))\n(define shopping (lset-difference eq? wanted pantry))\n(display (list 'buy shopping 'items (length shopping)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define a (list 1 2 3 4))\n(define b (list 2 4))\n(define d (lset-difference equal? a b))\n(display (list 'subset-of-a (lset<= equal? d a)\n               'disjoint-from-b (null? (lset-intersection equal? d b))\n               'partitions-a (lset= equal? a (lset-union equal? d (lset-intersection equal? a b)))\n               'not-symmetric (not (lset= equal? d (lset-difference equal? b a)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (diff-by-remove a b) (remove (lambda (x) (if (member x b) #t #f)) a))\n(define a (list 1 2 3 4))\n(define b (list 2 4))\n(display (list 'library (lset-difference equal? a b)\n               'hand-rolled (diff-by-remove a b)\n               'identical (equal? (lset-difference equal? a b) (diff-by-remove a b))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "A cook has a recipe list and a pantry and needs a shopping list. Candidates: lset-difference (states subtraction directly and keeps recipe order), remove with a member predicate (correct, and identical in output, but the intent is buried in a lambda), or filter with a negated member test (same thing, one more negation to misread). For what-is-missing questions, lset-difference is the honest verb."
     :explanation "eng: the shopping list is the recipe minus the pantry, in recipe order, ready to read down the aisle. math: the difference is a subset of the first list, is disjoint from the second, and together with the intersection it partitions the first list exactly. cs: the library answer and the hand-rolled remove are element-for-element identical, which documents the order and duplicate guarantees precisely."
     :meta "Why lset-difference and not remove with a lambda: both work. The difference is that one names a set operation the reader already knows, and the other asks the reader to reconstruct it from a predicate.")
    (:eng "What is missing is a different question from what is shared, and asking it backwards is the classic inventory bug."
     :math "A minus B, together with A intersect B, partitions A. That is the decomposition every diff report is built on."
     :cs "Difference is linear in the first list and linear in the subtrahends per element, so it is quadratic overall; hash sets fix that when the lists get large.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Difference: what is in the first collection and in none of the others — subtraction for sets."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(lset-difference equal? (list 1 2 3 4) (list 2 4))")
             (:domain "packing for a trip (the things still to buy are the things on the list that are not already in the bag)" :code "(define needed (list 'socks 'charger 'passport 'towel))\n(define packed (list 'passport 'socks))\n(define todo (lset-difference eq? needed packed))\n(display (list 'still-to-pack todo 'done (length packed) 'left (length todo)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "the relative complement, which partitions the first set together with the intersection" :code "(define a (list 1 2 3 4))\n(define b (list 2 4 6))\n(define d (lset-difference equal? a b))\n(define i (lset-intersection equal? a b))\n(display (list 'complement d 'shared i\n               'sizes-add (= (length a) (+ (length d) (length i)))))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, lset-difference subtracts. Packing for a trip, what is still to pack is the needed list minus what is already in the bag, and the two counts add back to the whole list. In math, that is the relative complement, and it partitions the first set together with the intersection. All three share: one collection taken as the reference, another subtracted from it, and two pieces that reassemble the original."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a to-do is a want minus a have: shopping lists, packing lists, migration backlogs, unmet dependencies.")
    (:eng "A to-do list is always a difference. Recomputing it each time from the want and the have is what keeps it from drifting."
     :math "Relative complement is only defined against a reference set, which is why the operation is not symmetric."
     :cs "Every incremental build system is a difference: the targets minus the ones already up to date.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define a (list 'p 'q 'r))\n(define b (list 'q))\n(define d (lset-difference eq? a b))\n(display (list 'difference d\n               'all-from-a (every (lambda (e) (if (memq e a) #t #f)) d)\n               'none-from-b (every (lambda (e) (if (memq e b) #f #t)) d)\n               'nothing-missed (every (lambda (e) (if (memq e b) #t (if (memq e d) #t #f))) a)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (laws? a b)\n  (let ((d (lset-difference equal? a b))\n        (i (lset-intersection equal? a b)))\n    (and (lset<= equal? d a)\n         (null? (lset-intersection equal? d b))\n         (lset= equal? a (lset-union equal? d i))\n         (lset= equal? (lset-difference equal? a (list)) a)\n         (null? (lset-difference equal? a a)))))\n(display (map (lambda (p) (laws? (car p) (car (cdr p))))\n              (list (list (list 1 2 3 4) (list 2 4))\n                    (list (list 1) (list 1))\n                    (list (list 1 2) (list))\n                    (list (list) (list 1)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'one-list (lset-difference equal? (list 1 1 2))\n               'empty-subtrahends-ignored (lset-difference equal? (list 1 2 3) (list) (list 2))\n               'dups-preserved (lset-difference equal? (list 1 1 2) (list 2))\n               'self-difference (lset-difference equal? (list 1 2) (list 1 2))\n               'non-list-raises (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (lset-difference equal? (list 1) 7))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "lset-difference keeps exactly those members of the first list found in none of the others. The result is a subset of the first list, is disjoint from every subtrahend, and together with the intersection reassembles the first list."
     :invariant "forall predicate =, lists a b. every member of difference(a,b) is a member of a and not of b, and every member of a not in b is in difference(a,b)"
     :meta "The pattern is relative complement. What would falsify it: a kept element that occurs in a subtrahend, a dropped element that occurs in none, or a partition whose pieces do not reassemble. See book-of-cs-curriculum on set algebra.")
    (:eng "The nothing-missed clause is the half that catches an over-eager filter; without it, returning the empty list would pass the other checks."
     :math "Difference with the empty set is the identity and difference with itself is empty — the two boundary laws that pin the operation down."
     :cs "Empty subtrahends being discarded before the scan is an implementation detail with an observable consequence: passing extra empty lists costs nothing.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (backlog wanted . done-lists)\n  (apply lset-difference (cons eq? (cons wanted done-lists))))\n(define tasks (list 'design 'build 'test 'ship))\n(define finished (list 'design))\n(define cancelled (list 'test))\n(display (list 'backlog (backlog tasks finished cancelled)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define a (list 1 2 3))\n(define b (list 3 4 5))\n(define sym (lset-union equal? (lset-difference equal? a b) (lset-difference equal? b a)))\n(display (list 'symmetric-difference (sort sym <)\n               'excludes-shared (null? (lset-intersection equal? sym (lset-intersection equal? a b)))\n               'de-morgan (lset= equal? (lset-difference equal? a (lset-union equal? b (list 2)))\n                                        (lset-intersection equal? (lset-difference equal? a b) (lset-difference equal? a (list 2))))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (sync local remote)\n  (list 'upload (lset-difference equal? local remote)\n        'download (lset-difference equal? remote local)\n        'in-sync (lset-intersection equal? local remote)))\n(display (sync (list 'a 'b 'c) (list 'b 'c 'd)))\n(newline)"
     :base "lset-difference keeps the members of the first list that appear in none of the others."
     :emergent "eng: a backlog calculator — subtracting both the finished and the cancelled lists from the plan gives what is genuinely left, and a new category of exclusion is a new argument rather than new code. math: symmetric difference emerges as the union of the two one-sided differences, and a De Morgan law relating difference over a union to intersection of differences is verified on real values. cs: a sync plan — the two one-sided differences give the upload set and the download set while the intersection gives what needs no action, which is the whole of a naive replication protocol."
     :meta "The composition takes differences in both directions and combines them. What emerges is the reconciliation family: sync plans, diff reports, and change detection are all two-way differences plus an intersection. See book-of-composition on symmetric operations.")
    (:eng "Reconciliation always needs both directions. Computing only one is how a sync silently deletes what the other side added."
     :math "Symmetric difference is the exclusive-or of set algebra, and it is the operation that makes sets into a group under it."
     :cs "Upload, download, and no-op is the complete case analysis for a two-way sync without conflict detection; conflicts need timestamps, which sets do not carry.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "lset<=" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(lset<= = lst ...) -> boolean"
  :summary "True when each list is a subset of the next one, read left to right."
  :explanation "lset<= is the subset test, generalized to a chain. With two lists it answers whether every member of the first appears in the second. With more, it checks each adjacent pair, so the whole call asserts an increasing chain of sets. Zero lists and one list are trivially true. Duplicates and order are irrelevant — only membership is consulted."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(lset<= equal? (list 1) (list 1 2))" :note "Every member of the first list is in the second, so #t.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(lset<= equal? (list 1 5) (list 1 2))" :note "The 5 is missing from the second list, so #f.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(lset<= eq? (list 'read) (list 'read 'write) (list 'read 'write 'admin))" :note "Each list is a subset of the next, so the whole chain holds: #t.")
  )
  :caveats ("The equality predicate is the FIRST argument, always. (lset-union equal? a b), never (lset-union a b). Getting this wrong raises a teaching error that names the fix." "It checks ADJACENT pairs only. The chain property makes the transitive conclusion follow, but the verb itself never compares the first list to the last directly." "Zero lists and one list both return #t. That is the vacuous case, not a bug." "Duplicates are ignored: (lset<= equal? (list 1 1) (list 1)) is #t." "It is subset-or-equal, not proper subset. (lset<= equal? (list 1) (list 1)) is #t. There is no proper-subset verb; write it as lset<= and not lset=." "The predicate is called as (= member-of-earlier-list member-of-later-list).")
  :usecases ("Checking that a requested permission set is covered by a granted one." "Asserting that a refactored list did not lose any elements." "Validating a privilege hierarchy or an escalating tier chain.")
  :related ("lset=" "lset-union" "lset-intersection" "every" "member")
  :learn (:concept "Subset: is everything here also over there?" :prerequisites ("member" "every" "equal?") :progression "Learn every and member first, then lset<= as their composition, then lset= as subset in both directions.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define requested (list 'read 'write))\n(define granted (list 'read 'write 'admin))\n(display (list 'authorized (lset<= eq? requested granted)\n               'missing (lset-difference eq? requested granted)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define a (list 1 2))\n(define b (list 1 2 3))\n(display (list 'reflexive (lset<= equal? a a)\n               'antisymmetric-gives-equality (if (and (lset<= equal? a b) (lset<= equal? b a)) (lset= equal? a b) 'not-both)\n               'transitive (if (and (lset<= equal? a b) (lset<= equal? b (list 1 2 3 4))) (lset<= equal? a (list 1 2 3 4)) 'na)\n               'union-is-upper-bound (lset<= equal? a (lset-union equal? a b))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (subset-by-every a b) (every (lambda (x) (if (member x b) #t #f)) a))\n(define a (list 1 2))\n(define b (list 1 2 3))\n(display (list 'library (lset<= equal? a b)\n               'hand-rolled (subset-by-every a b)\n               'agree (eq? (lset<= equal? a b) (subset-by-every a b))\n               'empty-is-subset-of-everything (lset<= equal? (list) b)))\n(newline)"
     :problem "An authorization check must confirm that everything a caller asked for is covered by what the caller was granted. Candidates: lset<= (states the subset question directly and returns a boolean), every plus member (correct and identical, but the reader has to reassemble the concept), or checking that the difference is empty (correct, and it also tells you WHAT is missing, which is better for the error message). For the yes-or-no decision, lset<= is the honest verb; pair it with lset-difference for the report."
     :explanation "eng: the boolean decides and the difference explains, so one call authorizes and the other writes the denial message. math: the subset relation is reflexive, transitive, and antisymmetric up to set equality — a partial order, with union as an upper bound. cs: the library answer and the every-plus-member composition agree, and the empty list is a subset of everything, which is the identity case that trips up hand-written loops."
     :meta "Why lset<= and not a manual loop: a hand-written subset test that returns #f on the empty list is a real and common bug, and it fails open or closed depending on which way it is used.")
    (:eng "A denial that says only no is a support ticket. A denial that names the missing permission is a fix."
     :math "Subset is a partial order, not a total one: two sets can be incomparable, so #f does not mean the other direction holds."
     :cs "The vacuous truth for the empty set is not a special case to code around; it is what makes the algebra consistent.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Subset: is everything in this collection also present in that one?"
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(lset<= equal? (list 1) (list 1 2))")
             (:domain "checking a packing list against a bag (you have packed enough only if every item on the list is in the bag; extra items in the bag do not matter)" :code "(define list-needed (list 'passport 'socks))\n(define bag (list 'socks 'passport 'book))\n(display (list 'ready (lset<= eq? list-needed bag)\n               'missing (lset-difference eq? list-needed bag)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "the subset relation as a partial order, with union as a least upper bound" :code "(define a (list 1 2))\n(define b (list 2 3))\n(define u (lset-union equal? a b))\n(display (list 'a-below-u (lset<= equal? a u)\n               'b-below-u (lset<= equal? b u)\n               'incomparable (and (not (lset<= equal? a b)) (not (lset<= equal? b a)))))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, lset<= asks whether one list is contained in another. Checking a packing list against a bag, extra things in the bag are fine and only missing things matter, which is exactly the asymmetry of containment. In math, that asymmetry makes subset a partial order: two sets can be incomparable, neither containing the other, and the union sits above both. All three share: one-directional containment, indifference to surplus, and the possibility of no answer either way."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever coverage matters and surplus does not: permissions, requirements against capabilities, packing lists, test coverage.")
    (:eng "Coverage checks are one-directional. Asking the question backwards turns a passing check into a failing one on the same data."
     :math "A partial order admits incomparable elements, which is why the negation of lset<= is not lset>=."
     :cs "Type systems, capability models, and dependency version ranges all lean on a subset-shaped partial order.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define required (list 'a 'b))\n(define have (list 'b 'a 'c))\n(display (list 'covered (lset<= eq? required have)\n               'surplus-ignored (lset<= eq? required (list 'a 'b))\n               'one-missing-fails (lset<= eq? (list 'a 'z) have)\n               'empty-always-covered (lset<= eq? (list) have)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (laws? a b)\n  (and (lset<= equal? a a)\n       (lset<= equal? (list) a)\n       (eq? (lset<= equal? a b) (null? (lset-difference equal? a b)))\n       (if (and (lset<= equal? a b) (lset<= equal? b a)) (lset= equal? a b) #t)\n       (lset<= equal? a (lset-union equal? a b))\n       (lset<= equal? (lset-intersection equal? a b) a)))\n(display (map (lambda (p) (laws? (car p) (car (cdr p))))\n              (list (list (list 1) (list 1 2))\n                    (list (list 1 5) (list 1 2))\n                    (list (list) (list))\n                    (list (list 1 1) (list 1)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'zero-lists (lset<= equal?)\n               'one-list (lset<= equal? (list 9 9))\n               'chain (lset<= equal? (list 1) (list 1 2) (list 1 2 3))\n               'chain-breaks-in-middle (lset<= equal? (list 1) (list 1 2) (list 1))\n               'non-list-raises (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (lset<= equal? (list 1) 7))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "lset<= is true exactly when the difference of each adjacent pair is empty. It is reflexive, admits the empty list below everything, and combined in both directions it is equivalent to lset=."
     :invariant "forall predicate =, lists a b. lset<=(a,b) is true if and only if difference(a,b) is empty; and lset<=(a,b) and lset<=(b,a) together hold if and only if lset=(a,b)"
     :meta "The pattern is a partial order defined by an emptiness condition. What would falsify it: a true answer with a non-empty difference, a false answer on a reflexive comparison, or a chain that passes while an adjacent pair fails. See book-of-cs-curriculum on orders and lattices.")
    (:eng "Tying the boolean to the difference means one implementation can be checked against the other, and the error message comes free."
     :math "Reflexive, transitive, antisymmetric up to set equality: the three axioms, all checkable in-language."
     :cs "The chain form short-circuits on the first failing adjacent pair, so a long chain costs nothing extra once it has failed.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (authorize requested granted)\n  (if (lset<= eq? requested granted)\n      (list 'allow)\n      (list 'deny 'missing (lset-difference eq? requested granted))))\n(display (list (authorize (list 'read) (list 'read 'write))\n               (authorize (list 'read 'admin) (list 'read 'write))))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define levels (list (list 'read)\n                     (list 'read 'write)\n                     (list 'read 'write 'admin)))\n(define (chain? ls) (apply lset<= (cons eq? ls)))\n(display (list 'is-a-chain (chain? levels)\n               'reversed-is-not (chain? (reverse levels))\n               'top (car (reverse levels))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (regression? before after)\n  (if (lset<= eq? before after) 'no-loss (list 'lost (lset-difference eq? before after))))\n(display (list 'ok (regression? (list 'a 'b) (list 'a 'b 'c))\n               'bad (regression? (list 'a 'b) (list 'a 'c))))\n(newline)"
     :base "lset<= reports whether each list is contained in the next."
     :emergent "eng: an authorizer that both decides and explains — the subset test gives the verdict and the difference gives the reason, so a denial always names what was missing. math: a tier hierarchy — chaining the test across the levels proves the whole ladder is increasing, and the same call on the reversed list is false, which is what makes it a genuine order and not just a comparison. cs: a regression detector — asserting that the new capability set contains the old one turns silent feature loss into a named failure."
     :meta "The composition pairs the boolean test with the difference that explains it. What emerges is the coverage family: authorizers, regression gates, and hierarchy validators all decide with lset<= and report with lset-difference. See book-of-composition on decide-and-explain pairs.")
    (:eng "Every gate should be able to explain itself. Pairing the predicate with the witness is how that gets built in rather than bolted on."
     :math "A chain is a totally ordered subset of a partial order; checking one is checking each adjacent link."
     :cs "Capability regression is a real class of bug that type checkers do not catch, because losing a runtime permission is not a type error.")
)
)

(verb
  :name "lset=" :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "pure"
  :signature "(lset= = lst ...) -> boolean"
  :summary "True when all the lists contain exactly the same elements, ignoring order and duplicates."
  :explanation "lset= is set equality. It compares adjacent pairs, and each pair must be a subset of the other in both directions. Because only membership is consulted, two lists that differ in order, in length, or in how many copies of an element they hold are still set-equal. This is the right test for comparing the output of set operations, whose element order is not guaranteed."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(lset= equal? (list 1 2) (list 2 1))" :note "Order does not matter for set equality, so #t.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(lset= equal? (list 1 2) (list 2 1 1))" :note "Duplicates do not matter either; both lists have exactly the members 1 and 2, so #t.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(lset= eq? (list 'a 'b) (list 'b 'a) (list 'a 'a 'b))" :note "Any number of lists is allowed and all must agree: #t.")
  )
  :caveats ("The equality predicate is the FIRST argument, always. (lset-union equal? a b), never (lset-union a b). Getting this wrong raises a teaching error that names the fix." "lset= is NOT equal?. Two set-equal lists can have different lengths and different orders. Use equal? when the list structure itself matters." "Zero lists and one list both return #t, the vacuous case." "It compares ADJACENT pairs, so with three or more lists the conclusion that all are mutually equal follows from transitivity rather than from a direct comparison of every pair." "Each pair costs two subset scans, so the comparison is quadratic in the list sizes." "A non-symmetric predicate makes the two directions ask different questions; lset= is only meaningful with a genuine equivalence.")
  :usecases ("Asserting in a test that a set operation produced the right members regardless of order." "Checking whether two configurations grant identical capabilities." "Detecting that a refactor changed the contents of a collection.")
  :related ("lset<=" "equal?" "lset-difference" "delete-duplicates" "sort")
  :learn (:concept "Set equality: same members, never mind order or repetition." :prerequisites ("lset<=" "member" "equal?") :progression "Learn lset<= first, then lset= as subset in both directions, then note when you actually need equal? instead.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 (Olin Shivers); implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js (surface completion 2026-08-02); documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define config-a (list 'read 'write))\n(define config-b (list 'write 'read 'read))\n(display (list 'same-capabilities (lset= eq? config-a config-b)\n               'same-list (equal? config-a config-b)\n               'lesson 'set-equal-but-not-structurally-equal))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define a (list 1 2))\n(define b (list 2 1 1))\n(display (list 'reflexive (lset= equal? a a)\n               'symmetric (eq? (lset= equal? a b) (lset= equal? b a))\n               'transitive (if (and (lset= equal? a b) (lset= equal? b (list 1 2 2))) (lset= equal? a (list 1 2 2)) 'na)\n               'is-two-way-subset (eq? (lset= equal? a b) (and (lset<= equal? a b) (lset<= equal? b a)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (set-eq-by-sort a b)\n  (equal? (sort (delete-duplicates a) <) (sort (delete-duplicates b) <)))\n(define a (list 1 2))\n(define b (list 2 1 1))\n(display (list 'library (lset= equal? a b)\n               'normalize-and-compare (set-eq-by-sort a b)\n               'agree (eq? (lset= equal? a b) (set-eq-by-sort a b))\n               'note 'sorting-needs-an-order-lset-does-not))\n(newline)"
     :problem "A test must assert that a set operation returned the right members, but lset-union does not guarantee element order. Candidates: lset= (compares by membership, so order and duplicates are irrelevant), sorting both sides and using equal? (correct, but it requires a total order on the elements, which symbols and records may not have), or comparing with equal? directly (wrong — it fails on a correct answer that happens to be ordered differently). For asserting on set results, lset= is the honest verb."
     :explanation "eng: two configurations grant identical capabilities while being structurally different lists, and only one of the two comparisons says so. math: set equality is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, and it is definitionally two-way subset — an equivalence relation built from a partial order. cs: the sort-and-compare alternative agrees on numbers but needs a total order, which is a requirement lset= does not impose."
     :meta "Why lset= and not equal? on sorted lists: sorting needs a comparator for the element type. Membership needs only the equality predicate you already had to supply.")
    (:eng "A test that asserts on incidental order fails on a correct change. That is the flaky test people blame on the machine."
     :math "Mutual containment as the definition of equality is how set theory defines it in the first place."
     :cs "Normalize-then-compare is faster for large sets but imposes an ordering requirement; membership comparison is slower and imposes nothing.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Same members: two collections hold the same things, never mind arrangement or repetition."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(lset= equal? (list 1 2) (list 2 1 1))")
             (:domain "two shopping baskets holding the same goods (the order they were put in and the fact that one has a spare of something do not change what was bought)" :code "(define basket-a (list 'milk 'bread))\n(define basket-b (list 'bread 'milk 'milk))\n(display (list 'same-goods (lset= eq? basket-a basket-b)\n               'same-count (= (length basket-a) (length basket-b))))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "extensional equality: two sets are equal exactly when each contains the other" :code "(define a (list 1 2))\n(define b (list 2 1 1))\n(display (list 'lset= (lset= equal? a b)\n               'two-way-subset (and (lset<= equal? a b) (lset<= equal? b a))\n               'both-differences-empty (and (null? (lset-difference equal? a b))\n                                            (null? (lset-difference equal? b a)))))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, lset= ignores order and duplicates. Two shopping baskets with the same goods are the same purchase even if one holds a spare and they were filled in a different order — though the counts differ, so the equality is about kinds, not quantities. In math, extensional equality says two sets are equal exactly when each contains the other. All three share: identity by membership alone, and deliberate blindness to arrangement and multiplicity."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever what matters is which things are present rather than how many or in what order: capability sets, tag sets, vocabulary comparisons.")
    (:eng "Deciding what counts as the same is a design choice. Sets say kinds, bags say quantities, lists say arrangement."
     :math "Extensionality is the axiom that makes a set determined entirely by its members."
     :cs "Multisets exist precisely because the blindness to multiplicity is sometimes wrong; choose the structure that matches the question.")
)
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define a (list 'x 'y))\n(define b (list 'y 'x 'x))\n(display (list 'set-equal (lset= eq? a b)\n               'structurally-equal (equal? a b)\n               'lengths (list (length a) (length b))\n               'order-blind (lset= eq? a (reverse a))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (laws? a b)\n  (and (lset= equal? a a)\n       (eq? (lset= equal? a b) (lset= equal? b a))\n       (eq? (lset= equal? a b) (and (lset<= equal? a b) (lset<= equal? b a)))\n       (lset= equal? a (reverse a))\n       (lset= equal? a (delete-duplicates a))))\n(display (map (lambda (p) (laws? (car p) (car (cdr p))))\n              (list (list (list 1 2) (list 2 1 1))\n                    (list (list 1 2) (list 1))\n                    (list (list) (list))\n                    (list (list 1 1) (list 1)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (list 'zero-lists (lset= equal?)\n               'one-list (lset= equal? (list 9 9))\n               'three-lists (lset= equal? (list 1 2) (list 2 1) (list 1 1 2))\n               'chain-breaks (lset= equal? (list 1 2) (list 2 1) (list 1))\n               'non-list-raises (guard (e (#t 'raised)) (lset= equal? (list 1) 7))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "lset= is an equivalence relation that holds exactly when both one-sided differences are empty. It is invariant under reversal and under removing duplicates, which is precisely what distinguishes it from equal?."
     :invariant "forall predicate =, lists a b. lset=(a,b) if and only if lset<=(a,b) and lset<=(b,a); and lset=(a, reverse(a)) and lset=(a, delete-duplicates(a)) always hold"
     :meta "The pattern is an equivalence induced by mutual containment. What would falsify it: an asymmetric answer, a false result on a reversed or deduplicated copy of the same list, or disagreement with the two-way subset test. See book-of-cs-curriculum on equivalence relations.")
    (:eng "The reversal and deduplication invariants are the cheapest property tests available, and they catch an accidental equal? substitution immediately."
     :math "An equivalence relation partitions lists into classes; each class is one set with many list representations."
     :cs "Quadratic cost is the price of not requiring an order. For large sets, hash-based normalization is the standard trade.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (drift expected actual)\n  (if (lset= eq? expected actual)\n      (list 'in-sync)\n      (list 'drift 'extra (lset-difference eq? actual expected)\n                  'missing (lset-difference eq? expected actual))))\n(display (list (drift (list 'a 'b) (list 'b 'a))\n               (drift (list 'a 'b) (list 'b 'c))))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define a (list 1 2 3))\n(define b (list 3 4))\n(display (list 'de-morgan-union (lset= equal? (lset-difference equal? (lset-union equal? a b) b)\n                                              (lset-difference equal? a b))\n               'absorption (lset= equal? (lset-union equal? a (lset-intersection equal? a b)) a)\n               'distributive (lset= equal? (lset-intersection equal? a (lset-union equal? b (list 2)))\n                                           (lset-union equal? (lset-intersection equal? a b)\n                                                              (lset-intersection equal? a (list 2))))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (canonical xs) (sort (delete-duplicates xs) <))\n(define (classes lists)\n  (fold (lambda (l acc) (lset-adjoin equal? acc (canonical l))) (list) lists))\n(define data (list (list 1 2) (list 2 1 1) (list 3) (list 3 3) (list 1 2 2)))\n(display (list 'distinct-sets (classes data)\n               'count (length (classes data))\n               'all-same-class (lset= equal? (car data) (car (cdr data)))))\n(newline)"
     :base "lset= reports whether lists hold the same members, ignoring order and duplicates."
     :emergent "eng: a drift detector — when the sets differ, the two one-sided differences say exactly what is extra and what is missing, turning a failed equality into an actionable report. math: the whole Boolean algebra becomes testable — De Morgan, absorption, and distributivity are all stated as lset= assertions on real values, so the laws are executed rather than recited. cs: canonicalization — mapping each list to a sorted deduplicated form and adjoining gives the distinct equivalence classes, which is how a cache key or a memo table for sets is built."
     :meta "The composition uses set equality as the assertion primitive for the other set verbs. What emerges is the law-checking family: property tests, algebraic identities, and canonicalization all need an order-blind equality first. See book-of-composition on property-based testing.")
    (:eng "An equality check that can explain its failure is worth several that only report it."
     :math "Executing the axioms on real values is the cheapest possible confidence that an implementation is the structure it claims to be."
     :cs "A canonical form turns an equivalence relation into a hashable key, which is the standard move for memoizing over sets.")
)
)
